      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>BOVRIL v.</head>
        <p>BOVRIL v.</p>
        <p>INFLUENZA.</p>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE UNION.</p>
        <p>UNITED RALLY IN PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>A united rally of the workers in come c ion with the 'Warrington Bar•d of Hope Union was held on Thursday last week in Parr Ha 1. There was a good attendance. Amongst those present on the plat form were :—Mr R A Naylor and Miss Niylor, Rev E T Reed and Mrs Reed, Mr Brough (conductor), Counciil,,r Ellison, Mr Prince, Mrs Yonge, Miss Green (of the Railway Mission), MOs Whitfield (matron of the Nurse .assoeiazion), Mr Lloyd Jones. Rev J Davies (St Ann's,Warrington). and Rev Dr 'Porter. of Southport. Tna piatiorm we aiso ocempiel by the Band of Elope Union choir. Mr. R A.Naslor pre- sided, and after part of the programme hat 4 been gone through called ep-n The Rev Dr PORTER who was very cordially received. Be congratulated the Chairman on the spleuctid intestine, gathered together on behalf ot the total abstinence -cause. There was no meeting he was more willing to attend than a Band ef Hope meeting, because they knew their own minds about the drink question. He must congratulate the conductor on the splendid choir tripe faad, and the singing they hart e joyed that evening. He wanted to impress upon the children the gigantic and terrible evil they had to fight. if they were to put all the public houses in the country ei le by side, thy would make a street reaching all toe way from John 0' 'Groat's house in Scotland to the furthest point away-- Lands end in England. He hoped those girls and boys would form an invincible army to batter down those public houses: With that street there would be a lunatic asylum every two miles, a disti lery every two miles, sod every two miles a workhouse and a gaol. They wanted Bands of Hope to fight against &amp;isle and to teach men to keep sound bodias. He hated the sight ot drink because it made kind men become cruel. One thing they wanted torn the government was legislation to stop puNicans from selling intoxicating drink to children. (Applause.) Be did not know what they were doing in War- rington but in Liverpool they had done naore for temperance than anywhere else he knew. Legislation threatened to take away the license from any publican who sold intoxicating drink to any child under 13 years of age. Ancther thing they wanted was Sunday closing. Ho was glad to hear they were going to Nave a Sunday closing meeting in Warrington. (Ap• planes.) It was high time too. They must not forgetl they had it in Scotland in 1854-45 years ago. All our ' •cleverest statesmen came from Scotland. The Scotch people knew what was goad for them, Be sincerely hoped the people of Warrington would attend the meeting on 31st January in favour of Sunday closing whatever their politics. He hoped the fathers and mothers present would consider the temperance question. If they wanted to make their children heathy, and brave soldiers let them take the pledge. (Applause.) During the evening an interesting programme was isubmitted, consisting of songs, recitations, dialogues, aiohn solos, and choruses by the choir. Tao songs— " The Gilt "and " 'lke Premi,e of Life " rec-ived care- ml treatment by Miss Hannah Brougla and a violin tolo by Miss Leas was creditably rendered. Mr. W. Brough and Mr. E. Wilkes, A L.C.M. es conductor and organist, perlorroed their duties in an able manner. Mrs. Horton made a gcod accompanist.</p>
        <p>As a preventive of Influenza, Bovril is supreme, because:it gives that healthy tone and warmth to the body which alone offers effectual resistance to the attacks of the dreaded disease fromlwhich so many suffer in winter time.</p>
        <p>Hasty meals, most often taken at irregular hours, engender that chilly discomfort and depression of spirits when the body is least impervious to the germs of Influenza. A cup of Bovril is all that is required as a mainstay and a warmth-giver. Taken between breakfast and lunch, it is an admirable stimulant.</p>
        <p>Bovril is not only a preventive of Influenza, but renders invaluable:asisstance in ridding the system of an attack. When the symptoms develop—headaches, chilly sensations, nervous- ness, and loss of appetite—Bovril will be found most acceptable as an appetising, stimulating, and easily assimilable food, strengthening the system when strength is most needed; and, when the attack has subsided, promptly re- newing the vital forces, and bringing about a complete recovery.</p>
