      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE ELECTRIC SUPPLY CO.</head>
        <p>THE SOUTH LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE ELECTRIC SUPPLY CO.</p>
        <p>TRE OPPOSITION BY WARRINGTON. On Friday the opposition was heard to the South Lancashire and Cheshire Electricity Company's measure for supplying electric power to an area in Lancashire and Cheshire Item tne west coast as far south as the river Dee, and north to the Ribble, omitting the borough of rreston end taking a cir- cular sweep round between Bolton and Bury, Eccles, and Manchester to Chester. There were two memorials lodged sgainst this measure. The first was entered by lies,rs Sharp and Co., on behalf of the Wallasey Urban District Council, and the second by Messrs Baker, Lees and Co., for the Corporation of Warrington and the Urban District Council of Ormskirk, Latham, and Burscough. The main ground of the oppositicn-was that no votice had been served as to the exact localities where powers were sought for the supply of elec- tricity. The Bill is one Of a set ice ot measures, all emanating from one source, by which different companies seek in differen'., parts of the country to provide electricity for all purposes. The Exan;iner decided on the petition of the Wallasey Urban District Council, that standing orders had nrt been complied with, inasmuch as the places included in tLe area ot supply bed not been mentioned in the Parliamentary notices. The same decision was given as regards Warring- ton, Ornisk;rk, Latham end Burscough.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Corporation have also me moralised against the EleC.rie Power Company's Bill.</p>
        <p>- NE , Transparent Ceme COAGULI Advt.. 1 . 01 broken articles.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY Dun NELLIB.</p>
        <p>I saw recently a very nice up.to-date evening gown. It was cut round to a fairly deep length in front. The dress was mainly composed of black satin, which came up at the back of the neck, in giving one the idea that it had been raised to pro- tect the back of the neck. On the shoulders were a couple of rings of gold and black braid on either side. The front was ornamented with layers of white lace, which was continued again down the left side of the skirt, one side being firmly stitched to the material, whilst the other end hung in grace- ful folds. The sleeves were continued to the elbows, where the cuffs were turned back and decorated with white lace.</p>
        <p>Yesterday I saw a friend of mine wearing a very pretty and becoming bonnet. The principal orna- ment was a large black velvet bow with the two buds turned up at the back. There was a black ribbon tied in a bow under the chin. The person's hair was not crimped or frizzed, but only just brushed lightly back.</p>
        <p>I boughs the other day a very useful costume of black cloth. The coat reached over both the hips, but not coming down any lower. The sleeves are not very tight although they are a bit large so as to allow of the-enlarged sleeve of the dress under- neath. The coat was an both sides lined with vel- vet which went round the collar. The edges of the jacket both the outside and the underneath were trimmed with astrachan. The collar was a some- what high one, but open in front.</p>
        <p>I see that the polonaise, with a short collar of sable or chinchilla, and a fur hat is again coming into vogue. This is the most dressy costume for 5 o'clock visits for women over 30. The more youthful cling to the attractive fur bolero over the cloth skirt, or, more frequently, the satin or plush skirt, banded with cloth, which is placed in graduated circles from the hips to the hem. The bolero, contrasting with the skirt, recalls the fancy bodice of long ago, adaptable to any skirt. The favourite bodice for this winter is of black velvet, high or décolleté; the former with short postillon cut in a double point in front, and shaped over the hits; the evening bodice with very long points back and front, and laced up the back. This cor- sage is perfectly plain and tight-fitting. It looks very effective in velvet, the same shade as the skirt. When in black it suits every skirt, but then it is less dressy. The black velvet corsage is specially becoming to blondes and good figures. Though these plain bodices are so general I do not wish to imply that the chemisette is non- existent, for it is still worn by women in their first youth, even with evening toilettes, ann is usally composed of delicately-tinted silk. White satin veiled with black tulle beaded or spangled with jet is very elegant for demi-toilette. We must wait the spring for chemisettes of foulard, gauze, and transparent fabrics, and meanwhile may safely content ourselves with those in black, white, and other neutral tints, trellised with velvet moire, corded on satin ribbon. We have also black chenille embroideries on white watered silk, a material which makes bewitching bodices, and indeed dresses for evening wear. Another toilette of white satin had a transparency of black tulle or gauze, embroidered with jet and steel. Though many handsome passementeries, braids and lengths of narrow chiffon, with lace edgings and motifs on it, may be bought by the yard, I think they do not give the same effect as when portions of the dress itself are embroidered. This, however, has been remedied by the introduction of sets of revers, collar and cuffs, large collars and zouaves in cloth or velvet ready embroidered, and in all the most fashionable shades, so that they are easily matched in plain material for the rest of the dress. The usual mixture on these adjuncts is beads, cord, lace, and chenille, and they make up a very elaborate whole.</p>
        <p>Your loving JEmmi.</p>
