      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>TIIE PARR HALL SMOKING CONCERTS,</head>
        <p>TIIE PARR HALL SMOKING CONCERTS,</p>
        <p>There was nothing to complain of on the seore of attendance on Saturday last at the smoking con- cert in Parr Hall; there may have been lees than the previous Saturday but the difference was not very appreciable. Tee programme was not quite so popular a one as that of the preceding week. Tilt- Doric Quartette (gentlemen performers), mandolin- ists, and guitarists, contributed some choice selec- tions including "The Mikado." "Popular Melodies,' and "The Runaway Girl" with good effect Their last item,a fa.stasta on Irish airs, elicited a hearty encore, and the artistes complied with" Queen o' the Earth." Miss Ethel iNewcombe's fine soprano voice was fully appreciated in the song "When the world is fair.' and in re-panse to a recall she sang, " Love was; once a little boy." Miss New- combe also contributed " Roses softly blooming," her voice traversing the difficult trill passages with excellent effect, the audience acknowledging her effort with a storm of applause. Mr. J. M. Bate appeared under rather disadvantageous circum stances as he was suffering lrom a cold. Notwith- standing that fact however, his first song received capable treatment. Mr. Bate's succeeding effort " Alice where art thou," was a decided success, and in response to an encore tie gave "Mary of Argyle." The humorous part of the programme was strongly supported. Mr. George Larmett was en. cored for his humorous song " Kelly the Car- man " and responded. His next • song entitled " Hamlet " was rendered in fine style, and the audience thoroughly appreciated the extravagance and humour of it. " Angus Mac- donald" was capitally rendered by Miss Mamie Peal, who possesses a contralto voice of exceptional range and quality. .An encore was demanded and complied with. Miss Peel also sang" Abide with me in a manner which left nothing to be desired. The audience heartily applauded the singer who responded with another verse. Mr. Cuthert Rose as a society entertainer bears a good reputation, and his humorous sketches demonstrated his ability. For " The waxwork show" Mr. Rose was recalled and his other sketch " People I've met," was re- ceived with warm applause. Mr. Alfred Ainsworth accompanied with his usual taste.</p>
