      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>rHE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 27 1899</head>
        <p>rHE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 27 1899</p>
        <p>11 WOMEN'S CHAT.'</p>
        <p>It is expected of all court officials that they shou attend all State functiona unlesa absolutely prevented b illness or absence. This is especially the case with th Lord Coamberlain who on these occasions is the "acting manager." The Lord Chamberlain is responsible for the issue ot the commands (or permits) of attendance at such functions as drawing moms, State balls, and cora certs, levèa. Royal marriages, christenings, coronations, and funerals. The Lord Steward is responsible for the arrangement of all the State functions. For these duties, both the Lord Steward, and Lord Chamberlain receive each £2,000 a year. F emerly, the Lord Chamberlain, aed the Lord Steward were the real rulers of the household, and as their authority was equal, and their jurisdiction ill- defined, much inconvenience resulted. It was teen arranged that a good deal of power should be &amp;legated to subordinates who were always on the spot, and whose spheres were exactly marked out. The result is that now the duties of the Lord Chamberlain and Lord Steward are mainly of a formal character. The State Ball Room at Buckinghara Palace is a mag- nificent apartment ma the South 'side. It is furnished with three tiers of seats, columns of porphyry support the gilded ceiling, and the filor is of inlaid polish, d wood. The Royal dais is at one end. The Grand Saloon decorated with designs of theifliwers emblematic of the three kingdoms, and mantbus leaves, is in the western front, overlooking the gardens, which are extensive enough to insure a certain amount of privacy. The State Dining Room, where the notabilities are en- tertained has a most ingenious contrivance for conceal- ing the musicians who perform during the progress of the banquet, their gallery being divided by a thin wall of perforated metal, from the principal part of the room. Corridors lead to the various suites of private apart- ments set apart for the use of any member of the Royal Family who may be visiting her Majesty. Those used by the Qaeen are in the north front of the palace, and communicate with the Yellow Drawing Room. Despite the fact that the Duchess of Saxe Coburg Gotha has just celebrated her silver wedding, she is still a very young-looking and beautiful woman, and few, from her appearance, would imagine her to be a grandmother. She dresses magnificently, and with ex- quisite taste, and has taught her daughters to follow in her footsteps. Princess Beatrice is undoubtedly the most handsome of the Duchess's children, and promises to be a very beautiful woman. It is to be hoped, however, she will have a little girlhood allowed her, a privilege refused her three elder sisters. Each in turn was mar- ried when but seventeen years of age, but their alli- ances can scareeiy be termed happy ones. ' Very early marriages are far less common nowadays than was the case years ago, which is distinctly a move in the right direction. A Rid for inetasce,of seventeer, can know little of the world, and her choice at that period of her life is too often bitterly regretted a little litter on. Moreover, to take a girl from the schoolroom, and thrust upon her the duties, and responsibilities of a married woman, is surely being a little hard upen that girl. She has no time for fun and frolic, no time t be gay, and light-hearted. Troubles and responsibilities come soon enough, to the majority, and a girl should have a few light-hearted years, between her school days and married life. The Princess style is more in vogue than ever, f wedding goons. It gives an opportunity fir Icing lines which can be carried out to go xi advantage by the train, now required to be longer than ever. The favourite fabric is still white satin, and there is something about this material, and the way in which the light falls upon it, that is exceedingly attractive. If the satin chosen is not of too stiff a quality, it can be made to fit the figure much better than heavy silk, which may he one reason for its popularity. Of all the recipes given at various times for maiotain- ing the family p« ace, the most inadequate it re ms to me is the recommendation to young wives.—" Always asset your husband with a smile." When a man comes home in low spirits or, dare we whisper, bad temper, he hates his wife to grin at him as though he were a good joke. Smiles are agreeable things—in (heir proper place hut a chronic arraneement of the kind is anything but delectable. Another piece of advice frequently given is, the recemmendation to count twenty before replying to a provoking remark. This is seldom successfully carried out. Suppose for instance an incensed husband to be questioning his wife after the following manner— " What! no breakfest reedy? What is the mesnieg of this delay Madam ?" " One, two, three, four, five." " Why don't you answer my question? Are you mad?" "Six, seven, eight, nine, ten." Well, the increasing storminess of the atmosphere may be tetter imagined than described. The " charming" woman is one who adapts herself to the varying circumstances of life. She knows how to say the right word at the right moment. In conve,st- don she studies the tore points of her acquaintance, friend or relative, and studiously avoids them, adroitly introducing subjects on which they can tall- beat. See is not in the habit of talking about herielf, and her own particular troubles and grievances. Sha.has her bad "days," like everyone else, but she takes care that others shall not suffer on her account, and when she has an attack of the " dumps" she isolate; k rself, in order that the infection may spread no further. She is rib iVei all, a sympathetic woman, and knows how to make people feel that she trios an individual late« e,it in them She is never too busy to lend assistange, and a eh,ke of her hand is as good as any number of words from any- one else. Every now and again a fresh method of shaking hands starts up. Sometimes we are supposed to raise our hand to the level of our bead, and limply wave to and fro, that similarly offered, by the person we greet. At anceher time the pump handle business is in vogue. Such freaks are silly, and, as we eonae to think of it, scarcely in the best of taste. A warm natural hand clasp is far preferable, in every way, aud this we should do our best toseultivate. It is not pleasant on entering a house, to be greeted with a flabby, spiritless handclasp. It acts like a cold douche and often creates a very unfavourable impression on the new comer. The more clinging our skirts, the' more uO-to-dete we are, and with a view to attaining th's end,. many women are patronising knickerbockers doing away with petti- coats altogether. These kniclterbockers, fashioned in washleather. cloth, or satin, are made to fit to perfection and are buttoned on to the edge of the corsets. There are other individuals, of course,whe still remain faithful to their dainty underskir's. but they take care to have them so beautifully cut that the desired " clinging' effect is not interfered with. With black, grey, and dark green gownsia drep shade of heliotrope is admirable for the petticoat, providing that this tint appears elsewhere, or there is no other to spoil the effect. Skirts of green heliotrope too, are very fashionable at the momeet for "dressy " wear. But for walkinglin the streets a gay petticoat is not permissible, "so for that purpose black satin specimens, simply trimmed, are generally worn. In purchasing corsets, we should bear in mind the fact, that any but long-waisted ones, will be useless to us for some time to come. With the present cut of the skirt, long-waisted corsets are imperative. Happily few women lace them more tightly than they should. The wasp waist has gone out,and bids fair to remain "out." We have grown too active, too athletic and too healthy to risk ruining our constitutions and comfort, by tight- la erg. CIBLERY wens thxzess.-ABoil a large head of celery till tender, drain it,and cut into pieces about two incties long. Make about half a pint of melted butter, put two ounces of grated cheese into it, a little pepper and salt. Butter a ph -clish,in wbietelay the celery,pour over it the melted butter, strew it with bread crumbs and a little grated cheese. Bake it just long enough for it to get crisp, and a light brdwn. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>(CoPYright)</head>
        <p>(CoPYright)</p>
        <p>PRINCESS DELILAH.</p>
        <p>By MRS. GEORGE CORBETT, Author of "What the Sea Gives up its Dead," "The Prince Physician," "The Vindication of Olive Trevethick, "The Star of Yukon," "The Adventures of an Ugly Girl," "Deb o' lially's," "A Fair Blockade Runner," dm</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: John Mills, part owner of a gold mine and of a most valuable star-sapphire, loses all to the millionaire, Mr. Rizzen-Quicke, at gambling. Rizzen-Quiae gives it him back on the condition that he never gambles again. In gratitude Mills gives the star- sapphire to his benefactor's wife. Meanwhile two aristocratic scoundrels, Prince Carlos of Mitylens and the Duke of Solltesein, are plotting with a stockbroker named Mosenthal to float a bogus gold. makers' company, and incidentally they hope to capture some of the wealth of Rizzen-Quicke and Mills. John Mills mysteriously disappears after withdrawing 4,000 sovereigns from his bank. Teddy Rizzen-Quicke falls in love with the Princess Delilah, who has some mysterious connection with the Prince and the Duke. She promises to marry Teddy if he can secure the star-sapphire for her. It is Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke's ambition that her son, Teddy, should marry the Princess, and her daughter, Polly, Prince Carlos.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHAPTER V. BOW DID lIE TAKE IT?</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V. BOW DID lIE TAKE IT?</p>
        <p>Polly Quicke was a pretty, sweet-tempered girl of twenty, whom most people would find it some- what difficult to describe, since, without belonging to any special standard of feminine beauty, she was yet a delightful compound of each and all of them. Had she been taller, she might have been gawky; had she been shorter, she might have had the mis- fortune to be dumpy; had she been fairer, some people might have called her insipid, while a few shades more added to the brown of her hair and eyes would have made her too Southern-looking to suit the tastes of those who carry their loyalty to its extremest limit.</p>
        <p>She was bonnie enough to win a certain amount of admiration wherever she went; yet unobtrusive enough to escape the envy and dislike of that class of women which hates to be outshone. In short, she was just the sort of girl that any wise man ought to fall in love with ; for she was also just the sort of girl whom a sense of gratitude would induce to return the affection of any congenial man whose happiness was bound up in her. It will thus be seen that there was ample excuse for Mrs. Quicke's motherly pride in her, as well as for her father's anxiety to avoid urging her to accept a suitor who might possibly find her prospec- tive riches a greater attraction than herself. Personally, she went through life in a very matter-of-fact way, finding a full measure of satis- faction in the present, and anticipating naught but good in the future. Up to date, she was certainly not spoilt by prosperity, and the multifarious blessings that were showered upon her lost none of their zest because they were of constant recurrence. A case in point arose when the wonderful frock which she was to wear at Princess Delilah's long- talked-of.ball came home from the dressmakers.</p>
        <p>"Mummle," she exclaimed, "you really will spoil me if you go on like this! I have so many new things that I hardly know what to do with them; and I am not sure that one girl has a right to have so much money spent upon her." "I don't think you need worry about that, my dear," was Mrs. Quicke's indulgent reply. "It is always better to spend one's money than to hoard it; for every penny that is spent filters down to somebody, and it is quite certain that silk weavers, drapers, dressmakers, Milliners, sempstresses, and scores of other people, will have some profit out of everything you will wear to-night. So you can make yourself happy by remembering that the grand folks you will meet at the ball will only be a small 'part of those whom it will benefit." "H'm 1 I never thought about it in that way before; so, in future, if I run up some tremendous bills, you must put it all down to my natural kind- liness of heart."</p>
        <p>"Yes; I am quite sure that will have something to do with it. But, at the same time, I know you are fond of pretty things for their own sake." "And this really is pretty, isn't it? I shall look even well enough to satisfy Teddy when I get this on, that is, if he condescends to look at me at all. Very likely he will have eyes for nobody but the Princess herself to-night."</p>
        <p>"And that reminds me—he is stopping in bed a long time this morning, surely ? " "No, he is not in bed. He went out soon after nine o'clock, before you came down to breakfast, nmmmie. I asked him if he would be in to lunch, but he didn't stop to give me an answer beyond saying: Oh, bother! Don't hinder me. lam in a hurry, for I have an appointment.' I couldn't help wondering who it was that he had an appoint- ment with, for he isn't often ready to leave the house so early as that." "Very likely he went to execute some commission for Princess Delilah. As you say, he seems to have no time to think of anyone else lately. But I wish he would come in, because your father wants to see him very particularly at lunch-time." Whatever might be the nature of Mr. Quicke's business with his son, it was soon shown to be unlikely of fulfilment, for a liveried footman brought in a letter, which was addressed to Mrs. Quick°, and which was in Teddy's handwriting. "Dear Mater," it ran, "I am leaving London for a week or so, to go on a yachting cruise with a friend of mine, who doesn't want all the world to know his whereabouts. I believe financial reasons are at the ' bottom of this wish; but that doesn't trouble me, for I expect to enjoy myself immensely. Excuse me to Princess Delilah, and tell her that nothing would have kept me from coming to her ball if I had remained in London. I have changed the cheque which pater gave me yesterday, so I have plenty of money to go on with; and for the rest, you can console yourself for my temporary absence by the reflection that I am having a jolly good time of it.—Yours affectionately, TEDDY. "How provoking!" exclaimed Polly. "I was relying upon Teddy for a few dances." Mrs. Quicke did not reply. She was seriously displeased, for this freak of Teddy's was not merely unlike him, but it had been indulged in at a most inopportune time. It also damped her confidence in the immediate satisfactory outcome of what had appeared to be Teddy's certain chance of winning the Princess Delilah for his bride.</p>
        <p>Of course, the Princess could .not fail to be very angry at his defection, for his absence from the ball would be sure to ba noticed; and to say that he had shewn himself careless about enjoying her society because of the casual invitation of a yacht- ing friend, was tantamount to the relinquishing of his suit, for the Princess Delilah Of Courland was not exactly a lady with whom it was safe to trifle. "The boy must be mad," fumed Mrs. Quicke, "for I never knew even him to behave so foolishly before. I was hoping that he would propose to the Princess and be accepted to-night, and now the whole thing is sure to fall through." "Why, Jane, whatever's the matter with you ? " exclaimed Mr. Quicke, who had entered the room unnoticed by his wife. "You look as doleful as if we had come to the end of oni• tether."</p>
        <p>"One of us seems to have done it, at any rate! Just read that." _</p>
        <p>- Saying this, Mrs. Quicke handed Teddy's letter to her husband, who read it carefully through, the while an angry frown gathered upon his usually unruffled forehead.</p>
        <p>"Well, Tin dashed!" he cried. "The young fool deserves a whipping, to throw over such a grand chance as that for the sake of a silly trip in a yacht. He couldn't have done anything orkarder if he'd tried; and it strikes me that I have made a mistake in letting him lead the idle life he does. When he comes back, I'll give him a talking to such as he basnt' had for many a year. The young scamp! He must have intended to do this yester- day. I knew that he had spent a good lot of money lately on presents for Princess Delilah ; but, all the same, 1 thought it pretty stiff when he asked me for a cheque for five hundred pounds, a month before his next quarter's allowance is due." "He says that lie changed his cheque yesterday. I wonder if the man he has gone yachting with was at the bank with him ? If so, we can, perhaps, find out where and with whom he has gone. You see, he put nothing in his letter which would let us know that."</p>
        <p>"I've an hour or two to spare, so I think I'll go to the London and Westminster, and find out what I can about it." _ _ -</p>
        <p>Mr. Quicke bad an expensively-appointed stable, sad was particularly proud of his carriages; but, unless he was accompanied by his wife or daughter, he did not use them a great deal himself, preferring to avoid fuss and to economise time by availing himself of the services of the humble hansom.</p>
        <p>Although the possible rupture of relations with Princess Delilah MIS not quite such a blow to him as it was to his wife, he was very angry at the cavalier manner in which Teddy had asserted his right to regulate Ills own movements; and his an found partial vent in the impatience with which he</p>
        <p>hurried to the Oxford-street Branch of the London and Westminster Bank. Scarcely waiting antil the hansom had drawn up to the kerb, he sprang there- from, and bustled up to the counter, where a few inquiries elicited the information that Mr. Edward Rizzen-Quicke had that morning changed a cheque for five hundred pounds. "How did he take it ? " was Mr. Quicke's next inquiry. "In sovereigns, sir." "The whole of it?' " "Yes, sir. It struck me as rather odd, for private customers usually prefer notes." Up to this moment Mr. Quicke had felt exceed- ingly vexed. But his vexation now melted into sudden dismay, for there was an ominous coinci- dence between the circumstances of his former visit to the Bank of England, and his present visit to the London and Westminster Bank.</p>
        <p>But he was not naturally despondent, and he tried hard to banish his uneasiness by the reflection that whereas John Mills had communicated with nobody since his unaccountable disappearance, Teddy had already written to his mother, explaining that he was simply bent upon taking a short yachting cruise. "So that's all right, of course," he told himself. "Only I felt staggered for a minute when I heard about the sovereigns. I wonder what could be his notion for taking gold instead of notes ? " "Was my son alone ? " he then asked; and it must be owned that his confidence was not increased when he was informed that, to the best of the cashier's belief, Mr. Edward Rizzen-Quicke had been alone when he cashed his cheque, and that nobody had noticed his departure. "I don't like looking upon the black side of everything," mused the successful millionaire as he thoughtfully walked along Oxford-street, oblivious of the fact that more than one "crawler" was touting for his patronage. "But I wish Teddy hadn't changed all his money into sovereigns, for I can see neither sense nor reason in it; though, after all, there may be nothing to worry about." "Good morning, Mr. Quicke. Are you so immersed in thoughts of gold-mining and kindred subjects that you haven't time to speak to a friend ? "</p>
        <p>At the sound of these genially-spoken words, Mr. Quicke looked up and saw the hand of Lord Alfred Vavasour-Pomeroy held out to him in greeting. He shook it warmly, for he had a real liking for this unassuming scion of an illustrious race, and he never felt the slightest constraint in his company, as was the case with most of his titled acquaintances. "Good morning," returned Mr. Quicke, "I was not thinking of business, I was thinking of Teddy. Did you see anything of him yesterday evening?" "No, I have not seen him since the early after- noon, and then it was only for a minute. He told me that he was going to arrange some business about a new yacht, and then he hurried off to keep an appointment." "So he has been buying a new yacht, has he ? " observed Mr. Quicke, heaving a sigh of relief. "Well, then, that settles the matter, and I have nothing to feel anxious about. But why does the boy pretend that he is going in somebody's else's yacht, instead of admitting that it is his own? Upon my word, riches don't always bring an easy mind, and every station has its troubles." Lord Alfred laughed as if he thought that a man of Mr. Quicke's resources would have to go a long way to find trouble of any kind, and lightly made a remark to that effect ere he bade him good after- noon, and pursued the even tenour of his own way. Left to himself again, Mr. Quicke chartered the nearest hansom and went home. But with his usual desire to make the best of everything, he simply told his wife that he had not been able to discovtr whither Teddy had gone, but that he had heard of him buying a yacht. This information was received very quietly by Mrs. Quicke, who had by this time got over the first irritation she had felt at her son's inopportune defection. But she was none the less very much displeased with him, and, but for Polly's sake, she would have given up the idea of going to Princess Delilah's ball, rather than face the anticipated chagrin of the hostess.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VI. OLD ACQUAINTANCES.</p>
        <p>"Polly, you look lovely," was Mrs. Quicke's gratified remark when her daughter presented her- self for inspection, just before it was time to start for Willis's Rooms. "Of all the grand ladies who will be there, there is not one who will make a prettier picture than you do." "Oh, come, mummie," remonstrated Polly, "I can stand a little flattery, but you really mustn't try to persuade me that anybody would look at me twice if Lady Fora Vavasour-Pomeroy or Princess Delilah were in the room."</p>
        <p>"Indeed, they would," persisted the affectionate mother. "I don't mean that you are grandly beautiful like the Princess, or as perfectly formed as her ladyship. But there are lots of people who would turn to you to rest their eves, after looking at the others, just as some would rather look at a bonnie flower than at dazzling jewels." "Speaking of jewels, of course you are going to wear your star-sapphire to-night, mummie ? Every- body will be looking their grandest, and there is no reason why you shouldn't do the same. You don't need jewels to make you, handsome, but I want you to create the biggest sensation in the room, and the star-sapphire will help you to do it."</p>
        <p>"It won't help me to do it to-night, dear, for I do not intend to wear it."</p>
        <p>"But why ? " "Because I should feel very uncomfortable with it on, for it would remind me all the time of Mr. Mills, and I should be wondering if it is really true that he has met with foul play." .trrom this decision Folly's persuasions could not move her mother, who was equally obdurate when Mr. Quicke suggested that she should wear the marvellous sapphire, and the latter readily under- stood the reasons which had prompted her objec- tion to wearing the jewel at present, for he would have been somewhat inclined to adopt the same course himself.</p>
        <p>"To be sure," he said. "I might have • known better myself, and if I were you I wouldn't wear the thing until we have found out what has become of John Mills." As Mrs. Quicke did not, therefore, intend to wear the mystic gem that night, she did not trouble to open the case in which it had been locked. Had she done so, she would have made an astound- ing discovery, which might possibly have thrown a new light upon Teddy Quicke's unceremonious disappearance. As a rule, the people who have the least experience of the ways of society are the ones who are most anxious to convey the impression that they are to the manner born, and as it is one of the chief articles of belief of this class that it is extremely unfashionable to make an early appearance at a society function, it is easily understood why the Rizzen-Quickes were among the latest batch of arrivals. This proved to be mistaken diplomacy, after all, for Princess Delilah, having welcomed nearly all her expected guests, was more at liberty to talk to the late-comers, and the Rizzen-Quickes were subjected to a crossfire of examination which made iul 'east one of them very uncomfortable. I am delighted to see you, .cizzen-Quicke, said the Princess, "and .T sure there are a great many gentlemen in I,e room who will be more than delighted to see your daughter. But what has become of Mr. Edward? He promised to be here very early, and several people have been inquiring after him." "There, I am in for it now," thought poor Mrs. Quicke. "She's sure to be vexed, and I'm blessed if I know what to say to her." She, therefore, felt considerable relief when her beautiful hostess immediately changed the subject, and remarked in a tone of great surprise: "Why, my dear Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke, you have come without your star-sapphire, as well as with- out your son! You must be a very different kind of woman to what I am." "How so ? " was the slightly mystified counter- query. "Of course, I t now that I can never be anything like the Princess Delilah of Courland, but how does the star-sapphire come in ? " "Easily enough. You become the owner of one of the rarest and loveliest jewels in all the world, and then you are content to leave it quietly at home, although everybody else who is here to-night has tried the utmost resources of her jewel-case. Now, if I had that sapphire, I should want to wear it on each and every possible occasion. In fact, it so bewitched me by its beauty that when I heard of the existence of another one, • which is said to be almost its equal, I at once commissioned Mr. Stree to use every effort to procure me the duplicate." "And do you think he will succeed ? " asked Mrs. Quicke, glad to talk of anythlng but Teddy just now. "Yes, I think so. Mr. Stree thought the price demanded was too excessive, and would not conclude the bargain until he hnd consulted me again. mit I tote mm that I meant to have it, no matter what the price might be. I was even hoping that it might be here in time for me to wear it, to- "</p>
        <p>"But you have been disappointed, I see." ; '"Yes, I have been disappointed. Excuse ite moment, there is Duke Otto. He asked me to let him know when you arrived, and I will bring him to you at once." A few seconds later the Duke was inscribing hie name upon Polly's programme, and Prince Carlos was not slow in following suit, though he did not ask for quite as many dances as Polly would have liked to give him, the fact of the matter being that Lady Flora Vavasour-Pomeroy was present, and his heart was urging its right to receive more con- . sideration to-night than he could usually spare from the daring schemes which were part and. parcel of his everyday life. As Mrs. Quicke herself put it, Polly had not the grandeur or the physical perfection of the two titled ladies mentioned. But she was sweet to look upon, and her prospective wealth made her ak personage of such importance that she was fairly besieged until she had promised every dance on her programme. Thus there was no fear that Polly would not be provided with plenty of amusement, and meanwhile, her mother managed to get through the dreaded explanation with the Princess. Instead of appearing vexed, the latter smiled indulgently, and remarked: "So he prefers yachting to dancing? Well, I don't blame him for that, especially as it is my own favourite pastime. But I think I shall take him to task for deserting us so unceremoniously, for I had promised to introduce him to a lovely young friend of mine, the Countess Sforza. ^Perhaps you don't suspect that I am an inveterate matchmaker? But such is the case, and I have concocted quite a pretty little romance concerning your son and the Countess. She is not wealthy, but she comes of a distinguished Hungarian stock, and she would make a charming wife for Master Teddy." Mrs. Quicke tried very hard not to redden with vexation at this palpable proof that she had mis- taken the nature of Princess Delilah's feelings for Teddy. Unfortunately for her chances of success, she was not as yet practised in the art of dis- simulation, and if the deep scarlet hue of her face was an adequate measure of her chagrin, it is small wonder that she found herself unable to answer the Princess otherwise than by maintaining an eloquent silence. "So she never intended having him, after all,' was her mental reflection, "and I never felt so much like a fool in my life as I do this minute. The worst of it is, everybody knows that their names have been coupled together, and we shall get nicely laughed at for our pains." Having' watched her shot go home, the Princest of Courland turned her attention upon some of her other guests, while Mrs. Quicke hastily sought refuge in the society of some ladies who, at that very moment, were envying her the intimacy which she apparently enjoyed with the Princess Delilah. It was some comfort to knoW that they could not guess the purport of the conversation which had just taken place, and so many flattering remarks were made about Polly, that the poor lady's mortification gradually lost its keenness, especially as her duty as chaperon heightened her conviction that her daughter was being duly appreciated, even if her son was not. Master Teddy's absence was commented upon a great deal in many quarters, for his devotion to the Princess had already been liberally canvassed, and there was much malicious smiling among those who believed themselves to have guessed why he had not come. "It is just as I expected," observed Lady Mayfair. "The young upstart has been flying too high, and has received his conge." "I don't know," retorted the Honourable Mrs. Fleete. "It is just possible that he has backed out himself before it was too late. You see, the Princess is not bad-lo_oking ; but a man with such a fortune- as young Rizzen-Quicke will have can afford to look about him a bit before he marries; and he may be wise enough to know that he would be much happier with a quiet, gentle En;lish girl than he would be with a heartless foreign Princess, who it all glare and glitter." "How do you know she is heartless?" inquired Lady Mayfair, smiling inwardly with amusement as she drew a comparison between the Princess Delilah and the Honourable Mrs. Fleete's two marriageable daughters, which was not at all flatter- ing to the latter. "Everything about her goes to shew it," was the reply. "A woman may talk to her for an hour without receiving anything but cold and stereotyped answers from her; whereas men are invariably welcomed with the smiles of an accomplished coquette." "Well, I am glad that I have no son whom Princess Delilah could make a fool of." "I don't think she has made much of a fool of- young Rizzen-Quicke, anyway. Otherwise his people would not have come here to-night as Princess Delilah's guests." "I beg to differ from you. In all probability, the Rizzen-Quickes will be toadied by everybody in society who has marriageable sons or daughters; but I don't suppose that up to date they have had many chances of figuring at quite such a distin- guished assembly as this is, and they would not easily relinquish the prospect of rubbing shoulders with royalty." "The fringe of royalty, you mean. The Princess Delilah claims descent from Kings; and the Duke of Solltesein is a left-banded scion of the house of Hapsburg. But you can't exactly speak of them as royalty. "Perhaps / might not, but the Rizzen-Quickes would; and that explains why they are here, in spite of the apparent collapse of their son's ambition. But who is that short, stout, oily-looking man, who appears to be on such good terms with the Duke? I don't recollect having seen him before; and he doesn't look quite one of us." "That is Mr. Sammie Mosenthal, the great financier. If you judge him only by his looks, you will set him down as an impossible person; but as soon as you remember that he is considered the most phenomenally successful man of the day, your objections to him will give place to ardent esteem.** 'I don't know about that; but there will be no harm in making a social experiment. I will go and ask the Princess Delilah to introduce him.' All unconscious of the honour in store for him, Mr. Sammie Mosenthal, his face beaming with what a social critic had recently described as "his fat smile," was elbowing his way towlirds the refreshment room, when Prince Carlos buttonholed him. "Excuse me a moment, Mr. Mosenthal," said the latter. "I want to introduce you to the great Gold King and his wife, the Rizzen-Quickes. Between ourselves, I think the introduction will pave the way for you to do some business with the Gold King' himself in the matter of that forthcoming flotation of yours." "Certainly," responded Sammie, his smile grow- ing fatter still as he was conducted towards that part of the ballroom in which Prince Carlos had just seen Mrs. Quicke trying her best to look per- fectly happy and at ease. A few minutes sufficed to bring about the desired meeting, and then, much to the Prince's astonish- ment, Mr. Mosenthal's smile vanished, his face reddened with alarm, and he shewed an inclination to run away; while Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke, poor body, turned scarlet with humiliation. "Why, what is tile matter ? " inquired Prince Carlos, looking quizically at Mr. Mosenthal's rosy face. "Have you met Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke before?" "I don't know—that is—no. I'll be 'anged if I have!" was the surprising rejoinder. Then, re- covering his wits alittle, the speaker added: "I beg pard'n, Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke. I'm subject to a touch of the rheumatics sometimes, and I got a twinge of it just then. It always makes me feel red. "Drat it all!" he soliloquised. "Who'd ever have dreamt that I should meet one of my old customers in a place like this? There's one com- fort, though: she won't dare to snlit about ne:e keeping a pawnstop, for that Vskr6ild let her own cat out of the bag. Among the many other good qualities which Mrs. Quicke possessed was the faculty of rising to each and every occasion. re-haps not with lightning.. like speed, but still with sufficient dexterity to avoid floundering too long in the mire of confusion. In the present instance, she swiftly recognised the fact that it was as much to Mr. Mosenthal's interest to bury the past as it was to her own, and the couple were indulging in somewhat constrained commonplaces anent the weather, when Mr. Rizzen- Quicke appeared on the scene. He saw that his wife was talking to someone whose head was turned away from him; but all curiosity concerning the identity of this individual 'was for a moment eclipsed by a remarkable discovery. The stranger sported a huge expanse of white waistcoat, and the thickness of his watchguard, testified to his love of display. But what especially attracted Mr. Onicke's attentimn wss the seal wnicn aepenaea from Ms watchguard ; and, without taking time for a moment's consideration, he abruptly asked the question: "I say, sir, do you mind telling me how you cam% by that seal?" . '\ ill (To be continsed4</p>
