        <p>BREAKFAST AND SUPPER.</p>
        <p>T‘ilgElilVx4 AT STOG'ICTON HEATH. SPEEOR BY MILS. MOSS, MP.</p>
        <p>The National S lhoolroom, Stockton Heath, was I well filled on Aonday evening, when a meeting was held under the auspices of the Sautsford Divi- sion Liberal Association. The chair was occupied by Councillor John Crosfield, J.P. (president of the association), and he was supported on the platform by Captein Rodger, J.?. (cliaii'man of the execu- tive), Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Aldertr en Roberts, J.P., the Rev. J. S. klughee, Messrs. James HOU/UM (chairman of the local committee), Joseph Albiuson, J. W. Bouchier. John Derbyshire, A. H. Dunn, E. England, E. Lloyd Jones, Josiah Longland, Charles Roylance, James Strange, Felix Summers and Joseph Smith (agent). The Cnairman called upon Mr. Smith (the Liberal agent) to read a letter of apology for non-attendanee from Councilor William Pierpoinc, J.P., C.C. Mr. Pier- point wrote that he was very sorry that a very urgent call took him to Scotland that day anl therefore he could not be with them. He wished them a very sue- ceeeful meeting. (Applause.) The Chairm in, who was very cordially received, said he was very pleased to have the opportunity of meeting them at Stockton Reath and he was glad t see that there were a certain number of Liberals in that part of ; the county. (Hear. hear.) He often wondered now it was that any workingman could be found who would vote for a Tory. (Rear, hear.) He did not know whet the reason was. There were only twa things he coed I think of and ome of them was ignorance and the other was the beer berrei. (Laughter.) It was really aston- ishing how many men could b e inaueaced by a quart Of beer. There were some men who would swear that,black was white and blue no colour at all if they offered them a quart of. beer (Laughter.) But the present generation he was bound to sty were really ignorant Of what had been done by our tethers in generations past (Rear, hear.) They d d not know whrn they had to thank to-day for the in .asures which had been passed by the Liberal party of the last generatioe. Councillor Bennett to his amusement said the o her/ day that he (Councilor Croefield) must have a very short memery. If he had got a short oee Chincillor B meet.. must have one a great deal shorter for he 0)1.114 re- womb r the repeal of the corn laws in 1346 wean C eine cillor Bennett wasn't born, (L melte* Toe repeal of the corn laws was the most blessed measure ever pissed in this country. (Hear. near) Everyone of them was happier, more contented and more properous owing to the repeal of the abominable corn laws. Who laid them? They were laid by the Tory party when the Ei mete of Commons was cempesed of T=rer landlords and when they held every Borougn in Eleland in their pockets (Shame.) Thee drove the teeents to the poll just like so many strap. Men had no souls of their own. He had seen them himself driven 'to the poll just as though they were cattle. It took Cobden and Brighe—(epplause)—six years of agitat'oa before they convinced the country and the House of Comm ins of the wickedness of the aorn taws. At that time the wages ot an ageicultural labouring man were about 84. a week. (Shaine.) The price of corn was teigh,y shillings to one hundred shillings a quarter.. A four pretend loaf cost sixteen pence. Toe poor meg could no: afford te hue it; they were on the verge of starvation. He had heard John Bright say that the people ate b welt bread thet his dog would not look at. All the time this starvation wee going on there were houses in Liverpool bursting with corn. Thousands of tons of wheat rotted because the peeple could not afford to buy it. What did the landlords care? The Tory lencrords cared little about it. They wanted their high rents as long as the corn laws allowed them to exact them hem the farmers. It was their own pocket's they were thinking of and, as be had said baf.re, the true defininon of Toryism was selfishness. (Hear, hear.) It was the Liberal p trtv.chiefly throuen the instrum vitality of Cobden and Brit, who repealed those wicked 1 tV73 and England had advanced by leaps and bounds and had continued in prosperity ever since (Hear, bear) yet Mr. Chemherlain had the au lecity to say that au swill reform h.d come fr ern the 0 mservative perte. eus,,hter.) He would like to conscieatieuely k C run. calor Bennett or anyboly else Po tell him of a goel measur e ever passed by a Tory Government. He did oot kilow on e. rive Li oeral party had no; vet finished it; work. (Hear, hear.) Trine was plenty for them to do— (heir, hear)— nd the time would come when they would do it. (Applause.) Their land laws were a dis- grace to the counlry. Let them take the qpiestien of unearned increment and the value of land. friend, Sir John Brunner.told him the other day toat he r !mem- bered 40 years ago there were square mile" of land at 1 Bootle, waste land, without evea a fence round it. New it was covered with houses let on shore leases brineing I - in to Lord Derby theusencll of pound; a year all wh those tales fell in telly would in ore ise his pcoso ority by hundreds of thousands a year. A Vaice : Lord D‘rbet is a good man. The Oh &amp;men : He may hi. I' don't lisp ite it. I am oily qu tug it as a fact. Continuing lh, Chairman said the owner of the land did net pay one shilling of roes or taxes on the increeeel value of the lend. (Hear. hear.) %Vas that right? A Voile : "No it is not.' The Chairman: I call it a burning shame. Proceel- ing h 3 said he was old enough to rementher the time when agricultural land in Warrineten was not worth £3 an acre. It was now covered with buildings and the land was worth from £1.20 to £240 an acre, and not one shilline went towards the rates or taxes of Warrington for that increased value. They must r -member that if a manufacturer built a shed or warehouse the easels- meet was instantly increased and the manufacturer had to pay rates and taxes for every building that w' erected. Ther3 was another paint to be remembered and that was that the increased value of the land was produca I ny the manufacturer;, for if it were not for them the land would temain as it was 40 years ago worth £3 en acre. These were facts he wished to instil into !their m'n is that they might think over and dwell upon. As Liberals they did not want anything but what was right. (Hear, hear.) In the language of John Bright "We plead for jest laws, justly adcninisbrel." (Ap- plause.) Captain Rodger said Ile had much pleasure in movhig the fi-st resolution whieh was : " Tnat this m tog declares its unswerving devotion to the greet principles of Liberalism ; it emphatically condemns the unecrupu- ions efforts of the present GovernMent to f ore, class legislation upon the English people, ani deplores the lamentable result of Coneery Vivo policy ahroa I ; it I. - Heves the country is awakening to the ineffi eiency of the Salisbury ministry, and looks forward to the time when an opportunity may be given to it of declaring that the affairs of the nation, domestic or foreign, shall be placed under the dire (Alen of the Liberal party." Before he said a few word; to beck up this resolution he hoped they would allow him to tell them, as cheirman of the Central Executi^e of this division, what they had been doing lately to help on the Liberal cause. Most of them would remember the last meeting they had in that room. Up to that time Cony were working with an hon. secretary, a man who did the utmost he pessib'y clu'd and did it well. But beginess interfered and prevented him going on with the work, and though the executive tried to retain his ser- vices they had to let him go. After some difficulty they were able to get the services of their present oreanisine secretary and registration agent, Mr. Smth—and he was bound to say that since then meters had greatly progressed. During he past year the registration had been properly attended to, and besides that they had been organising Liberal committees all over the division. This would be a work of a simple nature if they could find men such as they were able to do in this district. But it was difficult to bene them forward in each of the nailing districts, though the executive did its 13,st. Besides that, they had recently taken up the very essential matter of educetioe. They had had one or two meetings in cliff snt parts of the divisien and thee meeting; had had a wonderful eff ,ct in invigorat- ing the Liberaes of the district rounclabeut Not only did these meetings make them more determined to stand up for Liberal ideas, it made them Liberal in another way. They heel no objection to putting their herds in their rockets, and the work the executive was doing re- quired a cettain amount of that kind of help. (Laugh- ter.) Last year they had no less than tier. , large meet- ings in different parts of the division. He sincerely hoped the present meeting would be the means of en- couraging Liberalism in Stockton Heath, which he thought was,perhapiethe worst end of the division. Tater were working gradually towards c msolidatine the (Bei- siren, and he for one did not, think the division hopeleas. The difficuty nowadays was to get a Liberal candidate re come amongst them, make their acquaintance and the acquaintance of every Liberal and Conservative in the division. They were continually prodding the central author;tv in London to see if thee could not find them a good man. (Applause). Alluding to the resolution, Ceptain Rodeer said that they would see that one of the clauses was to the effect that they considered the foreign affairs of the country would be better in the hands of the Liberal party than in those of Lord Salisbury. (Hear hear.) They must also have heard that in the Liberal party there was a discussion which many people put down to differences as r Tarn.; policy. Ne doubt the Tory party made the meet of,, it, but ha must candidly say that he had read bath sides of the qiiestion, and he could not see any reason for such an assertion. There could not be the slightest doubt that a good number of our I aders, and a large section of the Liberal party had become to some extent, Jingoes, What had been the reason that they had come anywhere near to being Jingoes? It seemed to him that they were all so afraid of Lord Salisbury's graceful concession;, that everytedy in the countr7. Conservative and Liberal, was doing his best to strengthen and stiffen Lord Salis- bury's beck. That was the reasan, it seemed to him, that some members of th. e Liberal party had been called Jingoes. Be did not think there was such a thing ask a !Jingo in the Liberal party. (Laughter and Applause.) What was theirs they inteaded to hold and were not gc- km to be done out of by any other country in the world. They would not go to war if they could help it, but he and many others considered that thee graceful concosione were the means of bringing</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>EX- A.MEINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 27, 1899</head>
        <p>THE EX- A.MEINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 27, 1899</p>
        <p>us very near to w tr with Franee. (eppleuee.) Re thoug At that the disti act' y Ji trio speeches m tde by mest of the C tater.: ttive ministers w .re made really to cover L ord S tlisbary's w otkness. (Heir, hear.) ['hey saw t re cluntry did not like these graceall Concessions when it was evident that some- thing was being taken from this kingdom, and the r,- suit was that they did thei very ut noat by being extra Jingo in theiesp3ches to hide Lard Salisbury's week- neei. Tne laree betty of Liberal speakers who 413010 to that effect hal, he believel also thas feslino. If it had no; been for this, how could they tell wile.: would have been don as regards Fashola. Taere Was a lit of fear and tren:bline abut Bahr el Gizal. Most people be- lieved we ought to have the whole of the Nile district in our charge, net only for the glad of E4ypt but for the pad el the whole world, to scaese evnee we t ook pesses- sloe of any cocci on of terri or, we left it open to every ca-leer country. We did net 4E Frenee does, pat a pro- hihitive tariff on go ids which prevented foreign mer- chandise being b meht. (Applause.) Mr. A.. U. Crosfi del said he had reat pleasure in seconding the resolution proposed by their friend Cap- tain Redeer with tiler; eloq ogee ant cta-_,,lrfulness which they all appreciated. A resolution was very often con- sidered a peg on which a speaker might hang his remarks, aro ue theuget toe rasa:ix:ion blihre them was one of the lerust pegs in their peliticed wardrobe. (Laughter.) He thought Mr. M&gt;ss would agree with him tnat it was very cnenorehenst ye aead left full scope for pelicical arguments, and perhaps for political critic- ism. Reference was made in it to foreign politics, which in its elf, ra'er it oceuey a good deal of their time that evening, andhe was deli !hted to second the reso- lution in tte. vim which Captain R. ;deer hal dealt with it. (Hear, hear.) They had heard a ;poi deal abut foreign p elitice and the personal equation had been largely to the fore. Captain Rodger hal told them that the divergence df opinions in the Lbeial party hid been ereggerated. He thought Captain Hedger had hit the nail on the head. (Hear, hear.) lie also thougnt that Sir Edward Grey the other day treated this matter very justly. raking the whole body of the Liberal party he did no bet eve there was one in a hundred who dis- approved of Leg I Reetelery's policy either in respect of the Soudan or that unereky—le would not say ornened—camp sign in toe North West of India. In the one case they had an important example of where Lord Rosebery w as able by no means to play the Doge, but to hold his hand. In the other case he put his feot down, and he thought he was fully entitled to do so. Speaking for himself, tie thoueht it was .a great pity that one of their best provincial j ournale, perhaps the most influential Liberal journal in the kingdom, should attack Lord Reeebery in a way that was un- wise, uncalled for, and unjust. (Rear, hear.) Mr. Morley himself in his speech at Brechin— and he did not believe any sliegle Liberal would forges the life-lone debt they owed to Mr. Merley—(hear, heer)— in in- flaence that would remain with them to their dying day —told them thee; from time to time it was neceesiry to revise their pilicv. Hs thought that in the affairs of State it might very prop erly be as necessary to revise their policy as it was in their own private baeine is. (Hear. heir.) He d d not thi ik they had had a more signal ex ample of that than the important and pregnant manifesto they had the other day from the Cobden Club. If, however, in the language of their friend Cap- tain Rodger, they treated with want of confluence the uncertain action they had had from the present Foreign Secretary, from a certain quarter they were told that they were trying to outbid the Jingoes. Net a bit of it! He would not try to d ,fine a Jingo. Tow had had des- criptions of him lately in tne press. The other day their astute friend Mr. Lebouchere, when the South Polo was said te be or was to be discovered, remarked that the best thing they could do was to go and emelt it— (laughter)—end Rend Ashneea I Bartlett there to act as governor. (Renewed leuehter). Arnenest the many des- criptions he did not know that they could hit off the Jingo more happily that in that description of Mr. L '- beach-re. That, however, dirt not hit them as Liberate off. They believed in cutting their coat aceordine to their cloth. (Hear, heir.) -They did not believe in this earth-lounger that would rush them into war with this country or with that country,and which was usually accompanied by an amount or arrogance. brag, an! bluster which were superfluous and undignified. (Applause.) Vacillation and uneertaia3y of purpors did not pay. (Hear, hear.) They wanted a firm policy firmly prosecuted, and if as people told them, the eereonal abilities of leaders were going to tell MOM and more in the future in the fortunes of parties, then he subinit'ed that tteey had in L ord Resebery a more able and vigorous man for the conduct of their foreign affairs than they had in Lord Salisbury. (Applause.) But there was another expert and vigorous gentleman familiarly kaown as " Joe--" but there were drawbacks to their friend "Joe," if they would excuse him using the expression. There was a certain amount of playing to the gallery and there were undignified methods in his now Melo- mecy which were not in keeping with the best tradi- tions, and not in keeping with their dealings with for- eign questions. (Hear. hear.) Whethe. it was in his deal- ings with colonial metters:or questions of home Policy he thought that in either ease they had in Lori Resebery and in Mr. Isquide men they could better trust thee they could Lord Salisbury and Mr. Joseph Chamberlain. (Hear, hear, and applause.) He wool! only say one word in conclusion with reference to home matters. The Chairman had told them that their work was not. finished. Tat was a very goad answer to those who said that they, the Liberal party, hadn't got another shot in their locker. They knew very well that there was a good work in front of them as there was behind them.—(hear, hear,) and he could not say more than that. Is was not fir him to enter intodeteils, as he was only a tyro,and they were to listen shortly to an expert in political matters. But there had been a reference to the question of uni earned increment. It was only one of many, but it We, certainly one of the most important qeestions. Liberal- ism would have to coaseientiously deal with it in the future. There were few questions which were more difficult and complex. But tee believed this question had already been dealt with in one of the States of America. add theymere advised by lawyers of the highest stand- ing that it was a question which it was competent to deal with, and when the time came it was one of those questions which, with all due regard for private pro- pe.ter, they would be able to deal with, not only retro- spectively, but with regard to the constant jeer Ass of value in lands which was going on in towns. Waether they took that or their old friend the drink question. which aroustel so much feeling, in either came there was any amount of work before them, and he believed the people would follow them and support them (Arpeause.) He believed they would support them better if they hod not a nameless cenjecture that Liberalism was associat d with revolution and communism. Les them remem- ber what Mr. Gladstone said" That the best and highest kiod of Liberalism Was in the last resort the best kind of Conservatism." He had much pleasure in seeendine the resolution. (Applause.) Mr. S. Mesa, M.P., who was cordially received by the meeting, said he congratulated the Liberals or the Knutsford Division on the fact that they were bestirring themselves. He thought they had, for lone enough, left' Sir John Brunner in a position of splendid isolaeion. Not that he was unworthy for that or any other position, because a better man or a sounder Radical never entered 1 the House of Commons than Sir John Brunner. (Hear, hear and applause-) He dial not think they were doing Sir John or thernielver justice, when they expected him to fight the whole battle of Cheshire Liberalism in the House of Comrnonr, and he had every reason to hope that when the next genera election place he would have half-a-dozen ijaibderattak members ' to accompany him. (Hear, hear.) T resent time, a crisis in the Lib Tat thought the Liberal Party would come bad as with extreme revs:. that, a short welt Party,teaelire o iwtbauostf a it t h. e the It wp time ago, along with almost every oth sr Liberal, he saw that Sir William HirlOUrt bad thoueht fit to resign his position as leader. No one had done more valuable ser- vice in the House of Commons, or better service for the country, than he lead. As Chancellor of the Excheqixer, his famous budget alone sufficed to immortalise him as one of the foremost and most benevolent of Eiglish Statesmen. (Hear, hear.) Whatever might have led to his resignation they might depend upon it that when the time came a leader would be forthcoming to lead the party again and again to victory. (Applause.) He was not one of those wine looked with a Liberal party to-day. From his own experience he pessimistic eye on the condition of the would say that the rank and file of the party were never stronger, more combined, more hopeful, or more deter- mined to stick to Liberal pripciples than they are to-day. (Applause.) The Liberals had an ad .erse inajtrity in the House of Commons, and a law one too, but the Liberalism of the country Was rising, and they might depend upon it that when thenext general election came Lit oral principles which had been turned aside by the futile promises made in '95 would burst the bounds whine had held them down so long, and they would find in power once again the party of prosrress and peace. (Applause.) Who were they who firing stones at the Liberal party and said they were rent asunder by sectional differencts 2. One very powerful stateman was speaking a few days ago at Wolverhamp- ton, on the same evening that an rqually celebrated gentleman was entertaining his friends in the ancient city of Bristol. Both gentlemen were entitled to speak on , tehalf of a very large section of the Conservative party,and both were men of Cabinet rank. Mr. Chamber- lain was one and Sir Micha4 Hicks Beach the other. Was the party that was flinging stones very united P Let him tell them in a few words what Mr. Chamberlain said. He said that the British Empire, to maintain' its unique positien, must be ready to take its fair share of further expansion. If we got into a limited corner we must exnaed, get further out, and take territory wher- ever we found at. Sir Michael Hicks Beach. Clean-eller of the Exchequer, and a most prudent gentleman, said it was of no Use for us to add to our territories. We could not do evers thing at once, and in his (Sir *ewers) opinimi we should be wiser if we attempted te develop what we had acquired than to add still further to the extent of our Empire. Mr. Chamberlain at Welverhatni • ton, sad Sir Michael Hicks Beach at Bristol. were. said Mr. Moss, flinging a stone at each other. This flinging of stones seemed to him vary much like a case of the pot calling the kettle black. (Rear, hear, and applause.) Mr. Chamberlain had also said that the Con:ervative party was the party to carry</p>
        <p>I the English flag all over the world, and the trade in its Itrain. Had he forgotten Madagascar ; had he forgotten Siam; had he forgotten Tunis; had he forgotten that this Tory ministry whice was geine to meke the world tremble did not hesitate to send an Eegliee ironclad out Ira tele bile iiterrenean to shell tbe Cretans fighting for ' freedom. ("Sowell.") Had be forgotten that? Turn- ing to affairs in Mad igascer and the alteration of t %riff declared by the French PresilenteMr. Moss said that the experts of British produce fell frnm £34,000 in the first three months of 1897, to £10,0J3 in the first three months of 1898. It Mr. Chamberlain had cared to di- vulge the whole of the knowledze in his pesssssion, he mignt have told his hearers its Walverhempton that al-1 tnoegh we were endeavouring to open up fresh sources of trade we had not been strong enough or wise enouzh under a Conservative Government to stick t 3 and develop what we had got. What about ' China ? Mr. Chamberlain aliuded to Chinese affairs with gusto. If there was any piece of policy in this wide world which made any man, even though he was not a Jingo, feel ashamed of the foreign policy of this country it was the policy of viccillatioa in China. Uneland had ironcleds in Chinese waters, and about Port Arthur, when Russia declared that these ironclads were not very acceptable to her. Then this iron minister, or as the Germ ans would rather have him, this lath painted to look like iron, told the irenclads to withdraw. Lord Charles Beresford said about it, and he was one of the party which thought proper to fling stones at the Liberals for disseasions,that the episode of the witedraw al of our squadron from Port Arthur was one of the most nurniliating 'binge that ever happened to this Empire. (Applause.) Thus snake Lord Charles fteresford of his own Government and of his own foreign minister. (Laughter.) Talk about , sectional differeaces.there were almost as many amongst the Conservative party as there was of a majority. Th-re was scarcely a measure which was not opposed by the Conservative members,thought they always wets loyal to the crack of the Conservative" whip." (Laugh- ter.) Throughout the whole of last session the Conser- vative press and the Conservative party were as much divided as they could possibly be, and he believed al- though for the moment without a recognised leader, the Liberal party had never been stronger,more full of hope and never been sounder in its allegiance to Lioeral pria- ciplee:than it was at this moment. (Applause.) Mud- nog to the assertion that good trade followed in the wake of tee Conservative Government be said he thought that bubele had been burst long ago. I seemed to him to on one of the in oat redieulous and foolish statements that could passibly be made by any man who was at all conversant with the history and trade of his country. Almost every Conservative candi- date at the last general election th ought fir te put such a statement in his election address, at the same time asserting that under a Liberal Government wages went less and prosperity vanished. As a matter of fact id 1879 when we had a Tory Government the export and imports per head of the popu- lation came to £l7 18s 3d; in 1883. under a Liberal Government they came to £2O lls 3 1 ; in 1887 , under a Fory Government they amounted te £l7 111 8,1 and they worked out to something similar up to the present time, except during toe last few yeees in which mere has been an increase which would, no doubt, con- tinue. If they took the exports wily in 1878, under a Tory Gevernmant, they were £5 lie 8 1; in 1835, under a Liberal Glevernrnent, £8 173 al ; in 1887, under a Tory Government £8 lie. 3d; and in 1792 under a Tory Government, £5 19s. 21. Ic was enough for him to say that that trade argument was all non- sense. Neither Government had to any extent a right to claim good or bad trade, because the Government and policy of this country was continuous, and could not be changed when one Government stepped into the shoes of another. If any vary had a claim to the good trade, the figures, as it s 3 happened, were on the side of the Liberal party. (Ap- plause.) The foreign policy of this Government ha] not been pleasing either to the manhood or Christianity of the English people. On the North-Western freatior of India sound pledge; that toe previous Government had given not to occupy that pert of the country were broken, and a war with toe people was the result. The English name was tarnished, and an Englishman's word was no longer looked upon as his bond. The people wrio would have been our strongest allies in the event of war with Russia were now our determined fees. In Armenia the benevolent policy of Mr. Gladstone was reversed, and while the Sultan sat gloating over the atrocities commit-ed on the Armenian Christiens, we as a member of the European concert, were doing nothing. ("Shame.") He did not think fur a moment, it a man was Conservative or Libe- ral,h id he one spark of English manhood and Christian- ity in his nature that he would give his support to the foreign policy of Her Majesty's Government.(Applause.) Fh ey were told at the list election teat if the Conserva- tives were returned to power they were going to do certain things. Toey were going to give a measure of' lend reform; an improved system of education; old age pensions and atiout a score of other things, teiree or four only of which they had touched and touched in a very imperfent manner. Old age psnsione were still discussed by Mr. Chamberlain but this united Conser- vative party coal , n cc agree atout the Bill which was to be introduced for granting old age pensions. Sir E I ward Clarke did not aporove of it ; Mr. Bilfour thought it was a vary difficult subject; Mr. Chamber !ie still advo- cated it ; and Sir Michael Hick- -Beach—if he under- stood him rightly—did not agree with the proposed Mea- sure. (Laughter.) What was to become of the old age pension scheme he (Mr. Moss) did not know. He quite agred that it was a very difficult measure, but pe vie should not promise to solve difficulties at election times .!.iet shelve them when they get Into office. (Aeplanse.) What about the measure of land law reform? (Hear. hear.) He did not know whether there were any farmers present but it seemcd to him that land law reform was still a lon way off. They had had an Agricultural Rates Act by means of which the farmers were relieved of half their rates; they were at least supposed to be by the Act.(Leughter.)Some landlords told him they reaped seine benefit from it, and no doubt the farmers would agree with the landlord who said he was very much benefited by it. (Laughter.) The Agricultural Rating Act sees a retrograde mmaure and a very iniquitous measure. (Hear,heer.)Ele thought they would agree with him that the people in the towns—even those employ- ed at the brewery close to—had as much right so long as they were carrying on a legitimate trade which the law sanctioned to claim the laws to protect them in their industry as the farmer had. (Hear, hear.) He did not wish to say one word against the farmer. Rates as a ; rule did not come down from heaven. Tiny came from the workmen of this country, and if the farmer was ' relieved of half his rates who paid the other half? (Rear, I hear.) Not the Government. No. The Government ' got the money from somebody. The rest of the money ' came fro= the towns and the manufacturing districts, and he centende 1 that the Government when they freed Ithe landlord, the farmer and the farm labourer of half their rates were dealing inequitably with the men of the ' towns and manufacturing districts when they called upon them to pay other people's rates. (Hear, hear) It. the Government intended to legislate in the interests of the people of this country why did they not help Mr. Lambert to carry his Bill for the refo-rn of toe land laws in the previous Parliament? Everything which the agriculturists advocated was practically em- bodied in his bill, but they found that nearly tbe whole of the Conservative party opposed Mr ',embed when he introduced it. If the present Government really inten- ded to reform the land laws did they think they would. have left untouched the question referred to by the chairman in his eloquent address? The question of land values and the question of mining rents and mineral royalties needed attention. (Hear. hear.) The Chairman rightly told them that if they added one little building to a manufactory, if they added to a colliery or even if they added a pigstye to their dwelling the assistant overseer came round, and added to the assess- ment and rates were paid for it. But Lora Ersrby and the great owners of land, drawing royalties from minerals in Lincaehire, in North Wales and South Wales some of whom received thousands upon thousands of pounds in the course of every year for minerals which were being removed, and consequently the capitalised value was leaving the distries involved, these men were not paying one single fraction of a halfpenny towards local rates. (shame.) The collier who frorn his hard earned weekly wage built himself a cottage had to pay rate*. The mine owner— he did not mean to say the colliery owner—but the owner of the minerals, the ground landlord, might take £15,000 oe £50,000 per annum from the same concern and yet he did not contribute one single fraction of it towards local rates. (Shame). There was a marked difference between the principles of Conservatism and the pincieles of Liberalism. (Hear. hear.) They would find in the history of the past that from the time when the House of Commons was occupied exclusively by the landlord class until to-day' that the Conservatives had legislated for class privilege and for the protection of ' the classes. (Hear, hear.) On the other hand from the time when the English democracy was first admitted , into the English House of Commons until now—from the 1 time of the great ref orm bill through the time of Cebden anti Bright until to-day—Liberal principles had been principles of trust in the people ; principles which broke down class legislation; principles which tended to the uplifting of the working classes and principles which gave them a :heap loaf and which took away from them the grinding poverty which was making paupers of the people of this country. (Applause.) If the Conserva- tives had agreed to grapple with the question of and law reform they must and would have to include the neiehlenarine question of ground rants and mineral royalties and would have to relieve the t ixation of the eliontry by imposing a tax upon £13.000,000 or £15,000,000 which was now received for mining rants and royalties. (Applause.) He need not go further into that question. They had had from the Government an education act. He complained of the conduct of the Government in the matter of education. (Hear, hear) In the year 1870 they had passed in this country an act which introduced elementary education. There was at that time a compromise between the two extremes in the country. Such eleme itary schools as that in which they were now gathered were allowed by the compro- mise to receive from the Government in return for being allowed to teach their own particular doctrine, a grant equal to the exact amount of the voluntary contribu- tion,. In 1876 that compromise was broken,and Rehr-</p>
        <p>ian or velunt try schools were all oval to ITC3i7, as much as 17s. 61. per head without taking hat° coasid ation the v Auntary contributions. Time went on and in tee manufacturing districesseepeci- ally in his c ountry—W ales ; weere they were strong educatienelists and in alvance of England in this matter of elucatiln, having already in existence the intermedi- ate E location Act ant in possession of couaty schools, it was f ound that there was not sufficient accemmola- tion for the education of the children. It was decided to form a school rate he order to build schools and give their children the best education the land could give. (Efeaie hear.) Toe teachers were paid well and the lads arid I lases turned out to be intelligent and well educated people. Whet happened? A year or two ago the ex- treme clerical party got alarmed-and went to land Sells- bary and said !‘ We cannot compete with these rate- aided schools. Our schools cannot stand it ; tee ptople won't pay for it and we c oanot give our teachers as much as the School Boards pay theirs." One would have thoioght that if the Board Schools were the best then the better policy to adopt would be to make them all board schools. That, how- ever, dii not suit the extreme clerical party. They wanted to teach their own particular reegious doctrines and at the same time receive public money to do so. He (%le. Moss) strongly objected to b dug compelled to pay for the religious teaching of any child or of any per- son with which he did net agree. (near, hear.) The man who finds the money has a right to com- mand the tune. But Lord Salisbury thought not. He decided to assist theee voluntary schools and what was the result ? Tee National and voluntary schools cf this country were getting to-day 53. per child mere than they were a year or two ago, whilst the Board schools for ail psactieal purposes were left out in the cold. (Shame.) Toe people who were prepared to undergo heavy sacrifices to enable them to build schools and maintain schools from the rates w. re now cast aside in order that s ect irianisin in 5,000 or 6.000 parishes where the people were not prepared to put their hands into their pockets to maintain their own schools might be maintainee by an additional grant. He would give them an illustration to show how improper it was that schools sheuld be assisted out of 'public money when those con- nected with teem made no attempt to carry on the edu- cation of their children. The school was St. John's School, Birkenhead, and he was quoeing from &amp; report made in 1898. The report showed that the average attendance was 1140,and the voluntary enntributioes amouotee to the magnificent sum of £7 7s, That showed clearly that if they could get the money from Government they would not pay. (Laugh- ter.) The tetal expenditure for the year was £1,780, and the Government d-ducted £l3O from the grant of 17s. 61. per head. Nevertheless the income exceeded the the expenditure by £lOO and the managers pre- ferred to lose this sui plus rather than pay attention to the demand of the Government teat certain improvements in the acecommodrtion should be made. Hundreds of similar cases might he produced in Lancashire and Cheshire. He was net going to say one word against the religion of any man. God f rbid that he should stand between any man's religion and his carrying it out, but he did say " don't let that man ask ale to pay for it." (Hear, hear.) If they thought it wise to educate their ceildren in their own religion by all means let them do so but d not let them coins and say we want the Government tolpay for it. He hoped they would bring their minds to bear on the questions which hal been discussed and if they found that the principles of Liberalism which had been spoken of were the principles which were going to uplift and benefit tee democracy of this conntry then he asked them to be true enough to themselves and their censcieades to sup- p net a Liberal at the next election. (Loud cheese.) Alderman Roberts said he had a very agreeable duty to perform, and that was to propose that the best teanks of the meetidg be given to Mr. Moss for his ad- dress. (Applause.) An abler or more instructive ad- dress he had net listened to fora long time, and he was not without hope that it would have ice effect upon some of the gentlemen present that night. (Hear. hear.) He had heerd it sail that there was no such a thing as gra- tituds in politics ; but so far as he was personally con- cerned he was a living example of that being an abso- lute falsehood. He was a Liberal in politics because if is were not for the measures which had been passed by the Liberal party in years gone by he would not now have been holding that very honourable and dignified po -Noe of an Alderman of the Borough of Warringtoe. (Hear, hear, and applause.) It was a fact that in his lath-rea time people who had the impertinence to hold Nonconformist views were by statute debarred from holding any civil position in the State, and that was not very long ago. He was very pleased to see such a number of young men present. It Yves worth their while to real the political history of the;r country, to learn where they were and from whom they had got their good. It was the sacrifices of the Fowl° of the last generation or two, who gave up many chines, cecafort of mind, money,position and everything el,e for principle, to which they owed the privileeea of the positions they were allowed to occupy that day. (Applause.) On that account he was Liberal, and he believed that the party wnich had done so inula for None einfoemity in the past was much more likely to be the party to ch something in the same direction in the future thin tee Conservative party was. He was elec. a Liberal because he had a certain lamellae towarls temperance reform. He was not going to say what particular platform he was on,but it was extremely likely that whatever legislation they might have ire the direction of temperance reform it would come from the Liberals rather than from the Conservatives. (App! 'use.) On the question of the Liberal leaiersnip, tno speaker said that if there was anything wrong with the Liberal party with regard to it, it was the fact that they had too many possible leaders rather toaa too few, but the differenee of opinion which always arose wnee the party suffered de:eat for the Ulla being had arisen in years gone by, and would arise again, but that was no reason why the p arty should be dismayel as long as their priocipies were right, fair and reasonable. (11.er, hear.) It was only for a time. Alluding t 3 Mr. Kensit, Alderman le Alerts said he Was a man who was going to 03US3 a considerable amount of trouble for the Conservative party et the next general election. He, like Mr. Moss, claimed for himself weat he would allow to every other man—absolute right of private judgment in all matters connected with religion. (Hear, hear.) He had a right to worship God according to the dictates of his own conscience, and when. and where, ani how he liked, and so had ()tree), other man ; but it w ould be a difficult thing for the minister of tee church to which Li. b iloneed to teach them Romanism,and all that be`onged to it. What he said was that in the National Church, which was supposed to be the bulwark of Protestantistn,the Govern ment of the country and the people had at right te oecti that men who entere I inside its borders as the teachers of religion seoulcl at any rate be loyal to the vows they rindertoek wheit they joined the ministry. (Hear, hear.) Referring to the lieutsford Division, the Alderman said that he was not without hope, given a suitable candi late, that there would be an opportunity some day of gettine the right man for the right place for the division,and then the wails of that Tory Jericho would be brought to the ground. (Applause.) Mr. Longhand seconded the snotionetad said he hoped that the result of the meeting, as had been expressed by a previous speaker, would be to put new life into them as a Liberal party in that division. (Hear, bear.) Tll9 motion was carried untntmeusly, and Mr. Moss briefly responded. Mr. Mounfi eld prepesed a vote of thaeks to the chair- man, and remark ed that they had in I-. Crosfield one whe was always ready to put his shou.sier to the weepl for the welfare of the public gener ally. Tney respected and esteemed Mr. Crosfield, and the speaker hoped he might lone live te help on the Cause of freedom and justice. (Anplause ) rhe Ref. J. S. litighes seconded, and said that in the immediate neighbourhool there Was no man more highly respected anti esteemed by all classes and sectio as than the chairman of that meeting. (Hear, hear.) They liked his robustness, his outspokenness, and his sturdi- ness and general peilanthropy, and they also liked him because he was loyal to the Liberal party. He felt that they were indebted to Mr. Crosfield not only as chair- man of that meeting but as Chairman of Knutsford Division, iii which he worked quietly anti steadily. The rev. gentleman mentioned that the division Mr. Moss represented was his own native division, and in 1863 he did his very utmost, as a boy with ate excellent pair of lungs, to further the candidature of Mr. Moss's illustrious predecessor, the Rieht Han. Sir George Osborne Morgan. (Applause.) He was pleased indeed to find that Mr. Moss was Ca eturde Liberal of Welsh make Come to this united Saxon meeting to stir them up. (Applause.) In conclusion the speaker expressed the hope that more meetings of this character woold be held in that part of the Divis'een, and was convinced that some day victory would result from their efforts. I (Hear, hear, and applause.) This motion was agreed to with heartiness.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : This is the best Liberal !meting I have attended in this district for the last 20 years. (Laughter.) We may be a binighted lot, but we have some strength in us yet. The gathering then dis,.:mel.</p>
        <p>WARUING TON POLYGLOT CLUB.</p>
        <p>Oa Frilay evening last the Polyglot Club com- menced Ps second term of meetings, when a good number of members and friends assembled. The chair was occupied by Mr. Comber, who opened the meeting with an address relative to the objects of the club. An interesting programme was gone through, including vocal and instrumental music by Messrs. F. Fieldhous3. W. A. Woods, and Miss Bouchier ; legerdemain by Mr. A. J. Sherwood, and addresses, readings, &amp;c., in French, Spanish, and German by Messrs. Ronan,Storey, Gerrard, Kirk, Kirtland, and Sherwood.</p>
        <p>For the past quarter of a century there has been to ontinuous flow of letters bearing testimony to the tray wonderful cures effected by CLARKE'S WORLD-FAMED BLOOD MIXTURN, " the finest Blood Purifier thlt science and medical skill have brought to light." Sufferers from Scrofula, Scurvy, Eczema, Bad Legs, Skin and Blood Diseases Pimples, and Sores of any kind, are solicited to give it a trial to test its value. Sold everywhere, at 2s. 9d. per bottle Beware of worthless imitations and substitutes.—Advt. (6)</p>
      </div>
