      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>JESSIE</head>
        <p>JESSIE</p>
        <p>The letters wa are permitted to publish,written by pelplo who have had every opportunity for judging of tha merits of Scott' Emulsion, are surely worthy of more than passing notice. A. preparAtion which has for its basis such standard remedial agents as cod-liver oil, hypoph)sphites and glycerine, stands out conspicuousiy as a genuine remedy, free from any of the taints of qua "Try. Everybody who is ill, or in poor health, want 4 a remedy t'-:at is effec- tual, and we know of no better proof of the efficacy of Scott's Emulsion than the testimony of those who have tried it. Here is a letter which speaks for it- self :</p>
        <p>" 30, Millbank-street, Westminste,r, "August, 6th, 1898.</p>
        <p>" Geatlemen,—Our little daughter Jessie was very weak last winter as the result of having bad a bad attack of measles. We treated her with various medicines, but she grew weaker, and we were at a loss t) know what to do for her. White she was in this weak state we read one of your little pamphlets about Scott's Emulgioa, and as the medicine she was taking was not doing her any good, we thousfht we would try your preparation. The result of taking Scott's Emulsion can be told in a very few words. The child simply began to gain</p>
        <p>COCK</p>
        <p>from tha beginning of the Scott's Emulsion treat- meat, and was quickly restored to health. Yon have my heartfelt thanks for giving to the public a genuine remedy. Scott's Emulsion is all that you claim tr it and more. Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>"(Signed) %V.. COCKS."</p>
        <p>The foregoing letter is a t Air sample of the many we are receiving every day from all quarters of Her Majesty's Empire. Ie is free from borrabagt, and tAls our story for us in a few words. The child was in poor health,and after taking Scott's Enauision she speedily got well again. The nourishing and curative properties of Scott's Emulgion are unquestionable. This is the most pglataVe and easiest focal of cod-liver oil, and the addition of glycerine and hypophospiiites to the oil greatly enhances its value Scott's Emulsion has the endorsement of the medical profession and medical journals tnroughout the world. For sale bv all chemist.</p>
        <p>You may obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by meniLng tbrelpence to cover postage to Scott and flowne, Ltd., Manufacturing Ctiemiats, 95, Great S &amp;run Hill, Loath; E C,, and mentioning this paper.</p>
        <p>lecture t+ An appreciative audience. Mr. d. fnornto oc luoied the chair, and opened the meeting by a byrn selec'el from El yie's Temperance Melodies, after which he made a fvw remarks about the lecturer and the edifying and instru3tive treat befor s these present. Mr. Taylor opened the lect,ire by a few well-directed remarks dealing with toe subject before blin,after which he illustrated the evilf effects of ale !hol upon several kinds of fool, showing how it hardened the same, making it indigestible, and consequently injurious to the sys'em. Re also extracted the alcohol from several kinds of intoxicating drink,barnin,elthe alcohol. At the close of the lecture Mr Thornton moved a vote of thanks t) Mr. Taylor, and this being seconded by Mr. J. Drury, t was ctrried nnaniolowtv vr•ir,h applause.</p>
        <p>BILLIARDS AT HOME-A cVARRINGTON INVENTOR.'</p>
        <p>PATENT.</p>
        <p>This week we have had an opportunity of in- specting an ingenious invention patented by Mr.. James F. Brown, of Marshhouse-lane, for improved table billiards. Many inventions have, of course, been placed on the market with the same object in view, but the results obtained have been so unsatis- factory that they have generally been thrown up in disgust, and billiards at home has been confined to the limited few who have been able to furnish a billiard room. Mr. Brown, however, seems to have soled the difficulty, for by means of his invention an ordinr.ry dining table can be transformed into an excellent billiard table in the space of a few minutes. The table is cover:ld with the ordinary billiard cloth and is secured at the sides by a de-. tachable cashion in six pieces handsomely fitted up and supplied with the usual number of pockets. Special care has been taken in ihe construction of the cushion,which is made of the best rubber and enables the player to make cushion cannons or other shots with the same ease as on an ordinary billiard table. The balls and cues used are the same as in billiards; in fine, a table fitted up with Mr. Brown's inventions is the nearest approach to a standard billiard table we have yet seen. A true level can be obtained by the use of lifting cups under the legs of the table, and a spirit level. A great a'vant age is the ease with which the table can be fitted up and detached without marking or injuring the table in the least. Already several of Mr. Brown's inventions are in use in the town and they have given the utmost satisfaction. The reasonableness at which they can be supplied and their adaptability to any size of table should lead to a large demand.</p>
