      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>OUR, LOCA.L CEICTIZCIIES.</head>
        <p>OUR, LOCA.L CEICTIZCIIES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIErY.</p>
        <p>Toe meeting of tali society on Suo.-lay afternoon was presided over by Mr. Mill, who spoke upon the import-- anus of choosing the right way in life. The speaker was Mr. Sharpe, of Manchester, and he discoursed on the subject of self-denial, if by their conduct they were likely to turn others into a course which weal be to their disadvantage. Mr.A.Griffiths gave a capital ran .er- ing of the silo "The Holy City." ie service was also taken part in by Mr.Farrar and Mr. Healer. There was a good attendance' and the meeting was greatly appre- ciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>CHRISTADELPHIAN ROOM</head>
        <p>CHRISTADELPHIAN ROOM</p>
        <p>The subject of the lecture on Suoday by Mr. Morris, of Manchester was" Thou art Peter and upon this rock I will build my church." Toe passage, quwel as a sub- ject,was, Mr. Morris asserted, one upon which tho Ro• man Catholics based their prof-Nsions upon teing the one and only Church of Christ. It was claimed to:it His Church had always been here and that wither Estab- lished Church nor Dissent could justly sac that they complied with th's condition while the Roman Catholic Church could 4how greatsr antiquity than asy ot thetn. Antiquity was not, however, enough to estats'i this claim for there was the Greek Church which bad equal claims in this respect and of toe two errors was to be prr. ferrei for some things named. The lecturer' watchword was "To the law and to the testimony;if they speak no according to this word, it is because there is no light in them," and it was on this basis that he woul.l exami the passav in que;tion. After a Qritical cinsiderat on of the meaning of the w ,rds used, and a det iile:l analy- sis of the character and life of Peter, showing in what particulars he was unsuitable as a foundation upon which the Church should be built, Mr. Morris proceedei to show that the HousehOd of God was built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ himself being the chiA corner stone, and that" other foundation can no man lay than that is laid, which is Chtist Jeit.l9." The excelFncies of Peter's character were not detracted from by exposiag the unrealities of los Churches' Peter and cmnparing them with the Peter of the Bible, and he appealed to his hearers t ascertain what were facts and hold them f sst irrospective of, who did or did not believe them, for th, belief of a false thing thoughlmoiyersal would not make it a fact nor would the universal rejection ol a fact make it an untruth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>HOLY TRINITY tTIUROH.-CHOIR FESTIVAL.</head>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY tTIUROH.-CHOIR FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday a choir festival and musical afternoon cc-- vice was held at Holy Trinity Church. Th music in- cluded the following items :—" Etc, was despised," MiS9 A. Bate ; chorus, "Once in Bethl,hem of Judah," the choir ; "If with all your hearts," Mr. J. Thorley ; them," Behold. hew good and joyful," choir ; organ solo, " Adeste Fideles," Mr. Percy Randall ; "Glory to Thee," Mrs. D. S. Stone ; chorus, " Gloria," from the 12th Mass, None Dimittis, and "Lord, now 'eV:est Thou Thy servant," the choir. The solos were, upon the whole, very creditably read -red, the organ solo was brilliantly executed by Mr.Ran(Vll,and the choruses and anthems were admirabl^ sum by tie choir. During the service, the Vicar, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, delivered a short address on Church Services. At the conclusion of the evening service Mozart's " Gloria" from the 12th Mass was sung by the choir. The collections which were in aid of the choir fund, arnoueted to nearly £lO. ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>At St. John's Presbyterian Church, Winwick-street,on Sunday evening a sermon was preached by the Rev. J. Warnock, pastor,on "The New Free Church Catechism." The rev, gentleman said that during the present month there was published simultaneously in England and America a little book of more than ordinary significance, weighty enough to demand their most diligent study. The authorship was due to the Federa- tion Movement of the Free Churches. There were eight different branches of the Christian Church connected with its Revision Committee. The draft canines of the Catechism were peepared by the distinguished head of their own The !o'cal College, the Rev. Principal Dykes. There were 52 questions with their answers—one for each week in the year. The catechism might io time help to solve the religious diffi- culty in public elementary scnools. Canon Gore, the other day, referring to that catechism, was reported to have said "it was in some respects a valuable docu- ment, and if the children of the Free Churches were taught what it contained, he believed that the gain to the religious life of the country would be incalculable." The appearance of the little publication was a memor- able event. Nothing like it had happened in this coun- try for the past two centlries and a half. It was just beginning to struggle for existence, and they ought to treat it with kindness and considerat on. It ought to awaken a touch of human interest with regard to it, not knowing whereunto it might grow. It might per- haps be submitted to rough handling. LECTURE AT EBENEZER CHURCH.</p>
        <p>At thii Church on Momliv evening Mr. Taylor, pqlice court mistionary, Madly gays a Ecient fi temrnacce</p>
