        <p>WATCH REPAIRS A SPECIALITY. STOPPING AND TROUBLESOME WATCHES EFFECTUA.LLY CURED. FIRST CLASS WORKMANSHIP. ‘York Guaranteed for 12 Months. Also Repairs in</p>
        <p>CLOCKS. JEWELLEhY:AND OPTICAL GOODS.</p>
        <p>_ . JOHN NICOT4, 30, I.iIi'f.VERNIARKET-STREET. WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>Tins approach of the session will The Meeting reduce to their proper proportiona of Parliament.</p>
        <p>many of the criticisms of the re- cess. It is usually a bad time for the Government when the House is not sitting; but on the present occasion it is the Opposition which has suffered, owing to unexpected resignations. The first busi- ness of the Opposition will be to elect a Leader in the Commons,a,nd circumstances have so narrowed the choice that only one name is really in the run- ning—Sir H. CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN. There are two younger men who may have a future before them—Mr. HERBERT GLADSTONE and Sir EDWARD GREY but their day has not come. The selection has been virtually made by com- mon consent; and happily, Lord KIMBERLEY will continue to load in the House of Lords, so that no change will be made in that quarter. The position in which Lora RosxßEßr will appear if he talks about foreign policy must be peculiar, and it will not be less so if he remains silent. He is exposed to considerable embarrass- ment, and probably he will refrain from making any manifesto or any bid for the recovery of his position as Liberal Leader. Mr. RHODES'S account of his views respecting Uganda and Egypt places him in a new light. Not only had he far-seeing views of his own some time ago, but he was able to. • impose Some of them upon Hr. GLADSTONE. But, , for Lord RosEBERy, according to Mr. RHODES, the British occupation of Egypt would have ceased iek 1893. The Ministerial ranks when Parliament meets will be bold and massive. They have not been thinned,and they have not been disheartened. The grave disappointments under which last ses- sion closed were removed by Lord KiTcv,NßA's splendid victory at Omdurman. Teat triumph hrs made people forget that Chinese affairs are nearly as unsatisfactory as over, and that Lord. S4LIS-. BURY is at times so feeble as to ,disgust even his , warmest supporters. The annexation, a the Soudan, under the joint protectorate of England and Egypt, has been a sore trial to France, but . the comedy of evacuation has long since came to an end. What we may live to sea will be a Cape- to-Cairo Railway, and commerce flowing to the Mediterranean. This also means that our navy will have to be strongex in the Medi- terranean than ever. Apart from the 30 Imperial responsibilities, there is the pro- gramme for the session, which must be far advanced by this time, and include bills of a con- troversial character. It is improbable that the f Government will take up all the matters which I I have been freely mentioned in the papers for some , , time past. If such should be the ease the session,. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <p>re- ent ent -ed, usi—</p>
        <p>7 in wed un-</p>
        <p>of hat ER? ust</p>
        <p>tss- :ing his unt aces</p>
        <p>sing 3 ta But tho 1 in, Drit not led. se3-</p>
        <p>will once more be unsatisfactory. • The tendency I to rise earlier without beginning earlier means taking time from private members, and this will once more be determined upon early in the ses- sion. The chances are that no private members' bills, will survive for long, and that opportunities for desultory debate will have to .be made by the members themselves. The session will open, however, with no elation on either side. The promised bills excite no enthusiasm, and Acts passed last session have worked so badly as to give great offence. Probably the chief feature of the 'session will.; be the sense that this Parliament is nearing its end.</p>
        <p>ASSUMING that the Powers Restricting Arma-agreed, when in conference, not to meats. make any increase in their res- pective armies and navies of the expenditure upon them for five years, how would matters stand ? All ships of war under construction would be pro- ceeded with, whether in public or in private yards. It must be assumed that each power would have to make a disclosure of its ships. Then would come the question of artillery for naval and land service. No new orders could be given, but exist- ing orders could be continued. The Powers which had armed themselves with quick-firing guns, such as France and Germany, would be as superior to this country at the end of the five years as they are now. Our slothfulness would have received its proper punishment. The, arrest of the numerical increase of armies would have no ter- rors for Russia, for Germany, or for France. Their yearly recruits would be called out . and drilled just- the same as usual. Their naval plans would be carried out to completion, and so would, the plans of this country. What would be the precise value of the five years arrest of augmenta- tion and of expenditure? That is the point no one has yet pretended to frankly consider. Time would be given for considering the other questions—a permanent reduction of armaments, facultative ar- bitration, and the restriction of warfare to present methods, excluding submarine boats, aerial to r- pedoes, baloons, and so forth. That is, if the Conference could not also deal with these matters at once, it would be possible to come to an undei- standing upon them. No pelitical questions are to be discussed at the Conference, so there should be no friction. But if it were possible to consti- tute a general court of European arbitration,would not the very first question to be submitted to that court be that of the restoration of Alsace-Lorraine as the sine qud non of a pacific settlement? The alliances, the armaments, the heavy expenditures of the past quarter of a century are all due to the Franco-German war. , If then, a period were fixed to the pause, and minor humane changes were made, and this arbitration conrt were formed and got to work,would the views of the enthusiast be fulfilled? The question is one that goes to the root of the whole business. It is fair to allow that the Conference can attain to certain conclusions, and will probably do so, but it is equally fair to ask what will be the practical result. If in the 'course of the five years we can get rid of the one Provoking cause of disquiet in Eutope, the re- vanche of France, we can do more than will be accomplished by the mere curtailment of naval and military preparations. But the CZAR is not at all desirous that this pelitical matter should be discussed. He is probably afraid that war might result.</p>
        <p>At a time when the ratepayers of the Borough are greatly concerned about the titure rates they anay be called upon to pay,due to he introduction of the Warrington Corporation Bill, it is interest- ing to study a comparative statement of Corpora- tion rates, excluding poor rates, which has been compiled showing the . rates of the -various County BFouglis :of England. Warring- ton is not yet a County Borough, although by its population and rateable value it is fully en- titled to be so. But on the Whole we think that</p>
        <p>Warrington comes out favourably with the • majority of the County Botoughs of England re- specting the rates demanded. Wbat for instance would the Warrington ratepayer say if he were called upon to pay a rate of .7s 4d in the £as is the case</p>
        <p>tti Dudle-y a-nd iniaddition to that a poor rate? Or again, he could scarcely be expected to "smile and look pleasant" if called upon to pay 6s. 10/d. for</p>
        <p>A Corporation rate as is the case at West Ham, or, „ to come a little nearer home, 6s. 10d. as at Preston</p>
        <p>where a dock rate amounts to 2s. 2id. There is, •of course, another side to the picture and no doubt' the ratepayer at such places as St. Helens where the Corporation rate is only 3s. 4d. or better still -at Chester where it touches the still lower figure ,of 3s. 2d., is a happy creature. It must, however, be remembered that a poor rate has to be added to this amount, and as the average rates in War- Tington since it became a borough, including the 'Guardians precept for poor rate, has been less than '6s. in the 2, there is some little room for con- gratulation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Stickney's paper in the February ilarper, entitled" With Dewey at Manila,"reveals a professional secret, and raises a question of vital impottance to this Empire. It has all along been Supposed that when Commodore Dewey sheered Oft halfway through the fight it was for the pur- pose of giving the crews breakfast. This pleasing little story was a diplomatic fiction, designed to hide from the crews the real state of affairs. The Americans had practically run out of ammunition. The magazines were beginning to look wofully bare, and, so far as the American officers knew, little damage had at that time been inflicted upon the Spanish forces. The point is that had the troops in the Spanish forts done their duty, and had the Spanish ships been properly armed and manned, thus allowing them to stand out for a longer period against the American fire, Dewey's magazines must have been depleted, and he would have had to have backed out of the bag as best he could, and Played hide-and-seek for two months, till ammuni- tion could have arrived from the States. Mr. tickney was on the Admiral's bridge all through the fight, and confesses that the officers knew that it was a near thing. The moral, of course, is that the storage capacity in modern warships is not nearly sufficient to meat modern requirements. It may be said that the ships of Great Britain, With coaling ports and fortresses all °ter the world, could never be far away from an ammuni- tion supply. But some official papers tell unpleas- ant stories of continuous lack of ammunition at British ports abroad. It is not only difficult to keep in tropical climates, bill; British Governments have displayed a certain amount of dangerous tardiness in getting it there.</p>
        <p>Bishop Moorhouse, of Manchester, has been brave enough in his diocesan charge at Preston to face a disagreeable fact as regards Church vitality --the decline in attendance at the church ser- vices. He attributes it ' to three causes; an increasing fondness for amusement, which dis- tracts; a greater attention to material gain, which occupies the mind; and a larger occupa- tion of time by edudational studies, which, in fact, have run into the' Sunday. It is noteworthy by the way, that this decline in church attend- ance has come in spite of the High Church move- ment, which by its insistence on early Communion saints' days celebration, and by the prominence Which it gives to liturgical observances might be expected to have stimulated church-going. But the real explanation appears to be found in what the "London Review" irreverently termed in an article last week the "Slump in Curates." Nearly a moiety of the assistant clergy to-day are 'men without any university training, and of the</p>
        <p>others only a few have distinguished themselves as men of promise. It is impossible that they should be leaders of the people unless they stand sufficiently high to be, in their own small sphere at least, leaders of thought. The weekly sermon do not fulfil this demand, and thus the churches lose their attendants. The appointment of Bishops too, has been sadly discouraging to men who have had the capacity to guide the Church. The Episcopal Bench does not represent the highest strength in talent or intellect of the Church,</p>
        <p>_ Captain A. Von Tiedemann, a German attache present at the battle of Omdurman, has written to the "Times" defending the Sirdar against any allegation of inhumanity preferred against him. The Sirdar himself is generally acquitted of any personal ill-treatment of the woundea Dervishes, but Captain Von Tiedemann's letter admits that, on the whole, they were killed. Whether this in- humanity is to be laid to the account of the coloured troops, or their civilised comrades in arms, still remains an open question. Notwith- standing the repeated denials of these charges there is sufficient ground for attaching some cre- dence to them, and in our opinion, °the matter ought to be settled once for all by a full inquiry</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CHESIIIRE COUN 1 COUNCIL SCIIOLARSHIP SCHEME.</head>
        <p>CHESIIIRE COUN 1 COUNCIL SCIIOLARSHIP SCHEME.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Technical Instruction Com- mittee of the Cheshire County Council held on Mon- day, the complete scheme of scholarships to be offered during the current year was adopted after careful revision. Considerable and important ad- ditioas have been .made to the scheme hitherto in use with a view to make the scholarship more com- plete and to suit the requirements of the county. The scheme now put forth is the most compre- hensive yet issued by the Committee, and it i 3 earnestly hoped that all friends of education will give their hearty co-operation to the Committee in bringing the same under the notice of parents and suitable personeeso that a large number of excellent candidates may be forthcoming, The scheme may be briefly summarised as follews : Class 1. consists of Scholarships to enable boys or girls who have been in attendance at Public Elementary Schools to proceed to Sscondary Schools. In this class 30 are offered, which are tenable for a period of 3 years iE the reports as to conduct and progress are satisfactory. Candidates must not be less than 111 or more than 14 years of age on May Ist. 1899. The examination is of at least equal difteulty to that o. Standard VI. of the Education Code now in force. The Scholarships practically cover the cost of attendance at the schools. Class IL—These scholarships are meant to be of assistance to those who being engaged in some occupation sduring the day have only the evenings or Saturdays in , which to pursue their studies Hence they are confined to persons of 15 years of age or over, and consist of 21 for science or techno- logical subjects, 24 for art, anu four for commercial subjects. They are of such value, as will pay the fees at the Institution attended, and any travelling expenses incurred. The maximum value, however, of a scholarship in this class is £5, and the mini- mum amount allowed XI. The scholarships will be awarded on the results obtained by the candidates in the examination of the science and art depart- ment for the subieets they are now studying, or for commercial subjects. the results of the examina- tions of the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire in- 'stitutes or the Society of Arts. Class 11. (al—This is practically a new set of scholarships, and specially designed to meet the requirements ef teachers of various grades. ('l) 12 scholarships are available to head teachers or assist- ant teachers in Public elementary Schools, to enable them to attend classes in some technological subject, such as manual training in woodwork for men, and cookery for women. These subjects are selected on account of the special difficulties that are to be met in obtaining the necessary instruction for a teacher's certificate or diploma therein. For the scholarships to male teachsre the maximum value is £5 but for the Cookery Scholarships £lO is allowed. In selecting the holders of these scholar- ships, special attention will be paid to the claim of teachers in schools in rural districts. (2) 32 scholarships are offered to pupil teachers in Public Elementary Schools to enable them to sittend classes in the evenings or Saturdays in Science, Art, and Commercial subjects,at properly recognised centres. The maximum value of these is £5, and in selecting the &amp;Aden, special attention will be paid to appli- cations from rural districts. ,(3) Two scholarships are offered to enable pupil teachers to proceed for two years to a training college. They will be , awarded to candidates who shall have passed the Queen's Scholarship examination to be held in December 1899, and to be available tor two years commencing in 1900 at any training college ap- proved by the Technical Instruction Committee. The value of the scholarships is £l5 for each of the two years. Class lll.—This consists of scholarships to enable students to proceed to a University College,or higher seat of learning for the period of three years. They also enable those wlio have distinguished them- selves during their tenure of a scholarship in Class 1 to climb another step in the educational ladder, The minimum age of candidates is thus put at 16, and the maximum at 22 years. Seven scholarships are offered to be allocated on the results of an examination as follows, if possible. One to a candidate desirous of pursuing some branch of the chemical industry ; one to a person anxious to follow the profession of a mechanical engineer; one to a student desirous of taking up sanitary engineeeing, or plumbing, or the work of an architect or surveyor; one to a candidate desir- ous of being trained for a veterinary surgeon; one to a person desirous of being trained for a teacher; one to a person wishing to become an electrical en- gineer. or to enter some profession approved by the committee, but not included in the preceding; one to a candidate desirous of being trained for some branch of the Silk Industry. For the scholarship for the silk industry, and for the one for a veterin- ary surgeon,special examinations are provided. The maximum value of these scholarships is £5O per annum,except in the case of the veterinary scholar- ship which extends over four years, and for which £4O per year is allowed.ln this class also are,a scho- larship for Art with a special reference to designing and tenable at South Kensington and of the maxi- mum value of £5O; also a scholarship for music, tenable for three years at the Royal College of Music, Ducie-streete Manchester, the maximum value of which is £45 per annum. Special exami- nations are provided for the scholarships in Art and Music. Class IV.—Provides for dairy and domestic scholarships, tenable for ten weeks at the Dairy Institute, Worleston, and covering the whole cost for fees and board incurred there These scholar- ships are given in five sets of ten in each set. Class V. is concerned with scholarships for agriculture and horticulture' which are tenable at the Agricultural School. at Holmes Chapel. These scholarships, 14 in number, are tenable for three years, and cover the whole cost of fees acd board charged at the school. The minimum age of candidates is placed at 14, and the maximum of 18 years. To meet the case of any young men who are employed with their parents in farm work, a set of scholarships, 12 in number, are offered for short courses of instruction in special subjects for nine or ten weeks. These are also tenable at the Agricultural School, and are of the value of £6 each. Class VI. deals with scholarship; specially for women. ,Two scholarships are offered tenable at the Scnool of Domestic Economy, Cheater, for ten months to acquire instruction in Cookery, Laundry Work, Dresssmaking, ,and the Diploma for teaching these subjects. The scholarships cover the cost of fees and board at the school, viz., 35 guineas each. It will thus be seen that the total number of scholarships embraced in the scheme is 215. A IL um of £3,250 has been granted and set aside to pay for the scholarships for the year ending March 31et, 1900. The various sets of scholarships will be adver- tised from time to time in the county papers. and full particulars in regard to any set may be obtained by applying to the Organising Secretary, whose office is in Crewe.</p>
