        <head>INVESTIGATION AT CHESTER.</head>
        <p>INVESTIGATION AT CHESTER.</p>
        <p>A. Special Consistery Court was held in the Refectory adjoining the Chester Cathedral on Thursday, to investi- gate certain charges brought against toe Rector of Nant- w.ch (Rev. C. E. Jackain, M.A.), and in which Mrs. Woods, wife of Councillor A. Woods, jeweller, Warring- ton, is implicated. The circumstances leading; up to the inquiry may be briefly summarized. Early in November the two curates, Rev. M. S. Baylis and the Rev. H. Thomas Dixon, deemed it Expedient, after coo- sultation with the Ray. Canon Webb, of Crewe, to tor- ward their resignations to the Bishop of Chester. They subsequently had an interview with his Lordship, and the result was that Mr. E. S. Giles, one of the diocesan procters, was depated to visit Nantwich and obtain evidence with regard to the truth or otherwise of the allegations which had been circulated. About a score of persons were interviewed, and their Watements having been presented to the Bishop, his Lordship issued what is known in eeclebiastical parlance as a " complaint," I and the matter was in consequence referred to the , Consistory Court—a special day being fixed for the pur- pose. Chancellor Espin presided, and was assisted by the following assessors :—Rev R J Fairclough, Rev Cs non Moore, Rev Canon H I Blackburne, Meesrs C E Thosnyeroft (Thornycroft Hall), and James Tomkinson (Willingten Hall). Mr Moreton &amp;nth and Mr Honoratus Lloyd (instructed by Messrs C 0 Humphrey and San, solicitors, London and Mr T. 13 Whiteley, selieitor,Nant- wich) appeared for the Rector of Nantwich, and Mr Collingwooci Hope (instructed by Mr. Giles, proctor) for the Bishop ; and Mr Gibbons (instructed by Mr Willett, of Warringzon) for Mrs. Woo is. There was a consider- able number of the outside pubiic present, hailing prin- cipally from Nantwich and Warrington. Amongst them w .re noticed Councillo- Woods,Mis Wocds,Canon Webb, Mrs Jackson, Messrs Henry Harding e (chairman of tlyi Nantwich Urban Council), J Leah, "J M Johnson, E D Elliott, L Vaughan, W Bayliss, M B )yer, J Bebeingro.a, , A Furnivsll and Joseph Jackson (churchwardens), G Forsdick, G Garnett, Mesasy,S H Platchard, F Edwards, S Rose-Wood, G Evans-Jackson and Mrs Evans-Jacksou, and Mr A Parkes. Mr. Moreton Smith said he appeared for the Reet and he might as Well say that his friend Mr. Lloyd, who was bifore the Court the other dal and who was with him in the case, was detained on a part heard ease an i would not be there that day: With reference to the ap- plication that was made the other day for further parti- culars, he understood the difficalty ia which his learned friends on the other side were with regard to the dates. One date had been given approximately since, but now they had takers aefull statement perhaps his friend would be able to give him something a little more definite. The Chancellor said he asked Mr. Collingvvood Hope to be so kind as to furnish the Rector's counsel with any further particulars that he might have. The statement ! that was made on beealf of the prosecution was that they could no': furnish any further particulars than those already given,for which reason he (the Chancellor) saw neaadvantage in postponing the trial. Mr. Moretsri Smith said on the part of the Rector he desired that the affair should be disposed of as speedily as possible,and they had done their best to bring every- body as far as they could tee who might be material, hut one of the charges they had to 'meet was seine day betweea Fetroary, 1898, and November, 1898. or course there was a tremendous burden thrown on the Reciter to aleount for every day between that period. The Chancellor: On the other side the charges are given in detail. Mr. Moreton Smith said he admitted that it was a most r . Hope serious s achargesaid in answerm a made against gtao rt hism or client.eton Smith he Mr. must say at once that he was in possession of the same facts when that application was made two or thre- days ago as he was in possession of that ailay, and he could not give any further facilities. Mr. Gibbons said the charges were practically being made against Mrs. Woods, as well as against the Rector, and he claimed the right of representing her. in that court. The Chancellor said he could not allow him to address the court on behalf of Mrs Woods, or to ex- amine or cross-examine witnesses, but there was no ob- jection whatever tO his being present. Mr. Moreton Smith contended that as in the proceed- ings in the Divorce Court co-respondents were entitled to be represented. Mrs. Woods ought to be represented in that court. The Chancellor said Mr. Gibbons might ask whatever questions he wished to put through Mr. Moreton Smith.</p>
        <p>Mr. Collitsgwood Hope, in- opening the case, said he appeared on behalf of tee Bishop of Chester to p-osf c its Charles Edwin Jackson, reetor of Nantwich, for having on or about February, 1896. and Ju varieus dates be- tween that ard Ontober. 1898, been guilty of immoral conduct with Mrs. Woods, wife of Alfred Woods, jcweller, of 14, S.ankey-stre .1, Warrington, at Poole- cottage and the rectory, Nantwich. The defendant was a curate at the Parish Church of ,Warrington, and wl ile there he got to know Mrs. Woods, and apparentiy formed an attachment for her. He left there in 1894, and was appointed rector of Nante ich, and from that time up to the present time he had remained at Neut. wich. In February of 1896, Poole-cottage, Nantwich, was taken by Mr. Woods. and it was chiefly at that place and at the rectory,Na.ntwich.that the charges were laid. Mrs. Wroes used to stay at the cottage from Saturday-4o Mondays. Re was there less frequently than Mrs. Wolds,and the court wiuld be satisfl, d that the Cir- eurnst Incas were su-h as showed that there were facilities for immoral relations taking place as was alleged. It was impossible when al attachment such as that sub- sisted between two persons to limit it to any particular date or occasion. From the relationship subsisting be- tween the parties it was impossible to specify actual dates. At Poole cottage some of the general acts were noticed by people living in the immediate vicinity. The ream. of Naetwich was seen going to Poole cot ' tage to which he had a latchkey, as late as midnight, and he was seen leaving the cottage as early as seven o'clock the following moraine. Most of the times the charges related to there was no other person in the house except Mrs. Woods when the defendant called. On one occasion when Some Persons called at the cottage no one respon- ded to the knocking, but after they had gene away Mrs Woods and the rector were rein leaning out of the same bedroom window. They were seen en many occasions walking together arm in arm or the defendant with his arm round Mrs Woods' waist. Defendant had been seen kissing her, and on many occas'ons they were seen throw- ing kisses to each other on parting. Mrs. Woods stayed at the rectory when she was the only lady in the house. Other ladies were there on some occa- sions, and on one or two occasions Mr. Woods was also there. He would leave on the Monday morning, pre- sumably to go back to tusinege and Mrs. Woods would sometimes stay a day over the time and on other ocea- sioes a week over the time. No other persons were in the house except the servants. When Mrs. Woods was at the rectory she and the rector used to occupy the study, and a label was won the door when they were in on it,so that no one tshheousitduddyiswthirtbh tthheemworodn.asEnnyzagede minus whatever. They used to go togeteer to bed after the servants had gone, sometimes as late as one or two o'clock in the morning, , and on one occasion the rector was heard talking in her bedroom after the servants had gone to bed. The ser- vants' bedroom was immediately over that occupied by Mrs. Wends, and when she was there sounds were perfectly audible. On another occasion • the rector was heard in the bedroom late at night, and one of the servants would say that she never heard him leave I thl room again. Another servant would speak to an occasion when Mrs. Woods who had gone to bed before the rector, opened her bedroom door, and in her night- dress asked the rector if he. vras going to bed, and the rector said he was. They came upstairs to eether and went together into the rector's bedroom. Evidence would also be given that on one occasion evh.n one of the servants was brushing the rector's clothes she found in his pocket an article which the Prosecution alleged went to eepport the charee. The housekeeper on another occasion found a letter on the defendant's desk presumable left there through inadvert- ence. It Was in the handwriting of Mrs. Woeci=, and it began, " My dear Charlie," and it referred in terms of endearment to him saying, "I am afraid you do not want me, but I hope you do, dear," and some such terms as that. Evidence was then heard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>LW:ITER:I SERVICE.</p>
        <p>An interesting goipel lantern service wa3 trli on Sun- day in the Albert-ro td Wesleyan Sunday Scho2l,Widnes, after the ordinary preaching service ia the chapel. A. goodcompany assembled to hear Mr. C. S tge'e instruc- tiva leaura upan the life of St. Paul. Miss Halme ren- dered in excellent stile the sOlO3 " The Holy City " and " The Better Land."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUNCORN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY, LI3IITZD%</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY, LI3IITZD%</p>
        <p>In c3nnection with the above society a lantern lfeture wai given on Mmd ty evening in the Oakland-stree, Wesleyan Schoolroom,by Mr. Dudley. There was a large attendance. Mr. Dudley's subject was entitled "An haur with Cio,-)perative prod ictions " Toe slides,which were very good an I interesting, il:ustrated the various ma .ufactures and processes of the Co-operative SJcieties. The lecture was also enlivened by several humorous musical items. The lanternist, Mr. Plinsi-,on, performed his duties in a skilful manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>ACCIDENTS AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENTS AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>_ On Friday James Kelly,.a young man residing at 24, Catherine-street, was admitte I to the Accident Hospital suffering from severe burns to his foot. Kelly, who is employed at the Widnes Foundry, was following his em- ployment, when some hot sand got into his clog,causing the injuries referretto.—Thom is Lunt, 7, Thomm-3t., three years of age, was admitted on Saturday to the Ho pital, having been seriously scalded at.,out his face, arms, and right foot. A pan of boiling ...at-A had been put upon the jockey btr of the grate, which overturned upon the child.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>FIRE ALA.RII.</head>
        <p>FIRE ALA.RII.</p>
        <p>_ About 2 o'clock on Tuesday afternoon, an alarm of fire caused the turn out of the Runcorn Fire Brigade. It appears that a fire had broken out in the stackyard of Mr. Baxtir, Frodsharn, and although the fire was gol; under in a short time, the Runcorn Brigade, was sum- moned as an extra prectution, and thservl great credit for the alahrity they displayed. The extent of the dam- age is reported at about £l5, but bhd the tire gained ground several valuable sacks must have been sahri- ficed.. It is stated that the tire originated in the vicinity of the" Irishmen's shed," and had travelled along a train of loose straw which was lying about the place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.</head>
        <p>BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>The usnal monthly ineezing was held last Monday evening,. Mr. Edwin Marshall presided over a good attendance. Temperance ad iresses were delivered by the Chairman and Mr. W. A. Unsworth, both of whom emphasised the great imvrta.tic of abstaining altogether from alcoholic drinks. The addresses proved interost- ing and instructive, and were listened to with marked attention. During the oy ning a gh e was rendered by the choir; a trio by the Miss Taylor and Simkirt and Mr. R. Morris ; a duet by Miss Williamson and Mr. George Johnson ; songs by the Misies Carter, Hallding, Simkin. and Mr. R. Morris ; and recitations by Anni,i Gerrard, Willie Rawlinson, J3nn. l'arry and James Lea. Before the meeting ended Mr. John D. Bisbrown exnresed the hope that many would that night avail themselves of the opportunit'y afforded them of signing the pledge. Rghteen pledges wore taken. Mr. J. H. Pickthall acted as accompanist. BETHESDA P.S.A. SOCIAL GATHERING.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday night, a s.,cid ga%hering war pro- moted in connection wi.h the 13,,:rres1a P,S.A., and pr3ved highly successful. Tire proceedings commenced with an excellently spread supper, catered by Mr. J. Lea, Temperance Hotel, High-street, to which atrout 30 members sat down, The Rev. Joseph Osborn-, 8.A., (president) presided, arra the vice-chair was ocarpied by Mr. W. Handley. Mr. F. Beck was unab7e, trrough ill- ness, to be present. After supper r&lt;f,rcnce was inane be the president to the usefulness of the society, an the extent of its work and influence' RS well as t, the value, of sucn gatherings as the one that night. They were productive of good fellowship amongst the members. Recitations and sogs were contributed and a pleasan.: evening was spent. B RIINNEEt MOND AND CO . , AIM TREI R WO RRIIDIN</p>
        <p>On Toes, lay night at Norilivrich, Sir John Brunner replied to slveral queAlons respecting the decision of Brunner Mod Co., the great chemical manufac- t.irers, not aroneage any pew men above thirry years of age, which announcement has camel corisidsrible feeing in the. district. Sir John said the firm's trade had ,n iecialcei by the enormous taxation upon alkali in America, and as they hut/ decided to give a limiteti weekly amount to ail who had served them faithfully for a long, P,riod, he bitterly regretted they ceuld not aff3rd to take on men past (toil best days in life. • The Urban District Council on Tueday night decided to en- deavour to promote new industries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SHOCKING DISCOVERY AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING DISCOVERY AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Monday, Rtitont Tipping, 198, Warrington-rmad, Widnes, aged 52 years, was. found dead in a pool of water, in a meadow off Greenoaks-rod, Gorsey lane. Deceased had ben in il'-health for ssvetral years, and tor the last fortnight had b-en confined ta bed. Ho had toqate become very low spirited, and was medically attended to by Dr. Hutchinson. About 830 a.m. on the day in question deceased got up, and at 10.0 a.m. he went cut, telling his wife that ,h 3 was going for a walk. About a quarter of arm hour after, a young man named Wilson SAW deceased on the footpath in Greenook-road, from whence he climbed over the fence into the field, and walked towards the water. Wi's-iniupm turning ronnd again a mornent or two after, mirsei deceased, and surmisin toat something was wrong went to tbe pit. Ttaere be found deceased lying face downwards in the water. The young man immediately summoned the assistance of Mr Valentine, and Tipring was taken Put of the water, hut life was extinct. On Wedneiday evening at the Castle Hotel, Wiines, Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest touching the death ot Robert Tipping, aaed fifty-two, wires° body was found in a pit on Mon- day morn:ng. It appears that the ceeeased had been in failing tvalth for about eix years, and dur;ng the latt fortnight was bedfaot. He got up on Monday mnrnine and informed his wire that be would go fora walk, re- marking that he wished he was dsad. He was seen hy a lad to go across a field, in the direction of is pit. The lad proceeded to the pit, and saw • the deceased in the water. When the body was recovered life was extinct. —Elizabeth Tipping, wife of the deceased, ssid her hip- band had often expressed the wish that he was dead.— A verdict of " Suicide w bast of unsound mind " was re- turned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</p>
        <p>A concert was held at the Seamen's In,titute, S' ation- road on Tues lay night,when tie 0 Orland-street Concert party submitted a tasteful selection of music to a large and appreciative audience. The chair was taken by Mr. J. H. Sfarby, who said it gave him great pleasure to ' come and assist in such good work. He had many pl,asant recollections of the last Vine the party had come ov-r and felt that they W0.,1d do their test to give a pleasant evening's entertainment. The following programme was then rendered :—Part I: Song. " Scarlet and Blue," Mr Harper ; song, " Fiddle and I." Miss L Hague ; song. " Queen of Angels," Mr W Hague ; recitation, Mies F Moore ; song, " Dream of Paradise," Miss C Lloyd ; song, "The Amateur White- washer," Mr A Garner ; glee, "There was a Mouse." Second part: Song, "An Old Path," Miss Lloyd: song, "The Village Blacksmith." Mr S Pope ; recitation, " Shining Light," Miss Moores ; song, "I have no Home," Miss L Hague ; humorous trio, " Three jolly Sailor Boys," Misses J Harper, W Stocker, and J Pope; humorous song, " Let 'em -all come," Mr A Garner ; dialogue, " Waisted a Clerk." • Several of the itlms were so well rendered that encores were demanded. During the interval refreshments were provided, and at the close a vote of thanks was passed to the chairman and the artistes, on the propeaition of Mr. W. Shaw, secon- ded by Mr. Inman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN MkNPE ADVENTURES,</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN MkNPE ADVENTURES,</p>
        <p>Oa Saturday -nit-rt George Dunning, residing at 28a, Bridgewater-street, Runcorn, had FOITI extraerdinary adventures. It transpired that Dunning went home about 6 30 the worse for drink.and some words ensued between him and his wife, apparently about his con- diton. The upshot was that the man left the house, taking with him his bey Joseph, aee 1 five years, and nothing more was heard of them until Sunday after- noon. The Wigan police telephoned to Rancern the in- formation that Dunning had been tat-en to Wigan In- firmary, after having been picked up on the railway at Newton Bridge,near Manchester. Be was suffering from severe wounds cu the head,ancl it was suppo:ed that he had junaeed or fallen from a train. His son "Joey," was fern d in a railway carriage at Manchester early on Sunday morning. The by was crying, and he was taken to Creswell Polies Station,a.nd detained unti! Men- day morning, when his mother brought him back to Runcorn. The msn's wife believes it was his intention to go to Bolton, where he has relatives, and th it he got into the wrong train.—On Monduy white in the Wigan Infirmary he regained consciousness, and he then stated that his name was Joseph Danning,of 4,dridgewater.st., Runcorn. He was a doakman. Be wai very pleas( dto her that .‘ Jeer" WiS safe. 0-the circ mstances under which he came to leave the carriage he had not the slightest rec )11eztion: His appearance and manner do not in any way suggest an at7empt at suicide. The occur- rence was most likely accidental. He may perhaps have tried to leave the carriage under tho idea.that he was in or near a station, or that he had missed one.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD WESLRYA.N IMPROVBMENT FOCIETY.</p>
        <p>On Friday evening last this society met in the .vestry of the Wesleyan Church. The Preiiilent was in the chair and there was a capital meeting both for attendance and discussion: Mr. Joseph. Corbett gave a naper on the ," Great Pyramid," in which he spoke about the origin, age., and use of the monu- ment. He quoted authorities, and referred to many theories and fancies, and affirmed through it the truth of God's divine revelation. Mr. Pion, and Mr. James Bodle differed somewhat from the pre- vious speaker. Mr. shakeshat, Mr. Gleave, Mr. Osborne, and others took pert in the debate, whicb was animated and instructive.</p>
        <p>" LINSERD COMPOUND" for Coughs and Colds Asthma, and Broneh tie, Of Chemists only.—Advt. " UNSHED COMPOUND for Cou he and Culda, alleys irritation. Uives I mediate relief Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>INiE RESTING CASE.-,425 DAMAGES.</p>
        <p>AN UNPROTECTED HOLE.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn County Court,on Tuesday, before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, and a jury, a</p>
        <p>case in which Frank Antrobus, of Nelson-street,</p>
        <p>brought an action against William Edward Lea, ironmonger, High-street, and Robert Wilton Harrop builder, Wellington-street, for £5O damages for in- juries sustainel by falling into a hole which defen- dants had left uncovered ani unlighted, cn the 4th of October, was heard. Mr. Pugh Instructed by Mr. C. Linaker (Messrs. Linaker and Linaker) appeared for tl-le plaintiff,and Mr. Swift instructed by Mr. Joseph Burton fcir the</p>
        <p>defendants.—There was a large nusmber of witnesses on both sides and the court sat from 10 until 6in</p>
        <p>the afternoon, the whole of the time being devoted</p>
        <p>to the case.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pugh opened the proceedings by stating the case for the plaintiff. Four or fiye years a4o Antrobus was a hod earner, but s nce that time he had been in the . _</p>
        <p>• habit of taking up small cantract3. Oa the 4th of Oct. he was employed by Mr. T. Lea, Greenway-road, to - sist at cleaning 80E119 flues. He commeP.ce.d the worli about one o'clock in the day, and finished at about 7 or _ _</p>
        <p>a little aftEr the same eve',ing. Mr. Lea paid him and he started off home to Nelson-strea.t. On the way home</p>
        <p>he called at the Nelson Hotel and got a glass of ter and from that place be was in company with his wife. They were proc9edinz side by 911 e wnen suddenly plaintiff _ .</p>
        <p>caught his left leg in a 'hole and was violently thrown with the whole weight of hi 3 .body on 013 right leg and</p>
        <p>Lis right knee WAS twisted. Defendant's sm— came on</p>
        <p>the scene, struck a light, and se.ling the condition of things, covered the hole up seas to prevent further ac- _ . __</p>
        <p>, cident, The plaintiff had. subi(quently been attendei by Dr McLennan and had been triable to do any kind</p>
        <p>of work siac,, thf, amideat.</p>
        <p>That was the only Lim! h</p>
        <p>could appear bsfore them and if they carnet° deal with the question oti damages he hoped the jury would con- sider that impprtant point and find accordingly, as the amount claimed was realty a small one. Frank Antrobue, said he was a genera' j ib'aer, and on the 4th of Octoher he was engagel to do a job in Green- __ _</p>
        <p>way-road for Mr. Thowas Lei. -He cornmeocel the work at ahtait one_o'cloek,and finiabPd about ten minur.e3 piet</p>
        <p>s'ven. He then went on his way home, calling at the Nelson Hotel for a glass of beer. On coming out he met his wife by the door of the Nelson. He and his wife then went home tog,,ether. As he proceeded up Nelson- Et,et,.he slipped into a hole about a foot deep on the parapet. His left foot slipped into the hole in the foot- path, and his right leg was twisted under him. When he pulled his left leg-out of the hole he found there was nothing to cover the hole—no fencing or anythinf, else. His leg was caught in "some corrugated iron and the hole." With his wife's assiitance he got his log out. Harriet Jones and young Edgar Lea came</p>
        <p>up shortly afterwards. Harriett Jonrs came up first. Witneas's wife slid to Mr. Lea " Was that afi place to leave for anyone to fall in ?" Young Lea came to wit- nem; and ask:d if ho was hurt. He replied " I have</p>
        <p>hit my knee." Lel said he was sorry ald wauld see to i, and the. 4 went and put a plank acrois the hoe. Witness saw the place next morning as he was going to</p>
        <p>the cnctor's ; heholc had been filled no and a i'ixo foot</p>
        <p>flag was put norors it. He had been suffering from injur 7 to his knee ever since. The doct,r he consulte i WAS Dr. Mell:lbnan. His average earnings before tue</p>
        <p>accident were 303. a week. He ha.ci been unable to work</p>
        <p>since, \and had not earqwd anything since the accident. By Mr. Swift: He was not a loifr. but was; a hard working man.. He went on Ilia</p>
        <p>way home after finishing his jb, and naiad ;:t the Nelson. Be did not call at ills NeW</p>
        <p>He did not hehr Mr. Houghton, the mansfzer, say</p>
        <p>"That if I had known you werpt so ba;l 1 would not lia7-0</p>
        <p>served you"? He. had not toid Mrs. Wainwright at any titw, "If he ever fell down thtr, .he would make Mr,</p>
        <p>1.e.,. sit no for at." He was perfectly sober the night of</p>
        <p>the accident. Mary Jane Antrobus, wife of the complainant. siid she met her husband at the bottom of Nelson-3treet be- tween 7 and 8 o'clock. She accompinied him home and saw him fall into a hole in the_ parapet. Sho corrobor- ated complain Int's statement.—ln eross-rx mination, she said she was perfectly eiear as t] meeting her bus- band outside the Nelson. She bad no: »eon him inside tke Ntlson.—P.essed by Counsel this witness a mifted she saw her husband in the N,.lS)n, and th,y came out togethir. Mr.. Lea tried to covJr the hole with a small</p>
        <p>tile but he could not do so. Elize Joins, lson-street, said rho was cerning up the rtreet on the night f the accident and saw Antrobus lying on the floor. The h-le was net in the footpath but in the openiege against the corrugated iron. Sne saw the hole being m ade dating, the day time, and after tea the iron was put there but it did not quite cover the hole. Samusl Davies. architect and surveyor, Runcorn and Frodsham, said he made e. plan of the plaree On the 9th November he measured the hole reed made the plan last week. There was a hole extending seven inches into the parapet or footpath. It was a pare: path (eranite setts). —By Mr. Swift : On the 9:h of November he found the hole was covered up and filled in. Be had not seen the place on the 4th of October. There wee concrete iv the hole and he a,sumed that the hole ',rots that eize, but he could not swear as to the state of the hole on the 4th of October.—lly Mr. Pugh: He weull say that the pillar could not have bsea place i in that po Non without dis- turbing the footway. James Wildinc.architect and surveyor to the Rancorn Rural DiVrict Council, said be had examined tee place that mien:rig: The iron pillar spoken of was anoarently embeedel in concrete, which extended abornt 81- inches ! on the one side and 6 inches on the other side of the parapet. It was an irregular hole and not a Naar() one. '1 --By Mr, Swift : He knew nothing about the hole ! previous to that morning and could not testify to the state of it on the 4th of October. Dr. endrew McLennan,surgeon.practising at Beacom said the complainant celled at the surgery en tte morn- ing of the sth of October. On examination he found him to be suffering from acute inflammation of the knee joint. There was a large swelling and - great pain. He had been under his earn ever since. Antrobus was not able to do any work at present, and in his oeinion would not be able to do anything for a month. It was as yet uncertain to say whether the knee would ever to as serong azain. at any rate' he would suffer from weakness in the knee for some consi- derable time. Ile submitted his aCC,..,11, t for seven guineas.—By Mr. Swift : He had attended the plaintiff about 20 times, on each occasion the plaintiff came to him at the surgery. It was very indefin'te what he charged workmen who came to him at the surgery, hut perhaps se.—Mr. swift: And what do you give them?— Witness: The best advice I can.—And so for advice you charge seven guineas ?—I have not been paiii.—l suppose you expect to be paid anyway ? No answer.—Were you ever consulted by him befere?—Yes.—Mr. Swift having characterised a charge of 7s. 61. a visit as an exhoehitant amenut to pay for words ! words! words I.—questioned witness as to the probability of the acute inflammation developing by the morning. Was it prosible it could be developed so early the next morning P—Witness : Undoubtedly.— You were present, I think about a weak ago when Dr. McDougall saw the plaintiff ?—Yes.—Do you agree with Dr. McDougall that the plaintiff is quite able to do ordinary work on level ground 7—Not at present.—All his visits were paid to you at your surgery.—Yes.—l suppose that being so he was able to walk every dee of his life from that day to this P—With difficulty.— What's your general charge for making a visit t the house of a labouring man P—Perhaps half—crown.— And bscause he saves you that trouble by coming to you you chargli him three times as much ? (Laughter) Witness : I think not ; that is for cousultal ion and for the treuble with the CaS9.—Mr. Swift: It is three times cheapettfor von to visit him than for him to come to your house P—Witness : It is all nonsense.—Mr. Swift : Just FO. (Laughter). James Lea, Temperance Hotel keeper, High-stre-it,and Thomas Lea, Greenway-road, gave evidence as to the sobriety of the plaintiff. William Frodsharn, butcher, Nelson-street, stated that he saw the plaintiff on the night of the aecident at about 8-30. He looked right enough. Samuel Houghton, lanliord if the Nelson Hotel, said he knew Antrobus very well. He conld not recelle t to plaintiff being in his house on the 4th Oetober. Net to his recollection had he the night previeusly taken b,er from him and placed it in his who's jug In robus was not drunk tee night before. If he had hem, witness would not have served him.—Crogoexamined by Mr. Swift: He had told :Ur. Burton's clerk that he did not remember ane thing about the 4th of October. He came simply to defend his house.—Mr. Swift Yes ; that is so.—That en'2e i the case for plsintdr. Mr. Swift, addressing the jury, said there was no drubt that on the 4th of Ootetier, Mr. Lea, or those for whom he was responsible did melee a h 1i at the corner of Ws Wainwrigt t's cottage. Theve was no time to.fill the hole that night, but he weu'd eubmit evidence to prove that the hole was quite cleat of the parapet e for , in order to prevent accident thee blot:Ate fence of cor- rugated iron over 10 yards in length and 7 feet high, and placed it an as to cover the whole of the opening leading to the Fair Ground and so shut off the hole in cpestion. It reached quite to the grened. and at one end wee held in position by some suppoiti and at the eth.er was nailed to the gable end of Mre.Wainwright's catage. When the man Antrpbui met with the accident he raid a cry. Mr. Edgar Lea went out to see and found the plaintiff riantrobes tend his wife there. He said to Antrobue,"what is the naat`er ?" but Antrobus himself was too drunk to make any answer, and altheueh he lead told them a lot of rhapsedy, it was his wife that said *I Frank has tumbled." He did not like saving it hut those were the facts. He submitted that it was an attempt on the Daft ol the man Antrobus to make Mr. Lea pay for an injury that Mr. Leo never occasioned. either by neglieence or anything else. Mr. Lea from the first down to the last had done everything in the power of man ; and if with all his care and all his atten- tion the accident eccurred that was a misfortune.</p>
        <p>It was an action for negligenCe ? There had been no neglieence. Re suggested then, in lace of the fact that there was a fsotpath sft Bin, that the man could not have been sober or was overc-me 'by drink when he got into the hole. Referring to the doctor'e evi- dence he questioned whether acute inflammation could have developed in so short a perioi. It was, be thought a more gradual process. Finally he submitted first that Mr. Lea took every precaution that was pos- sible on his part; secondly that even supposing there was negligence art the patt of Mr. Lea there WAS also contributory negligence on the part of the plaintiff due to him walking the streets in a state of intoxication. Edgar S. Lea, son of the defendant, said he asssisted his father in his business of engineer and iromesonger. The fence they put up was a substantial one made up of two parallel spars, with corrugated iron nailed across; and was supported by buttre3ses of spars. One ena was fixtei to Mrs. Wainwright's house and the other supported by an old gate post. The whole was clearly fenced off from the public. Witness was standing in his father's garden the same night and heard Antr piens come up the street bawling and shouting. He could' swear to that for he knew his voice perfectly. Subseque.ntly he found Antrobus leaning against the window bill of Mrs. Wainwright's. As he went tewards him Antrobus' wife met him and said "Frank has tumbled." Mrs. Antrobus pointed oul a hole to him, but it was not the hole tney had ma le. After the conversation he want and looked at the place pointed out by Mrs. Antrobus, and found a mall channel lead- ing down to the hole they had made. The channel would be'about three inches deep at the deepest part ; but he thought that no person could have got into the hole unless they deliberately tried to do so. There was a lamp in the street about seven yards away. He submitted a plan of the eve; which he had mule himself.—By Mr. Pugh: The reason for commenc- ing the hole that night was to ease the wcet of the next day. Witness was sure Antrobus was drunk locause he had a drunken lurid look." He might have said he was'serry, it was only natural that he should do se.</p>
        <p>--William Rig:lr Lea, e mob irated his son as to the making of the hoe and the prooortions of the fence pro- tecting it. the fence protEcing it was such that a oat could not have got through it. Thomas Wilton Harrop, builder, said thoue'h one of the defendant's he was simply an employee of Mr. Lea's. He gave corroborative evidence as ta the making of the hole, and leaving it safely. Jahr Williams, joiner, w:th 35 years' expetience gave evidence of a similar nattne,and testified t 3 the stability of the fence erected.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift at this point stated he had no wish to pro- long the case, buthad a further number of witnesses to testify to the protection of the hole, and the strength of the fencing if required. His Honour considered that the evidence a'ready sub- . mitted was sufficient on that point, and the plaintiff &amp;I not dispute its existence. Samuel Gregory said he was a police constab'e (Ni. 41) stationed at Runcorn. He had known the plaintiff for the last five years personally, and for two years pre- vious to that. As far as he knew he load not followed any re2ular employment dur:nz .the last five years.if he had he -would have known ir. A,trobus was generally , to be found at the bottom col Nelson-street, where he . spent most of his time.—By Mr. Swift': With .regard to sohrifty what were his habits P—Mr. Pugh objected.— His Honour to vrtness : Do you know a.nvitiag about p`a,intiff's condition as to sobriety on the 4th of aluoter —Witness : No, &amp;r.—Witness further stated that from his knowledg , of plaintiff's general c',arscter he did not think plaintiff war a man to be believed on nath.—Hi4 Honour: You would not believe him too oath P—Witries: I would not, sir.—His Honour: Why do you say sP— Witnes explainei his reasr,n to ba because the plsiniff had put him on. wrong scents by giving him information whirda subsequently. proved to be untrae.—By Mr Pugh : Witness wis not slyly isiod to hoar that plaintiff could get gore' cOaracers from Inspector Hicks or Saper- Intendent Okll, but fib had known the pliintiff for a longer period than they. At the conclusion of this wit- ness's evidence His Honour admonished him,for if those were Ills only reasons for thus talking about a man he advised him to be more careful.</p>
        <p>John Owen Wainwright, of Ne's)n-3trevt ; Waterworn, Nlso-tne mlia Wainwript, wife of previous witness, :-,nd JeFsie Wainwright, all gave evi- 'denne as to the plaintiff being drunk of ,the Ch of Ontober.</p>
        <p>Mr: Pugh having addressed the jury. His Hmiour firit pointed out to the jury that before proceeding any further in the C3FC it would be neceFsary for them to decide as to wEether they considered the man was drunk or not on the 4th of Oltober, and if so whether the drunkenness of the plaintiff brought about th- accident. Having reviewed the evidence oa the plint the jury retired and on corning into curt again, pronounced aftre.ement as to the man being sober. After a cal eful summing up h's Honour asked the jury to bring in their verdict, which thov did without ',tiring, finding for tl,e plaintiff with £25 damages in- cluding doctor's fee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCOItN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCOItN NOTES.</p>
        <p>LBY A COILLIRSPONDEIsiT</p>
        <p>There was -a very long sitting f the County Court, on Tuesday, when his Honour Judge Wynne Foulkes devoted about 7 hours to the hearing of a 'cass in which Frank Antrobus claimed damages for injuries sustained by falling, through the alleged neglect of Mr. Edgar Lea, in not protecting a hole</p>
        <p>he had made in connection with 6-mae operations he was carrying out in Nelson-st..on the 4th October. The case gave riqe to many interest- ing features, and excited considerable local com- m.-lit. Damages for £25 were awarded the plaintiff.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Philharmonic Sotiety submitted a second concert for the seison on Wednesday night. at the Public Ilal4-Runcorn, which testified to their abibity as caterers of goad music. The programme was a good one and well sustained. The Society , will give their third concert on Wednesday, March 22nd, when Gounod's " Nasse Solennelle " will form the first part, and a happily selected miscellaneous reportoire, the second. The artistes engaged in- clude Miss Ada Standen (soprano),Miss Clara Taylor (contralto), Mr Tom Barlow (tenor) and Mr Robert Grice (bass).</p>
        <p>The weather during the last few days has taken a turn and a very seasonsble frost prevails. Naturally the thOughts ot tIE are turning to " skates " and "skating," and as I write a happy thought strikes e. The Heath and Common in the vicinity of the Runcorn pumping station might be adopted and flooded to a shallow depth to provile safe skating for the inhabitant&amp; Perhaps the suggestion may prove of practical utility and at least a skate may be enjoyed on the" White Elephant's back."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Runcorn's victories in the football field are the outcome of no " fiagh in the pan " but the rault of a steady progression towards their ideal perfeltion and with the still improving form, their position in the league will b. vastly improved before the com- petition closes. Their last victory over Stockport retains for them their title to the champions of Cheshire.</p>
        <p>The following team has bean selected against Leigh, to-morrow (Saturday) at Rut:morn :—S Houghton. beck ; G Wilkinson, T Warder, J Butter- worth, D•Jones.three-qu%rters ; J Tolley, J Faulk- ner, halves; A Taylor, 6 Walker, Ii Farmer, J Lang- ley, F Gayt&amp;r. Darlington, J Tomlinson, and W Ughtfoot, forwards. A goad account will an doubt be given of the Leigh team. The" A" team, which journeys to Leigh, will be composed of the following players :—Forster, back; 0 Butter worth, IP Peters, G ltioston, R YateQ, three-quarters; J Kenyon, G Williams, halves; S Langley, G Dyer, A Kennion, W Woods, W Wilson, W Barker, G Messen- ger and F Jameson.—The kick-off is at three , o'clock.</p>
        <p>ELLii.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB VERSUS WIDNES ( LIBERAL CLUB. . The return match between these clubs took plriee at the ' Runcorn Liberal Club on Thursd,ty night. and excited no ' little interest. The previous encounter at Widnes resulted I in a win for the home team by 209, and considerable specula- ' thin war entertained as to whether Runcorn would be able to I retrieve the lass ; but as things turned out Runcorn only , just managed to secure a bare victory on the game of 49 , points, and thus lost on the home and home matches by I 160 points. Haliday and J Travers were the first to Antall 1 their tie and first blood was drawn by Runcorn, Carruthers . e ecHrecl the next game and Rowlinson the following one ; then came the first loss for Runcorn, W Brimelow going un- 1 der to F. Makin. This tie was lost by Brimelow failing at rather a difficult in-off when both players were 147. Qtr.lad secured an easy victory off Carter. Worrall had his own way , with Smith fairly running away. Disaster after disaster came I upon the Runcornians, Hughes, Lea, Wright and lilliot6 all being beaten, and only for Smith's vicaory in the last tie Widnes would have secured another win. Scores : RUNCORN. WIDNES. W Brimelow (capt) ......... 147 V Makin .... l5O It W Elliott - • W ; Carter T H Caruthers H Hughes .... 91 H Deakin T Lea. ...,.... 129 P Caldwell l5O A Worrall ............. ........ 150 J Smith &amp; Smith J Halliday l5O J Travers J Rawanson 133 James Travers A Green ......... ..........,..... 150 HlJolley R W Wright " 147 T What= --• 1657 16c)4 1608</p>
        <p>Majority for 'Runcorn 49 Miijority for Widnes on the two games, 161.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PHILHARMONIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>CONCERT AT THE PUBLIC HALL.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Philharmonie Society, on Wednesday evening provei quite an attraction at the Public Hall, Runcorn, when tney gave the second o oncert of the sea- son, Yet,the audience was not as large as the talent sub- mitted was deserving. The evening commenced with the aneouncetnent that Mr. William Green, who was ta have been the principal attrection was unable to be present, and though keenly ctsappeiated the audieace found ample atonement in toe singing of Mr. Fred Owen, of Liverpool (a baritone),whe came in his stead. Toe first item rendered was "Ave Maria" fr)rn the unfinished opera "Loreley" by Mendelssohn. Miss E Hubi New- combe took the solo very effectively, whilst the choir were noticeable in tile light and shade of their accompani- ment. The male ponion of the eh' ir were responsible for the rat number, a "Vintage song" out of the same work, wad acquitted themselves highly. The "finale" was a long piece and proved much more difficult, bet it fine rendering was given, and as for Miss Hubi New- combe's part in it. as soloiat,she displayed a voice of re- merkable culture to advent a;e.alr.Fred 0 wen in his &amp;meg adopted a happy style and appeared at case in all. His rine mellow Tome was of a widl cempass and pleasing to listen to. His songs were " Vete gallants in England " "The old Sod or," (encore " Mill wheel "), and" It is My marriage morn," all of whicl were well received. 'the Orchestral Scoey gave " Henry VIII. Daecce," with beautiful execution a,ad had to repeat. Miss Ethel Hubi-Newcombe in" Dear Bird of Winter," exhibited phenomenal powers, some of her trills being really fine Voice productiene She was recalled. rho selection " Poet and Peas in," by the orchestra wes very well given. Watson's vigorous part song " Tne gallant Troubadour" wee tie choir's uest performanee,and was as near perfection as pessible. Ttr, tone was good, and balacc and precision were carefu'ly attended to. The part song "Bridal chorus" (Cowen) eves the conclud- isee piece, and was rendered in cearacter with the other pet formances of the choir. On the whole the concert was a good one. The cheruses were pretty and lively rendered, whilst the bane excAled themselves, and but for the disappointment in Mr. Gr,.en not turning up, the whole would have passed off withoet a hitch. Mr. C. T. Reynolds, Macs. Dec. (Oxon) was the conduc- tor, and the aeecomp misc, Mii3 Berbera Johnsen, both of whcm were wanting in no part eular of their difficult peas. Toe arrangements for the eorteert were ad- mirably carricd out by Mr. George Pearson (treasurer), and Mr. Booth (secretary). and it is to their, efferet eh mks are dee for the suceess achieved. One thtn;2: which would have. added te the comtott of the audence, however, v; ould have been to have the room h ,ateci a htle but perhaps that omission was an ove:sight. kinzing of tile National Anthem brought the concert to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>LOCAL POLICE PitONIOTIONSI AND</head>
        <p>LOCAL POLICE PitONIOTIONSI AND</p>
        <p>CHANGES.</p>
        <p>The following premotions and transfers aro an. nouneed : —Soperintondent John Okell,Runcorn appointed first cla3s superintendent, :‘nd transferred to Altrineham. vice Superintsndent McDonald, who is re- moving to the North Wirral dision, stationed at E4re- mont. to fill the vacancy clawed by the retirement of Superintendent Ilindley. Dotctive Lespector D. Pear- s As, headquarters, Chester, to be second class superin- tendent and to be transferred to Runcorn. Mr. John Okell, superint-ndent t.f p )lice of the Run- corn divi.i.3n,hts been prom td o tee superinten floor of the Altrincham cliLiskoo, the duties of which he wid take up on February let. The following particulars of Mr. 011411's career in the police force will ne read with in- terest at this time :—Superin'endent Okell joined the forte in 1874,and after a br ef say at Hyde was r moved o Ceris. be sp-mt 18 months-, being prothe- ted to actiog sergeant. From thence he went to Aitrinc- barn, and alter two years was made full sergeant, and transferred to Crewe. He had only a shaft stay there, and was transferred to B illington,teng placed in charge of the police station. The suosequent eight years were spent at Tarporiey, where he wat greatly eet wined, and on leaving for New BrLhton he ens inaa the recipient of a handsome gold watch. He only put in three menthol at New Bsi.thton, and being promoted to the rink of in- spector—this is in 1888—he went to Wihnslow. About five years were spent there, a den leaving for North- wich in 1894 Mr. Okell received from the inhabit tots of Wilmslow an 3 district an illuminated address and a purse containing £25; while from the inhabit ints of the adjoining, village of Alderley Edge, he received a pretty clock. 'Mr. Okfill was anpointeu pupa intenient of the Runcorn &amp;vision in Navemb'tr, 1895, and during the time he his been in this part of the coutty he his gained the respsct and eiterm not only of those imrcediately as- sociated with him but of the public generally. He is to be sud6ceded at Runcorn by Inspector Pearson, of Chester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SIR J. T. BRITNNER, M.P., ON</head>
        <p>SIR J. T. BRITNNER, M.P., ON</p>
        <p>EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>Speaking at L've-pool on Monday evenine at a meet. ing, held under tee auspices of the Naitaara Counties' Education League,Sir J. T. Brannerol.P., wha presided* remarked that he had for over 30 years no v tem very keenly interested in the queeien of mticn al education. From 1867 to 1870 he was Ice! secretary of a branch of the National E lucstion League. A g ,01 many would remember that that famous league hei its headquarters in Birmingham. Those leade-s who 30 years agi.) be fol. lewe,i had been avociated with a party ef stagnation and a party of,eetragression in the inter of education. They had followed that party in their downward course, and had backed them in the proposal tin: came from the responsible Government that the public expenditure upon education should be limited in the case of national expenditure to 20s. per head per child per annum, and that in the cane of local expenditure it sheold be 293 per head per annum. That proposal, he teought, was the most shameful that had ever heel made to a civil- ized nation. (Her, hear.) It was the more shameful because the men who mach it were almoet without ex- ception in the position that their elecation had been provided for them at the cost of old 0 incitional chari- ties founded for the poor. He wished to utter a word as to the accusation that was brought against the party with which he had ranged himself. It was said some- times that the Noncoaformists of Englandland Wales valued too lightly the religious educ,,Von of their chil- dren. There could be no accusation m ne) eilly—(applauee) —and there could be none mote utterly untrue. The Nonconformists of this country had made sacrifices for generations and for c .nturies which, taken in the light of their means, were a hundredfoli greater than the sacrifices mace by the wealthy who belonged to the National Church. They were ready to co- Iperate with their UT() w-eitizene in order that relieious ins:ruction. as well as geed secular instruetien, should he imparted to their children. (Applause.) They claimed that this was now provided in the schco's that b togged to the public, and they also claimed that wherever public, money was expended it sheuld he expend td under public authirity. Their sole object was that the national intellect and the national couscience should be culti— vated to as high A point as was possible, in order that the nation might be great in numbers and in wealth. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND Eutcrnic LTGHTING.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND Eutcrnic LTGHTING.</p>
        <p>The memorial of the Corporation cf Warrington and others against the Lancashire Electric Power Company's Bill was considered by the Examiner of Bills in the House of Commons on Tuesday, and the allegations of non-compliance with Standing Orders. were sustained. The Bill has, therefore, met with the same fate as befell the South Lancashire and Cheshire Electricity Company' a Bill, which was thrown out last week. The Corporation are to be eongrattalated on their success at the early stage of their oppostion to these Bills, the objects of wnich were to obtain powers to supply eleetricity through- out the whole of South baucaehire and North , Cheshire, and to override the powers obtaiued by 1 corporations within their respective districts. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT HOMING SOCIETY.. This slciety held their annual general meting at the headquarters, Norton Arms Hotel, on Tuesday. Mr. B. F. Greenough occupied the chair. The fol- lowing officers were duly elected for the. coming season :—Mr. Greenough was again unanimously elecred president; vice-presidente,Mes,r.s.Councillor Longton, John Houghton, B Burrows, W Lymm and S J Woodcock; committee,. Messrs. II Clare, J rimperley, P Houghton, J Edgerley, H Moston, H. Woodward, R Wright, W Leigh, C Lythgoe, and A Whitt4k,:r ' • Mr A Whit- taker was again elected secretary and treasurer; and Mr J Timperley was elected assistant secre- tary. Toe programme for the season is (Old girds), Worcester,, Swindon, Bournemouth, Guern.. 8.37, Jersey, and Nantes (France.) Young Birds. wocceit.r, Gloucester. and Swindon. The rules were slightly altered to suit the above programme. It was decided to have two average Cups or prizes one for old birds to be flown up to and including Nantes and one for young birds up te. , and irte; •:ing Swindon. The produce stake race . for young birds was left in the bands of the Com- mittee to decide as to how the prizes shall be divided. Mr. Thomai Warburton was elected a 1 member and, Messrs. Lythgoe and Sou, Oddfellows Arms, Earlestown, were nominated for member- ship. • --</p>
        <p>THE " LEVIATHAN " PLACARD, Single Sheet 51t. Sin. by :3ft. Sin. THE LARGEST IN THE DISTRICT. A MONSTRE PLACARD may be foamed by joininK number of.tba ' LEVIATHAN" SHLEIS. JOHN WALKER AND CO.,</p>
      </div>
