      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRA.TE S</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRA.TE S</p>
        <p>SHAMEFUL EXPOSURE OF A GUILD BY A GoLBORNE 'WOMAN. At the Warrington Borough Police Court, on Saturday, before Mr. J. Geddes and other magistrates, Elizabeth Brooks, 1, Glass-street, Golborne, was charged with , beinc, drunk and disorderly and exposing her child in Buttermarket-street at 10-20 p.m., the previous day.— P.C. Howard said he was in Buttermarket- street at the time in question, and it was raining very hard. The prisoner was very drunk and the child was wet through, an i its head was uncovered. Witness watched prisoner go into the Lower Angel Inn and sub=equently the manager of the place called him in to eject the woman. Witness was afraid the child would CgMe to some harm and he tried to get it from her. She was very rough and used most obscene language. On the way to the Police Station she knocked his helmet off, and on one occasion she held the child by the left leg whilst she attempted to strike witness with her right hand. She was very rough at the police st ation. —John James Pritchard, c'erk, 67, Wellfield-street, gave corroborative evidence. He assisted the officer.—Mr. Howard, of the Lower Angel Inn, said the woman came into the bar but he refused to serve her, and afterwards called in the effl-,e,.. She had previoasly been in th, bouse.—The Chief Constable said the prisoaer had lo,ea xtp a good many times in Golborne. He thought it was a very bad case.—The Bench committed prisoner to gaol for three months with hard labour. Two MONTHS' BARD LABOUR, FOR A ROLIGR. At the Warrington Borough Police Curt on M-miay 'Morning, before Alderman Harrison and other magic- bates, John Morran, 30, Jamez-street, was charged with being drunk and disor ierly in Winwick-s'reet on Sa.'-ur- day night, and wi la assaulting Th rnis Koight, landlord Of the Alexandra Vaults, So Aland-ro ad.—Thomas Knight said that at 9.30 on Saturday night prisoner came to his house and ask.d for drink. Prisoner was under the in- fluence of drink, and witnese knowing he was a disor- derly man, reused to serve him. Complainant was taking drink on a tray to some people, when prisoner said" If you wontc serve me you won't serve anyone else." FI, then ki Iced the tray ()it of his handi, smash- Am; the glasses and spilling the contents. Prisoner 'truck him a Wow on the lace, knocking him to the ground. His teeth were loosened. On three successive satrirdays he had been assaulted, and he had stitches in his head then for an assault on him the previous Satur- day.—The Chief Con,table said there was an organized jAeling by a set of roughs against Mr. Knight, who tried 1; keep his house in goad order.—P.O. Lewis proved the tase of disorderly conduct and drunkennoss.—The mg s- e atm sent M-Tran to prison for two months with h ard hour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Wa.RRINGTON BOROU(iR POLICE COURT. TBIDAY —Before the Mayor (chairman) Councillor Sant-</p>
        <p>- hurst. and Dr. Gornall. TEMPORARY Atrrimerrr.—Neherni au Taylor applied for temporary authority to sell at the off-license at 103, Jones' Buildings, off Winwiek-road.—The applioation was acceded to. ComairrrteG WILFUL Dam IGE.—Harriet Daniels. 4 Pump - yard, was charged on a warrant with committing wilful damagelto windows on October 15th.—Defendent pleaded yullty.—Mr. Richard Bond said the value of the windows and frames broken was 10s 6d. She had no rightin the yard —The Bench fined defendant the damages and ths costs and adjourned the case for a month to give her a chance to pay. A SEPARATION ORDER ORANTED.—John Wright. 69, Cart- wright-street, was summoned for deeerting his wife Emma Wright.—Mrs. Wrighr, said on the 33th December her hus- band summoned her for assault but the case was dismissed When they got home defendant carried her out of the house and locked her out from 12 o'alock. until 3 o'clock. Witness had the door burst open and her husband then got his furniture taken away and threw hers out in the street. On the day after (Saturday) he made her an offer of 6s 6d a week but she refused it.—The M..yor : It is very evident you young people are not going to agree. It is a miserable state of things. —A separation order was granted for 7s a week and the costs. ALLEGED THREATS.—Charlotte Belly Was Summoned for making use of threats towards Martha Ann Kelly on the 12th January.—Defendant pleaded not guilty.—Complainant gave evidence of the alleged threats, but the bench dismissed the case, as there was no corroborative evidence. MONDAY —Before Alderman Harrison (in the chair), Alder- man Blaeklaurst, Mr. Birchen, and Councillors Shaw Green and Smethurst. Af leuespoNsint,E FA.THER.—John Dean, farm labourer, Croft. was charged with running, away frara Warrington, leaving his child chargeable to the funds of the Union. Mr Shearer, who prosecuted on behalf of the guardians, said the child had been in the house since the 23rd November, and the defendant was epprehendel at Croft on Saturday last.— He was committed to gaol foo a month with hard labour. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—JOsepII Boyd 2. Old-road, and Peter Dutton, 35, Elizabeth street, were sent to prison for 11 days with hard labour for this offence on Saturday night. FINED FOR OBSTRUCTION.—Thomas Hewitt was fined 58 for allowing rubbish to be left on the footpath in ilaydock- street, so as to be an obstruction to passengers, on the 17th January. A MAGISTRATE'S OPINION.—James Leigh and Charles H. Newton were summoned for allowing their dogs to be at large unmuzzled.—The Chairman asked what the maximum Penalty was.—The Clerk: £2o.—The Chairman: It is a bit of a farce fining people ls.—Both defendants were fined is each. A PRISONER'S RECORD—Elizabeth Ellen Fleming and Elizabeth Rosthern. were summoned for being drunk and disorderly in Dial-street. on the 14th January. Rosthern did not appear, alid Fleming, who is a fustian cutter, pleaded guilty.—P.C. Baxendale nrcred the case. —The Chief Con- stable said defendant now made her 50th appearance. She was only 25 years of age.—The Bench sent her to gaol for 14 days hard labour —Defendant (laughing): They could give me 14 years. P.C. Baxendale couldn't lock me up. DRUNK AND DISoRDERLY.—Mary Mannion and Patrick McDonald, Berry's-yard, were each sent to prison for 14 days' hard labour.—Mart,ha Sutcliffe. 3, BUckley's-vard, for a similar offence in Winwiek-street. on Saturday night, was fined 56.—Thomas White, of Wigan, was fined 53 for being drunk and disorderly in Scotland-road at 6.30 on Saturday night.—Johu Garrett Winwick-road, was summoned for similar offence in Winwiek-street, on January 14th, and was fined 5s and costs. ”A GOOD Reasox."—William George Turner.33,Longshaw- street, applied for an order for his child Edith to be ex- empted from the vaccination laws. He produced the birth certificate, and stated that his child was ill. It would have to undergo an operation shortiv.—The Chairman. of the Bench (Alderman Harrison), said that WAS a good reason to object to vaccination. He had never heard such a good reason given before.—The application was granted. A CAB DRITER CHARGED WITH FURIOUS Joseph Rowne was charged with furiously driving a horse and cab in Bridge-street, on the 3rd January, tattle danger of passsngera.—The Chief Constable said it would be within the recollection of the Bench that on the day in question there was a lamentable accident to an old gentleman named Br the ell, who was rein over and had a number of ribs broken. Be was taken to the Infirmary. where he lay between life and death for a considerable time, but he was hal py to say that be al peered befcre the Bench that morning. The corn • Visitant had had to be conveyed to the Pollee Station in a cab. lie understood that Mr. Browne who defended had agreed to pay the costs of an adjournment of the se.—The case was adourned until Monday next. UNIICENFED DRITERS.—WiIIIam Fettles was charged with plying for hire with a waggonette without a driver's licence. —P.C. Cooper said that at 2.10 on Saturday afternoon, the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>EVERY WOMAN</head>
