        <p>Should send two stamps for our ITI-EW BOOK containing valuable information to Ladies, how all Irregularitiesmay be entirely Avoided or Removed by Simple Means. Recommended by eminent physicians as the only Safe, Sure, and genuine Remedy. "Never Fails." Thousands of Testimonials.—Mr. W. R. BLANCHARD, Claremont Forest-road, Dalston, London.</p>
        <p>z 4</p>
        <p>14th January, he was on duty at Market-gate. when he saw the defendant in charge ot a horse and waggonette, the latter containing passengers. The vehicle started from Corporation- street, and went to Latchford. Re noticed th. defendant on two or three subsequent occasions, and at 5.50, before going off duty, he stopped him and asked him if he was a licensed driver. The man replied that he was not, and witness re- ported the matter to luspector Taylor, chief hackney carriage inspector.—Defen-lant, who did not appear, was fined 10s. and costs, gs.—Mr- Wibberley (the owner of the conveyance in question).—May I ask a question ?—The Chief Constable (emphatically) No, you may stand down. You have noth- ing to do with the case except that I may summon you for allowing this man to drive. Alfred Mhaett was charged with a similar offence, and fined 10e and costs. Inspector Taylor proved the C 1.30. TUESDATO—BaiOre M.. B. Garnett (chairman), and Messrs.</p>
        <p>Geddes and Birchall. A DlsonDEntor liouse.—Elizabeth Ellen Fleming and Elizabeth Hose were charged the latter with keeping a brothel at 30-Dial-street, and the former with aiding and abetting--P C.'s Leonard and Stewart and Sergeant Evans proved the case, and the prisoners were committed to prison for three months with hard labour. ATTEMPT (?) TO CoMMII SUlClDE.—Harriett Williams, 23, New-road. was charged with attempting to commit suicide in the Bridgewater Canal at Blackbear Bridge at 5.30 the pre- vious afternoon.—The Chief Constable said the defendant was only about 17 years of age. It was her second so-called attempt to commit suicide On the previous occasion she was taken in hand by Mr. Taylor who said there was abso- lutely no good to be done with her.—Samuel Daniels em- ployed by the Bridgewater Canal Company saw the gie standing up to her neck in the water. He got her out, and she was eventually brouzht to the police station. He did not know what they co del do with toe girl, but he would ask for a remand for eight days to Strangeways Gaol, when she would 3erhaps coma to a better itate of mind.— A remand was granted. San° SLATTENDA.NCE CASP:I.—The follo wing parents were summoned by Mr. Jones, School Attendance Officer, and preved.fia di being granted and orders made in some cases for the b.d attendance of their children, : —Thomas Barton. 17. Chester-street, order ; Patrick Collin, 17, Ship Yard, sent to Hightown Truant School; Michael Flan Kan; 24. Sliver-st. fined 2s 6d; Joseph Green, 1, Sid lad street, fined 2s 61; William Lewis. 43, Laira-street, s3nt t a Hightown School ; Wm. plolyneux. 10. Glazebrook-street, fined Is for three children ' • Isaac Acton Monks '.33, Hoylestreet. order ; James Sculley, 9, Crossl- y-street, fined 2s 6d, Francis Whitetlouse, 41, Hoyle-street fined 2s 6d. WEonEsos.Y.—Before Alderman Sutton (Presiding) Alder- man James Fairciongh. and Messrs. Birehall and Geddes SPECIAL LICENSE.— Mr. Harrison applied ter a special license at the Cemetery Hotel. from 9 to 2 a.m.. on January 25th. and 27th, on the occasion of the Bowling Club ball.— Granted. Scirmatows CoNDUCT.—Mary Ann Carley was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Scotland-road at 10 minutes past three the previous day.—P.O. Edwards proved the case.—The Chief Constable said a minister had complained that defendant's conduct in Scotland-road was scandalous.—The bench fined her 53 and costs or seven days. A BURLY BEGIGA.E.—John Walsh, a well set up fellow, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Irlam-st , at 5 minutes to seven the previous night.—P.C. Wright who proved the case said prigoner was in a drunken condition going from shop to shop bagginz.--Sergeant Cheney. corroborated. Defendant was fine! 53 and costs or seven</p>
        <p>days. ABSCONDING FROM Tag BOROUGII.—Thomas Clyaes, was charged on a warrant with running away from the Borough on January I.sitta, leaving his wife chargeable to the common funds of the Union. tir. B Shearer p-oved the case.—The Chief Constable sal I prbonar had a bad character. He had been before the court ten times.—The magistrates committed prisoner to gaol for two maths' hard labour.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PE rrY SEiSIONS. TILURSDAM—Before Messrs. T. J. Gillespie (presiding), C. B.</p>
        <p>F. Borron. J sv. Emmett. and Robert Stone. OCCASIONAL LICENSE —Alfred Tunstall applied for an oc- casional license to s -11 at the Conservative Ball at the Town Hall on February Bth from 8 till 3 a.m.—Granted. LICENSE TRA.NSFER.—Rxthur Wallace Bishop applied for the tran.fer o the license of the Pied Bull Hotel.—The police hat no objection an the application was granted. DRUNK AND DI3ORDERLY.— The foliowing were lined for this offence i-313.rtin Larkin. Newton, 31st December. 53 and come ;Thomas Hi 'gins. Golbarne. .1.1 tr v, Za 61 snd costs; William tlitchen. Gollsofne. 19th January. 23 ad and costs; James Noon, Golborne. 14th January. 53 anal costs. Catherine Qu,nn was fined 23 6d and co Is for being drunk at Newton on January 23rd. REPRESENTING T,3 RE TaAVELLER3 —ThomtS SiDgiStOtl, James Hewitt and William Stainer. ,pleadel 4ailty-to falsely representing themselves to be travellers on Sunday last.— Mr. Knowles who appeared for the comp ainant Alexander Olough of the Plou4h Inn said the defendants had not travelled the three miles reirtire I by la.w.—The be rah fined each defendant 5s including costs.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SE3SION3. MONDAY.—Before Messrs. P.I. Sp sakmsn. (presiding) and</p>
        <p>Join Oeklesion. DRUNK AND iII)TOU3. —Police-coastable Bdwerds told the Bench that he was on duty in WE.terloo-roed, on the 20th of the month. Whilst there he heard a row in a back street, and on investigati-on he fount a man nomad John Saetley. labourer of Nelson-street, kicking up a row and smashing Willi)W3 there. Santlev was very drunk and heft to be (octal up, but was let out on bail until that day.—Santtey who did not Contred.lo the Constable's story was fined sa. and costs. • ALLI,GED NEGLKOT AND CRUMMY TO autLOß4l.—Ad ward and Ann Philbin, 130,3arrey-31reet. Runcorn, were cleared on the instance of the National Society f the Prevention of Cruelty to Children for crust and unlewfal neglect of their 12 year old daughter *argent Ann —CI ef tets at first wished to be tried before a higher court ; but on being told that they would be remanded to Q carter Sessions And -would have to await trial in poison they decided to to • tr ed by the magistrates.—Betti pleads I net guilty.-- \lr. A.. Brown,selicitor. Warrington, appeared on behalf the society, land the Philbins conducted their owa defence.—Prene Mr. Brown's opening statement which was corroborated by In- speetors M cAteler (Warrington) and Owen ('Northwich), an I Police Constable Gre-rory (Runcorn) It appeared that the girl was an Invalid, and had been suffering for some considerable time from a diseased hip. Subsequently the case was reported to Inspector Ackteer. The mother was seen hut was mud tied in drink and no, hint could be done witn her. On examining the child inspeetor McAteer found her to be in a terrible con tit-on arti when tie touched the diseas sd psrtion. she fein'ed. In was pointed ' out to the defendants th at the child shreild have certain attention and that a doctor sheuli be called in. Con- sevently Dr. Rabinson was called in, and he described th case as one that should be Altera le I ti irnni edietel v. an i advised her removal to is hospite, er infirmary. He also soundly rated the defendants, and toll them that they should be ashamed of theme-Ives. The upshot was that the child sees removed to the Children's Hospital, at Liver- pool, ant remained there until the 21st of September. which wee salisfeetory enough. On that date either thrones settee alterations to the hospital or sorile other cause it was found that the inmates could be kept there no tenger, therecore the child was csaken to the Workhouse Hospital, Liverpool. Th e Scperintendent of the W rtheuse vecone the defendants resnectlng her m tint-mance there, and as a result the defendants remeved the elite, bringing her back to Runcorn on the 31st of December. and it was from that date that the el''is') aet to the child hid taken place, The defendants' house was visited on several o.33t- sions by Inspector McAteer, and whet he fowl 1 there was disgraceful. The child was lying on a sofa in the kitchen barely covered by a counterpane. she was in sprints, alit filthy, actually lying in her own excrement. Tao retritin te- of the evidence ref rred to the poverty and wretchedness of the abode, and the inemerality of the child's su*roaa tinge. Thera was a women named Mars' White tieing with the de- fendants and there was ns pl tca fp, her t sleee, in face she stated she slept in the chair in fro at of the are every night. It WAS alleged that the defendant II lw ard etsilibin, earned about 35s to £2 a week, and that the sad state of the home was due to the drunken habits of the defendants.—lividence having been given by Inspectors Owen and aice.teer. olaco Constable Gregory and Sergeant White the defendants elected to give evidence on thei.• own behalf. The tether of the child said he did not e arn 35s t 22 bit wily ahoen ?Se. or 27s is weak.He gave a £1 a week to his wife ant they cave p'enty of foot to the children and never starved them He could not read himself and theeght the tenter from the Liverpool Workhouse was for him to take his child zeal.— The female defendant stated that the child was net dirty et all. She got El a week from he husband when he got it, himself, end always provided sufficient food for the children. She had another chill (a boy) and he was all-right —.Ur. Brown pointed out that they did not content the children were not nourished ; they were desirous of protecting the child and securing her better treatment,. —qrs. Mary Haddock, a neighbour of the defendants' gave evidence on their behalf.—ln answer to the Beech both ths dereedalts stated they would do all they ce cle for the girl. and whateverlthe magistrates desire I. Tee parish mars iced been called in and the girl was being well cared for at present. and was to be removed to a hospital on Werinesde 7 next.—The Bench finally adjourned the case till wet Months,. to give the de'endents an oepertanity of domg what they promised for the girl. Judgment was reserved.</p>
