        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. LA.NCASHELE SENIOtt CONIPETITION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DEFEAT TYLDE3LEY A SECOND TIME.</p>
        <p>Warrington paid a visit to ferldelley on S iturday, and foend the " Hungers " prepared to play, though toe groutid was in a shockine state. Rain was falling he wily. Tao visitors took a strong team, the only ceange from th 3 ordinary c moosition, being that of Duckworth for Fish on th 3 left wing. Tyldesley ware short of Kirk Bagley, the new tiaresqu irt sr, aid alt)'ether had a poor team up. George Merry played full- back, Keens tilline tee centre pesitien. Tao War- rington team was as follows : Hellion, full back ; Deck- wo ti, Cr.”s, Isherwood, and Harris, threeq letters; Bate and Ashurst, half backs; N aylor, Taylor, B tard- m tn, SwW, Seholtze., Fairhurst, Towaend and Sullew, for warde. i The opening sta.ees were in favour of th 3 home side. George B orry puttine in a good run and kack. Hallam relieved, and aided by long kicks Warrineton attac tsd. rne visitors aim ost got over tram a bad mull by E ;kers- ley. From a soarp pass by Bekeraley France made a good run, but was kaockerl hate touch. Warrington attacked centinuously and forced numeraus minors, the game being of a scrambling and uninteresting char- acter. Folio sting a mull by George Berry. Aseurat scored a soft try, Bate'; kick at goal going wide of the mark. Warrington continued to attace,but the passing, owing to tile greasy nature ot the ball, was very prior, and aithough they forced many minors they could not add 3 tangiole paint. fialf-time score :—Warringtoe, one Vs- (three poiets); Tyldesley, nil. The second half opened in Tyldesley's favour, but although they Were repeatedly on tee line the passing was very poor, and they could not store. Ths eVarrineton forwards again eot geine.ani France m ikine a bid mu 1 the Tyldesley lin o was threatened. Bate tried hard to make an open- ing, anl Harris put in a strong rue bane overhauled by France. Be quick following up Worteingt on tecklei - liaLarn, play for a thus being contested as mid•field. , enere was no clever to 'that', the game resolving itself into a farce Near the close Dueksvertie made a sharp soriet down touch, and being bowled over by Gorges Berry threw the ball to Bate, woo crosed tneline at the corner, the goal kick failing. Final some :—Warring- too, two tries; Tyldeeley,nil. RUNCORN srim, SUCCE3SFUL. S-ooltpert always attract a good gate at R morn, but the we sther effec:ed the atteniance at the Irwell-lale tricloture on Saturd iy, and tee crowd did no; come up to expectations, still there was a fair attendance present when the teams made their appearance. Runcorn t 3am was si roller to the one last weak with just a slight alteration ie the for ward rank—Farmer and Langley aopeariag, bat Stockper: were minus the redoubtable Sivas., The following were the players : Runtern : S fleugecon,back; D Jones,J Butterworth, T Wardee T Wilieson, t areeciaarters ; J Jolley, J Faulkner. halves; II F inner, J Tenlinien, W Isiehtf set, S Walker, F Gaiter, F Darliagton,iV BsttlevAingley, forwards. Seockp ort : Simiiter. back; W le thinsoe, Cherlten, Griffiths, Grange, terentlearters ; J Hickey, J Gidlow, halve.; Wcize 7, Oran, Lamson, Garside, C oliias,Pearce, Diviis on, Brimble, forwards. Steckn at kicked off and at once the game opened lively. tiat owing to the nature of the ground which was heavy sv th mud and covered with water ie parts ri-o re— liable play could be got in by either side for some little tiere After a c onsiderable number of unprefitable at- tempts Faulkner secured from a scrummage and pass ug after a neat hit of dodging, to D. Joess,the latter made off and seoesd at the corner. Farmer's kick was good, but the mud was master of the situation. Stockport re- turned and should neve got over, but their efforts at passing were not quite up to the mark. Hoaghton was good and returned ell. His tackle?. was superb. Run-. r corn eoa t tinued to old the advantage, and some beauti• , ful work was put nby Jolley and Faulkner. On the. ' Stockport side Hickey responded in fine style. Teie Run- corn pack were far too kern, however, and he came in for some rough handling. Runcorn again got going bit were repulsed. Both teams were playing a good game when Ruaoorn initiated a fine passing movement—Faulk- ner, Jolley, Butterworth, and Jones, all havine a hand in it—which resulted in Jones sm.:urine a further try. No other score resulted up to half-time when the ac ore wis —Kuncorn. 2 tries, Seockpert, nil. On ree'artiag Runcorn at once became ageressive and the men of "hatter's" fame were at once hard pressed. A further disadvantage WAS the appearance of the sun which had been obacured behind the clouds,now coming out and showieg right into the faces of the Stockport men. Raecern early on began to cause anxiety to the Stockport defence, and Simister was troubled time on time. No Matter how strong an attempt was made to respond Scockporc were slowly but surely being beaten and it was only a matter of time as to when the Runcern team would scare. A number of chancaa occurred but owing to the slipperiness of the leather, no finish could be ac complished. Jolley charged do qn a kilt{ by Sinister an Farmer, and Tom- linson raced off after it. Tomlinson was there first, and scored just before Simister could prevent him. Farmer improved cleverly. Stockport came away from the kick, but Houghton was"a'l there,"andnent them to the right about. A penelty against the visitors saw Farmer land a further god, which, however, proved the last, for try bow they would Runcorn, though mastert of the Rime- lion coeld not get a further point. Final result : i Runcorn 2 goals, 3 tries, (13 points), Stockport nil.</p>
        <p>SALFORD v. WIDNES. At Salford in wretched weather, and before 8,000 spectator.. Toe ground was a morass, and the play of a most scrambling description throughout. Aq the scoring was done in the first half. Williams scored for S ,Iford, Po.illins improving. Griffiths rAr.aine,l a glal ith a lucky fly kick, whilst Widnes got an unimproved try. Result—Salford, 2 goals 1 try (7 points) ; Widnes, 1 try (3 points).</p>
