      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>OLDHAM v. LEIGH.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>A close struggle wag witnessed at o;dham, where the 11me side m Inaged to secure a victory by t'IG narrow margin or one try (Arthur Lees) to nil. ROCaDA.LE HORNE rs v. MORECAMBE.</p>
        <p>Tile Hornets had a poor team at Ronidale. and were be:aten by two tries to nil. Hi.dwin played finely tor Nl:,recambe, and obtainel the tw, tries.</p>
        <p>YORKSEIAE SENLOtt COIEPErITION.</p>
        <p>LEEDS V. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>Ha,lifax joorneye.d to Leeds, watire good gime Wai witneised. The visitora wol by one try to nil. The score was ohttiaed by Kitaoa, who ran in ahortly after half-t itne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>HUDDERSFIELD v. BRAAILEY.</head>
        <p>HUDDERSFIELD v. BRAAILEY.</p>
        <p>Ahe contest resulted at Huddersfield, although the o t groiand militated agains'.l good play. The bornesters managed to gain a try just before the end, aud thus won by ttar(e points to nil. BRADFORD v. LIVERSEDGE.</p>
        <p>Ths wrAcned condition of the turf spoiled this match at Bra if )rd. Ribartson obtained a try in each half, Cooper converting. The home tsim thus won by two goa.ls and two tries to nil. BATLEY v. MANNINGRA.M. Batley wot .on their own grouni by three goals and threo tries t &gt;gine try. Dairei scored thres times for the winners and Oakland dropped a goal, while Rhodes crossed the line for Manningharri.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE "A" TEAMS COMPETITION. WALIRINGTON "A" v. TYLDESLEY "A." Toe wooden sooonists of tae" A " team League were the visitors to Warrington on Saturday, and proved easy victinu. Wirringt en had a fall team up, the com- positioa of it beiag as follows :—J Bosc aw, full back ; Holcrofc, Hockenhall, Ratcliffe, and Moores, thraequar- ters ; R B tte and Allea, half backs ; Joliey, Elen, Bar- rat, Dea,kiu, Hatton, Cunningham, Skelharn, and Wil- cocks, forwards. To. weather was of a shocking des- cription, rain falling he tvily at the commenzemont, while the ground was in a vary soft and soppy - state. Warrington had to face the wind in the first half, but they assumed the aggressive right away. Anything like combination amoag the backs WAS out of question, the balk of the play being left with the for wards. The latter were soon on the line, Ha-,tort having hard lines in crossing the dead btll [in.!. After Bate had made a clever dodgy run, the forwards took the blll along in a body and Cunaingham scored the first try. Hotcroft kicked a goal. 'Fyl !coley were defending almost con- tinuously, but kept their line intact, their efforts being ' aided by the great difficulty the home backs had in handling the ball. Before the interval Holcroft brought the scsre up ti 7 points with a fine penalty goal. The as:coati half wag; little more than a /scramble, and quite half- t-lozea tries were missad before Moores ran in at tile corner. Holcroft landed another grand goal. Later the forward; dr;bbled down the field. and looked like losing th s b 01 ,when flockenhull dashed up and grounded over the goal-line. Another Roll by Holcroft completed the scoring, the final being, Warrington 4 goals, 3 tries (17 points), Tyldesley, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>wEsr LA.NCASETIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>wEsr LA.NCASETIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN filliNEN V. WIDNE3 WHITE STAR.—It Rea- corn. Toe home team kicked off against the wind, a good return being made. Midfield Way follo wed, the ball being kicked across. A good opening was spoiled by a knock on J.:coal the pack. Joyce passed to Green who scored. 13 dley failed at goal. From the drop out scrurnm.ging ens led, White Star bola?, the Wtter team in p ick. Gain n; possession at the centre they kicked to home half. Hadley replied and geLting the ball again caused play to be • waged on the visitors' 25. Toe visitors being penalized for not playing the boil, Stringer kicked a goal. Midfield play followed, the home team playing a lose game. Robinson with a long kick sent to touch close to Hornets' goal line. Ballec' kicked c'ea,r. HOf time—Hornets, one goal one try.' , White Scar nil. Toe second half was more in Hornets fAvour. A dribble by the borne forwards ended in Riley getting a try an i T. Hughes kicking a goal. Corker next s-.:orad a goal. T. Hughes dropped another. Final score —Hornets, 17 points ; Waite Star nil. This makes the fifth win for the horne tiani, their line not having been cr:ssed since December 17;1. FLETCHER's GAS ENGINEERS v. CADISHEAD AND IRLAIC—On Saturday Fle nor's E agineers journeyed to 1 Cadishead, and succeeded in defeating the home team by three tries to nil. The home team was an indifferent one, swing to several prominent players who are ern ployed at the P artiagton coal ha,%in, hniqz called away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FRIENDLY 1A."1:011</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY 1A."1:011</p>
        <p>BIRKENFIEA.D WANDERERS v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At Btrkenhead, on Saturday, before a small attend- ance, owing to the wretched state of the weather. Dillon initiated a capital movement fer the hsme side, aryl passing to Watson the latter in turn transferred to Clayton, who dribbled ovPr the St. Helens line, Doherty kicking dead. After this St Helens moved away, and Liversage receiv- ing from S3ott raced through the Wanderers' backs and ob.ained a captal try, Whiteley failing with the shot at val. The Wanderers next attacked hotly, and during the rail Harris was hurt, the game being stopped for a short period. On restarting. the home side resumed the attack, and Watson was within an ace of dropping a goal. Taylor followed with a fine dribble, but Doherty saved well, and the " Saints'" front rank dribbled away, with a resulting try from Little, the kick at goal again ended in failure. Oa resuming, the play for a time favoured the Wanderers, a dangerous rush being staved off by Doherty. Then Simpson and Csvouy were prominent in a forward dribble by the "Saints," hut without any tangible result. Towards the finish Gladwin crossed the Birkenhead lines, and the game ended in favour of St. Helens by 3 tries to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOIBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOIBALL.</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM BEA.TEN AT HELSBY.</p>
        <p>--- On Saturday afternoon the return match in the Chester Junior League was to have come off at Helsbv, but owing to the wretched state of the grot:nd due to the prevailing rainy weather, the referee declared the league match off. The spectators who had ventured out were treated to a spirited exhibition of football, and the team; proved to b 2 very evenly balanced. Helsby were, ly,wever, the better fioi3hir3 and made no mistakes in front of goal, with the result that they won by 4 goals to I. When the league match does come off, judging by the enthusiasm displayed, the event will be a regular " Derby." NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS v. PEASLEY CROSS. 'rue above Liverpool and District Combination match 7713 played at West Derby on Saturday before a fair gate. Owing to the recent heavy reins tbe ground vas</p>
        <p>a perfect quagmire, and the players found it difficult to keep their feet. Newton were ehort of C. 11.11, and it is worth mentioning that this is the first time this player has been absent all the four years he has been connected with this club. The game was a one sided affair all through, and Newton eventually ran out winners by eight goals to nil RUNCORN A.F.C. v. WARRINGTON OLYMPIC.</p>
        <p>Played at Warrington ou Saturday in miserable weather. Hopwood commenced operations half an hour late. The visitors combined beautifully and Abel cen- treing aceurately.flepwool defeated the home custodian with a sple.iclid shot. Runcorn kept up the pressure, Lunt passing to Gleave who scored the second goal with a good shot. At this time of the game the visiting halves passed to their forwards grandly and Hopwool gaining possession, beat the home backs, and scored Runcorn's third goal. At the interval Runcorn were leading up three goals to nil. In the second half the home team pressed, but could not penetrate the sterling defence of the visitors, Burrows, Carruthers, Prophet, and Et,ans doing some splendid work in this respect. Runcorn had extremely bard luck, in not increasing their score. A god game r2sulted in th 3 defeat of the home team by 3 goals to nil. LIVERPOOL AND DIS TRIG!' LEIGUE.</p>
        <p>WA CtRINGTON ST. ELPHIN'S V. EVERTOb7 ALBION.--•</p>
        <p>Played at Warrington on Saturday its wet weather. The home team kicked cff, and after about two minutes. play Singleton put the ball into the net, the Everton goalkeeper making a brilliant effort to save. The restart saw the Everion men take the ball to the home quarters but Glover sent them back, and the Alblons were plac !d on the defence. Several stinging shots were cleared by the visiting goalkeeper. The goal kick eased the pressure for a time, until the Saints were awarded a penalty kick which the visiting goalkeeper cl:-ared. Soon after Riley gave the home team a further lead with a grand shot. For the rest of this half play was even, and at half-time the sclre was—Sc. aphin's two goals; Evertoa Albion, nil. On re-starting Lao Albion showed some tricky pas- sing, and sweral times got within scoring distance, but the state of the ground did not allow the ball to travel. Eventually St. Elphin's again attacked, and Riley added a third point. From now tophe conclusion play was of a give-and-take nature. Filial score—St. Elphin's, three goals; Everton Albion, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
