      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WIGA.N CUP TIES.</head>
        <p>WIGA.N CUP TIES.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN v. BAmFußLONG.—rhis match in the se- cond round of the above cup was played at Bamfurlong, before about 500 spectators. Nearly half this number accompanied the visitors (who are strong favourites for the cup). Before the match commenced Eerlestown en- tered a protest against the wretched state or the ground. Indeed the recent heavy rains had rendered tile ground so bad that before the game had been in pro- gress a quarter of an hour the majority of the players were almost uureeognisable. The homesters won the toss and elected to play with the wind at their backs, Hampson setting the ball in motion a quarter of an hour late. The game was very even at the start, and considering the heavy state of the ground, very fast and exciting. Smith and Alldridge at length dribbled the ball down the Eeriest° wn right, and Smith centred well, Hampson shooting just outside at a terrific rate. Following this, the home left got going and the ball was shot hard against the side of the net. A great deal. more roughness was shown than was neces- sary, Green on one occasion kicking the Bamtuileug outside left's legs clean from under him and sent him limping abler; the field. Just about this time the rain cased and the sun commenced to shine. Some grand passing movements by Settle and Leach enabled the viiitors to get a footing in the Batnfurlong qnartere, but so:ne nice play by the home forwards transferred the venue to the visitor's' goal where Linton spoiled a grand chance by shooting outside. Both teams strove hard to score but without avail and half-time arrived with a blank sh-aet. Upon resumiag the visitors at once took up tae remain and All- dridge was given a spldndid opening but he dallied too long, and the home backs dashed up and cleared. Earleetown still kept up the pressure, and their revised was not long in coming. Settle dribbled the ball finely up the wing and passed to Leach, who in turn passed to Hampson. This player dodged the backs and gave the goalkeeper no chance with!,a shot which entered the corner of the net. Tne goalkeeper fell full length in attempting to clear, and he got up clothed about an inch thick in mud, which he was vainly endeavouring t shake off. Encouraged by this success the visitors again invaded .the Banefuriong goal. and Hampson getting possession he struck the upright with a lovely shot. Just before the finish the Ilimfur-</p>
        <p>long forwards broke away, and Chew was called upon, but he proved equal to all emergencies, and brought off two really splendid saves. No more scorinr was done, and when the whistle blew the scores read, Ear'estown 1 Bamfurlong. Earlestown thus pass into the third round of the Cup and on Saturday's form, they stand a good chance of bringing the trophy to Earle ,tawn.All the for- wards played a &amp;forting game, Hampson in particular, his goal being a really fine effort. Tho half backs also played a good game and the defence were never found wanting when they were called upon. Fe pick of the Bamfurlong forwards was tha outside left but most of his efforts vvera spoiled by offside play. ST. HELENS RECS. v. HA.YDOCK.—At City-road, before a large concourse of spectators. The Recs. won the toss. Harrison started in a heavy downpour, and at once rushed down, Wignall shooting over ths line. From the kick off the Rocs got a footing in Haydock quar- ters, Gleve and Greenhall clearing in turn. A corner taken by Jones was disposed of. After some even play Preston passed to Srraith in dan- gerous quarters, the latter beating Lowe with a swift shot. Resuming, Haydock made an incursion to Recrea- tion territory, 13011 averting disaster with a judicious kick. Some neat work by the Recs.' halves transferred the venue to neutral ground, Jack,on and Jonea having hard lines. Grand yassing by the Havdock forwards saw them get a footing in the RPM' quarters, Harrison shooting wide when in a good p ,sition. From a further rush of Haydcck, Hunter cleared in fine style. Later Smith, Jones, and Baines tested the visitors' defence hotly. A c rn-r taken by Davies came to nought. Owing to injury Crooks bad to retire. From a miskick by Greenhall Baines scored. IN, ar the interval Jackson scored for the Recs. After 20 minutes' play in the second half Jones scored for the Recreation. Final :--St. Helens Recs., 4 ; Haydock, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>BLANCHARD'S FEMALE PILLS</head>
        <p>BLANCHARD'S FEMALE PILLS</p>
        <p>_ "NEVER FAIL." Are acknowledged to be the only genuine remedy for Correcting IRREGULARITIES &amp; OBSTRUCTIONS Theyare certain to cure the most obstinate case,and bring about all that is desired after all else fails. Thousands of Testimonials. In boxes, is. lid., 2s. 9d.; strong, 4s. 6d., From all Chemists or sent free from observation by post from I'. BLANCHARD, Fiprest Rd„ Dalstou, London.</p>
        <p>THE PEACE CRUSADE,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>MR. CECIL RHODES'S OPINION</head>
        <p>MR. CECIL RHODES'S OPINION</p>
        <p>BY W. T. STEAD.</p>
        <p>There are two good tests of success in a public, movement—" cash and cranks." If subscriptions are given it is certain that hearts are touched. If a movement is widespread some of the foolish ones of the earth will certainly have their say. Tried by these two tests the Peace Crusade, up to the time of writing, is cerlainly a success. There has been a steady stream of anonymous letters into headquarters at Talbot House during the past week. One of the broadsheets was returned with this written on the top: "To give you my opinion of you, I consider, if you claim to be a Briton, you are a disgrace to your country and deserve to be lynched." ' Happily the subscriptions have come, and with larger now. The "volunteer" idea has caught on, and half-crowns, mainly in postal orders, as sub- trriptions to the Crusade and to the paper and as enlistment for three months, have arrived by every post. These volunteers, once enlisted, have set to work to secure others. One warm-hearted friend enlisted on the Tuesday writes on Wednesday: "I fired two effective shots to-day," and the two eective shots meant. the enrolment of two other volunteers. Another volunteer writes: "Looking back to my childhood, I can only recollect one British war in which I could have engaged with enthusiasm or with a full sense of its rightness and necessity, and that one did not come off! It would have been England against Turkey, two years ago." Of the meetings the report is most encouraging. London is now fairly roused, and more than a dozen meetings are in course of arrangement in the districts which, cities in themselves, are owe- shadowed by the greatness of the Imperial city of which they form part. Hackney or St. Pancras, Islington, Camberwell, or Paddington, by them- selves would each form first-class provincial cities. There can be no question what their response will be at their coming town's meetings. In Paddington, Sir George Fardell, M.P., and Mr. Aird, M.P., the two Conservative members for the district, are keenly interested in the whole matter, and are organising the meeting over which Sir George Fardell is to preside and this is only typical of other places. The Battersea meeting, held after a gale which drenched the streets all day, was not so successful in numbers as had been hoped for, the Town Hall, which holds 3,000, being a little more than half full; but the meeting was repre- sentative of all shades of opinion, as the pro- ceedings shewed, and the feeling throughout was cordial and enthusiastic. When the Pilgrimage of Peace was proposed it was suggested that among the English delegates no better man could be found than Cecil Rhodes. The idea was ridiculed by those who did not know that Mr. Rhodes has always been the opponent of war. With the exception of the blunder of the Raid, he has always preferred to negotiate rather than to fight. Hence, it is not surprising that, on the very day of his arrival, Mr. Rhodes expressed his hearty sympathy with the objects of the Crusade, in which his most intimate friends, Lord Grey and Mr. Ila,wksley, are actively engaged. The practical point that struck the practical mind of Mr. Rhodes was the immense good that could be done with the money that will be saved if the Czar's proposal is accepted. The immediate result would be the saving by England of £l4, 000, 000, which will otherwise be spent in building ships to match the new Russian ironclads, which, if the Peace Conference succeeds, the Emperor will not build: With £1.4, 000,000 what might not be done? "One thing," says Mr. Rhodes, "you could certainly do with less than that sum. With £10,000,000 you could complete the railway from the Cape to Cairo, and still hays P.A. 000.000 to snare." When Mark lwain was in Africa he wrote some- what cruelly about Mr. Rhodes that he hoped, if ever the great African were hanged, heq might be there to hold the rope. But Mark Twain ,is now side by side with Mr. Rhodes in supporting the Czar's proposal. In an amusing autograph, pub- lished this week in the new number of War Against War, he contributes his shaft to the armoury of the Crusade. He says: IN EQUIVALENTS FOR WATERLOO</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MEN. BY MARK TWAIN.</head>
        <p>MEN. BY MARK TWAIN.</p>
        <p>VIENNA, January 9th.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Stead,—Peace by compulsion. That seems a better idea than the other. Peace by per- suasion has a pleasant sound, but I think we should not be able to work it. We should have to tame the human race first, and history seems to shew that that cannot be done. Can't we reduce the armaments little by little—on a pro rata basis—by concert of the Powers? Can't we get four Great Powers to agree to reduce their strength ten per cent. a year, and thrash the others into doing likewise? For, of course, we cannot expect all of the Powers to be in their right minds at one time. It has been tried. We are not going to try to get all of them to go into the scheme peaceably, are we? In that case I must withdraw my influence. Because, for business reasons, I must preserve the outward signs of sanity. Four is enough, if they can be securely harnessed together. They can compel peace, and peace without compulsion would be against nature and not opera- tive. A sliding scale of reduction, of ten per cent. a year, has a sort of plausible look, and lam willing to try that if three other Powers will join. I feel sure that the armaments are now many times greater than necessary for the requirements of either peace or war. Take war-time, for instance. Suppose circumstancez made it necessary for us to fight another Waterloo, and that it would do what it did before—settle a large question and bring peace. I will guess that 400,000 men were on hand at Waterloo—l have forgotten the figures. in five hours they disabled 50,000 men. It took them that tedious long time because the firearms delivered only two or three shots a minute. But we would do the work now as it was done at Omdurman, with shower-guns raining six hundred balls a minute. Four men to a gun—is that the number? A hundred and fifty shots a minute per man. 'I hus a modern soldier is one hundred and forty-nine Waterloo soldiers in one. Thus, also, we can now retain one man out of each one hundred and fifty in service, disband the others, and fight our Waterloos just as effectively as we did eighty-five years ago. We should do the same beneficent job with 2,800 men now that we did with 400,000 then. The allies could take 1,400 of the men, and give Napoleon 1,400, and then whip him. But, instead, what do we see? In war-time, in Germany, Russia, and France, taken together, we find about 3,000,000 men equipped for the field. Each man represents 149 Waterloo men in useful- ness and killing capacity. Altogether they con- stitute about 350,000.000 Waterloo men—and there are not quite that many grown males of the human race now on this planet. Thus we have this insane fact: that whereas those three countries could arm 18,000 men with modern weapons and make them the equals of 3,000,000 men of Napoleon's day, and accomplish with them all necessary war-work, they waste their money and the prosperity-creating forces of their populations in piling together 349,982,000 extra Waterloo equivalents, which they would have no sort of use for if they would only stop drinking, and sit down and cipher a little. Perpetual peace we cannot have, on any terms, I suppose, but I hope we can gradually reduce the war strength of Europe till we get it down to what it ought to be-20,000 men, properly armed. Then we can have all the peace that is worth while, and when we want a war anybody can afford it. MAIM TWAIN.</p>
        <p>Among the others who have placed their pens and pencils at the disposal of the Crusade are Mr. Bjsrnson, the famous Norwegian novelist, Pere Hyacinthe, the illustrious French preacher, Mrs. L. T. Meade, Mr. Grant Allen, Sarah Grand, Mr. Felix Moscheles, Mr. Silas K. Hocking, Mr. F. Carruthers Gould, Sir Lewis Morris, Max Nordau, and many other eminent men of letters. "Ouida," the novelist, has written declaring that the moment is most inopportune for a Peace Crusade, because "England is given over to a roaring, ramping war lust." To whom Madame Novikoff wittily replies that it is the first time she ever heard that when the conflagration breaks out it is inopportune to call out the fire brigade.</p>
        <p>Th.E WORLD'S GREATESE HYPNOTIST</p>
        <p>PROFESSOR T. A. KENNEDY.</p>
        <p>"Being one of the greai; number that has benefited thrcugh that marvellous remedy—‘Warner's Safe Cure' —I conscientiousty think it my duty to testify to the benefit derived from its use. I have only used it for liver troubles (symptoms—pain 3 in the back of sh oulder3, etc.), complaintl which it soon rid me of, and which I have never beenrroubled with since using the same. I r'r, honestly recommend this mecrcine to anyone trots )led with complaints of the liver." PROF. T. A. KENNEDY, Hypnotist.</p>
        <p>les.</p>
        <p>THE MANX STEAMER DISASTER. RECOVE ItY OF ONE OF THE BODIES..</p>
        <p>A fishing boat brought into Fleetwood or Tucaday morning the body of a well-dressed man, evidently one of the perions who were washed from the steander Fenella durinz the rdugh paisoge to the Isle of Man ie- cently. The features were almost eaten away, and past recognition, but a subsequent examination of the papers and pocket diary on the body, afflrdcd convincing proof that it was the body of William F Quayle, school.. master, of 49, noon-street, Tootetta, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. JAMES F. wairg—MEMORIAL. SERVICE. On Sunday evening a set. ?ice in memory of the late Mr J,F. White, the VVarriugton commercial traveller, who was washed overboard from the Fenella whilst crossing from Liverpool to the Fite of Man during the wads held, at the Padgate storm on the 2nd January, Wesleyan Chapel, whicn was well filled to its utmost capacity. The chapel was draped in black. The de- ceased 'had been a regular attendant at the sanctuary during the greater part of his life and fulfilled the duties of secretary of the Sunday School for the past ten years. Amongst those present were many who had been associated with the deceased gentleman in business, those connected with the Sunday school, an several members of the Warrington Y.M.C.A., of which Mr. White was an active member. The pulpit was occupied by the Rev H G Roberts, who preached from the words " For our citizenship is ia. heaven ;ye are no more strangers and sojourners, but ye are fellow citizens With the saints." Having spoken of our citizenship of heavee' the rev, gentleman said that the church had sustained a great loss by the sud- den and mysterious removal of tmir beloved friend and brother. Mr. White. Be was sure they would under-- stand him when be said that it was not an easy task for him to give a fair and fdithful representa- tion of his character. He only knew him slightly. He had two cr three conversations with Mr. White, and they were conversations relat- ing to the work being carried on there. Many of them knew better than he tth.e sp, aker) did how faith- .ful were the services he rendered to that Sunday school and church. From his knowledge of him he(the preacher), knew as a certainty that be would not wish him to say anything extravagantly in his praise. He, however,felt that justice required on such an occaaion that an excep•- tionat and sufficient estimate should be made of his real merit and character. Some of them knew by a kind of ' unquestioning intuition that he was one of the children of light, and he was sere that :he light of that church. was distinctly poorer by his death. He should not refer- to the awful soddenness of his removal. It Was mysteri- ously strange that within 24 hours of being in that sanc- tuary on the Sunday evening, listening to the Rev. W. Potts. their superintendent minister, preach on "What is life ?"he hould be taken into the p:esencs of his God. They must, however, bow to God's will. Mr. White was the secretary of their Sunday school for ten years, and a, little over ten years ago be gave hisheart to God in Bold Street Chapel during the Rev. Thomas Waugh's mission. They knew the interebt he tcok in that place. That could be gathered from the •report he had prepared of the past par's work. He had had converoations with gentlerk.en who knew Mr. White well.He felt it to be his duty under the circumstances to make those inquiries. He had seen Mr. Jenkins, in whose office Mr. White was for sir years, and he felt his loss ..freatly. Ho had also seen the directors of the company Mr. White re- presented, several of whem he noticed amongst the con- gregation. They had received lt•ers from merchants and business men from all part fon whom Mr. White had called in the course of his business. From a perusal of those letters he found that he was singularly pure minded, and scrupu!ously observant of the minutest re- quirements of commercial ethics. His kind disposition, his intogrity of purpose, devotion to duty, his affection- ate heart, his mooest humility won for him the esteem, and respect of his numerous friends. Into the sacred privacy of his home life it would. be something like an impertinence to intrude, but he might be permitted te. say that those relationships were ideal relationships. Tbat night in imagination they laid aside what was mortal of him, but in that great truth of the text they- dared to look calmly into the face et death. Let them consider for a moment the holy comfort of that truth. Each man and woman in this world sooner or later in one form or another was brought into deep waters. The light went out. The wild stores brake and beat upon the house of life; love lay bleeding in the dust, and words of human comfort sounded almost like sacrilege. Fee, were spared the bitter.- nese and terror of that dread experience. There was this comfort: God assured them it was not night for in the very darkest hour, in the profeuadest gloom, in the very heart of their unspeakable grief there was e great warm living light. The water should not over.. whe?in the hearts that put tack trust in Him. At the close of the service Miss J. Bennett (the organist) played the Dad March in" Saul," the cone- gregation standing.</p>
