      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Mar.</head>
        <p>Mar.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH 'BUSES.</p>
        <p>Sir—At last they are coining," the Penny 'Bus3s,'• —"Let em all come." Oh, what joy there will be in the Heathen Settlement beyond the waters. light I sug:zest as a further improvement to the owners, drivers, and all concerned that less stop- ping places be adopted on the journey. If only for the sake of the poor animals let us have this. Now my dear, kind ladies, remember this, also that the 'buses you should eadeavour no to travel by (with parcels, "bless nose parcels")are those ab)ut the times when poor man is going to and fro from business. We know you like them the bast of all the 'buses but do give us a chance,—Yiurs hop- i gly, "A HEATHEN."</p>
        <p>THE NEN WARRINGTON WATER SCHEME. Sir,—There are some cleawbacks to this scheme, as unfolded at the town's meeting last week, to which it might not be amiss to direct some atten- tion. In the first place, one would.have thought that an undertaking of such importance and so expen- sive stNould have had the earnest and prolonged consideration of the whole of the Town Council. This, however, was not the case. We learn on the authority of Mr. Alderman Sutton, one of the oldest and moss reliable members of the Council, that he was " perfectly staggered" when he read the an- ' nouncement of it in the local newspapers. Nor was he alone in his emotion, for Ceuncillors Sme-harst and Bennett seem to have been similarly affllcted. But in fairness I must admit that the conversion of these thee gentlemen was sudden and thorough. A few well directed volleys of figures rammed home by some of the characteristic eloqeence of one or two of their fellow coanciilors sufficed to disarm their intended antagonism, and to turn them into gushing advocates of the scheme. Again, some of the figures teat wee ad lueed in support of the undertaking are really apocryphal. bet it be granted at once that Mr. Deals is a clever man in as much as he appears to be able to fill two or three very important posts under the Cerpora- tion simialtaneously. But when ha laid aside his proper vocations and donned the mantle of the Seer fora time he assumed a positien for which he is not qualified. It is only given to the divine oracle to forecast the future, and Mr. Dees not being an.au- thority of that kind, I must regard his predictions as to the population and water supply of Warring- ton twenty years hence as pure speculation. Is it wise, is it just then to go on increasing the burdens of the town by schemes for the future of which we know absolutely nothing. Surely the true and righteous policy would be to quietly and economi- cally provide for our own wants as they are deve- loped,and leave after generations to act in a similar way. Then, in the third place, the .Z10)000 or so pro- posed to be expended ia three, four or five years will have to be borrowed al, an interest of three to four per cent per annum. And what provision Is there for the payment of such interest without an increase to the rates? Mr. Alderman Sutton stated that " the Borough Treasurer lead assured them that the money could be raised by mortgaging the surplus profits of the (water) undertaking, without increasing any rates at all." And the burden of Mr. Councillor Shaw Green's speech was to the same effect. But, strange to say, no figures were pro- duced to confirm the statement. No one doubts the veracity of the Borough Treasurer and Mr. Coun- cillor Shaw Green but their mere ipse dixit is hardly sufficient in a case of this kind. The ratepayers were entitled to and should have demanded, copies of the profit and loss account of the water depart- ment that they might see what the annual profits were and how they were applied. If they are used in relieving the pr sent rates their impounding to pay interest on new loans will inevitably lead to an advanced taxation.—Yours truly, lONA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>THE: WARRINGTON CORPORA.TION 7BILL</head>
        <p>THE: WARRINGTON CORPORA.TION 7BILL</p>
        <p>_ Sir,—Undoubtedly the proposals of the Warring- ton Corporation contained in their suggSsted Bill to Parliament, came as a shock to most Of the rate- payers of the tJwa. Howaver, I think what was disclosed at the town's meeting would tend to soothe most of them, for the proposals when ex- plained were not s 3 alarming as they first appeared to be.</p>
        <p>The water schemes were, as most are aware, the chief features of the Bill, and it was evident from what was st tted, that it was quite time the CON poration took in hand the important question of water for the town. Personally, as a ratepayer, and one who was present at the meeting,.l was quite satisfied with the explanations given, that the schemes propounded would notilauneh.the town in an unknown quantity of rates—in fact one mem- ber of the Council(and an able one to boot)was san- guine enough to assert,that they would not increase the rates one iota. Although not quite sharing this sanguine view, yet I was satisfied that it was essential for the future welfare of the town,iwhich is the main reason that the schemes should be carried out at an early date. Therefore the Bill as a whole should be presented to Parliament. There were two features at the meeting which particularly impressed me in its favor. (Ist) That the Council as a body were practically unanimous in favor of the Bill. (2nd) That the schemes for obtaining a greater quantity of water from Win- wick for domestic purposes, and for Vie construc- tion of additional reservoirs near Appleton for stor- ing water for manufacturing purposes, were the best that the Council could as business men advise the town to adopt. With regard to the alternative schemes of taking water from the Vyrnwy pipes Welch supply Liver- pool, and also the one advocating a supply of water from a colliery near "'resew, the arguments urged against them, were to my mind conclusive against entering into such schemes. With reference to taking water from the Vyrnwy pipes it was clearly demonstrated: (Ist) That the scheme would involve a heavier cost to the town than the one proposed. (2nd) That the conditions imposed as regards limiting supply rendered such a scheme impracticable and one that no business man would advise the town to adopt. With regard to taking water from the Colliery. This, it was disclosed, could only be of a tempor- ary nature, and therefore would necessitate in the near future a further scheme to be enlarged upon. Consequently it was not serion.ly debated. As the Mayor was good enougn to invite sugges- tions in connection with the matter, I for one ac- cept the invitation thrown out. As is plainly to be seen the schemes proposed will involve the town in a large expenditure of money, 1100,000 being named as the sum that would he spent in the course of the next three or four years. Tide being so, it will be allowed that great care will need to be exercised in seeing that this amount is judiciously and wisely spent. Without casting any reflection upon any present member of the Council, it appears to me that if the town could see its way, within a reasonable period and at the proper time to persuade and elect several well.known business men, and men of moral fibre, to enter the Council Chamber and serve them it would, I believe, create a great feeling of security. I suggest two names, viz. Mr. Frederick Monks sen. and Mr. A. J. Jolley,the latter of whom is rendering such able services on the Board of Guardians. The first named has already served and I think if he can only be persuaded to enter again at the present, juncture the town would be pleased. Both men I believe,are not liable to be unduly swaye4 by poli- tical feeling and would tend to strengthen in more ways than one, the morale of the Council. Let me say in conclusion, that the Mayor's con- duct at the meeting was as it should be, admirable, as he did not hesitate to give both advocates for, and opponents to the Bill a fair hearing.—Yours faithfully, E. ENGLAND. iloss House, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>WHY SUFFER ONE momEsr tro.n torturing. dB figuring skin hurnours,when a warm bath with CIITICURA SOAP and a single anointing with CIITICURA, the great akin cure, will afford instant relief, permit rest and sleep, and point to a speedy, permanent, and economical cure, when the best physicians and all other remsdies fail. No preparations ever compounded in the hi3tory of medicine have performed the wonderful cures daily made by CUTICURA remedies. They are, in truth, the greatest skin cures, blood purifiers, and humour reme- dies of modern times, and appeal to those who have suf- fcred long and hopelesily, with irresistible force.</p>
        <p>CITIIARTICUM PILLS. digestive, correc- tive, and agreeably aperient, 91d., 130.—0 f all Clumiate.—Advt.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 27,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 27,</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTON CONGREGATIONAL DEBITING SOCIETY The annual meeting of this society was held on Monday evening in the Congregational schoolrohm, Newton. Dr, Lathom presided and there was a fair attendance. The subject introduced by Mr. F. Weeks was "Nationalisation on land," and a veiy good paper was submitted. Mr. W. Sephton took the negative view and an interesting discussion followed in which many of the members took part, the evening proving most profitable. IRISH NATIONAL LEAGUER BILL AT EARLESIOWN. This annual ball was held in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Wednesday evening. The hall was very nicely decorated for the occasion by Mr. E. T. Evans, and a number of plants lent by Mr. Randall were arranged on the platform. There were about 60 couples present, the M.C.'s being Messrs. T. Burke, J. Loftug, and M. Fleming. The music was supplied by Mr. Peplow's band, and the catering was carried oat by Mr. W. Beatty. The ball was opened by Mr. and Mrs. J. H. Forster, and dancing was indulged in until early on Thursday morning. APPOINTMENT OF A SCHOOL ATTENDANCE OFFICER</p>
        <p>AT EARLESTOWN. At the ordinary monthly meeting of the Sehool Attendance Committee held at the Town Hall, Earlestown on Mon lay, Mr. 'C. Martindale was ap- pointed school attendance officer for the district of Newtonsin-Makerfield vice Mr. John Ball, receatly deceesed,at a salary of £lOO per annum,tee appoint- ment to date as from the Ist of January 1899 Dur- ing the meeting, the Ctiairman and several members of the Committee made co nments upon the satis- factory manner in which Mr. Manindale has per- formed his duties in the past. EARLBSTOWN AND DISTEacT PERFECT THEIFr BUILDING SOCIETY. A well attended meeting of the members of the above soziety was held in the District School on Thursday evening last. Mr. George Clarke pre-iding. After the minutes of the previous meeting had been read a ballot for the next appropriation took place, the result being that No. 203, was drawn, the for- tunate member being Mr. G. S. Dale, of 17, Rath- bane-street, Earlestown, who will be entitled to the use of £2OO free of interest for the next 16 years and 8 months. Thanks to the Sscretary,Chairman, &amp;c., closed a very interesting meeting. 1.0.G.T. HOPD OF EA.ELESTOWN LoDGE. The usual meeting was held on Monday, presided over by Bro. T. Cutler, C.T. Two candidates were admitted. Bro. E. Newman, read the digest of the Annual Juvenile District Council to be held at Liver- pool on the 28th inst. A visiting brother(Robinson) from Liverpool Onward Lodge,who had been out to Japan, China, and the Straits Settlement, also tendered greetings fr nu the Star of the BLit, and number one and two of the Naval District, gong Song, North West Yorkshire Regiment Lodge. Bro. Cliff from Atherten also addressed the meeting and recited, two others being present from Atherton. Thanks were teniered to the visitors at the close. \ POLICE CASES. At the Newton Town Hall on Saturday morning before Mr. T. J. Gillespie, Mary Molloy. of South- port, was charged with soliciting at Newton on Friday night, and in default of paying a fine of 5s and costs was sent t 3 gaol for 14 days.—P.C.Bnson supported the charge.—Agnes Insley of Clieetharn Hill for being drunk and disorderly was fined 2s 61 and costs, or seven days in default. P.C. Brindle proved the case. Agnes Baxter of Poolstock on the information of P.C. Clarke was fined 53 and costs for soliciting, and was seat to gaol for 14 days in default.—On Monday morning before Mr. Gillespie and Mr. Barron, Sarah Tartle of Wiaantwas charged with soliciting on the intormatioa of P. C. Gather- c3le, and sent to gaol for a month with hard labour. —Mary Sandford of St. Helens was sent to gaol for 14 days with hard labour, for a similar offence, P.C. Gathercole also proving the case. Hugh Caine Legh-street, Earlestown, for being drunk and dis- orderly was fined 103 and costs. James Hall,Athol- street, Newton, was fined 2s 6d and costs on the information of P.C. Clarke for being drunk and dis- orderly. William Henry Griffiths of St. Helens, for being drunk and disorderly and with assaulting the police, was fined 53 and casts for being drunk and lOs and clqts for the assault. The. charge was sustained by P.C. Benson. INDUCTION OF THE VICAR OF ST. PETER'S, INEwTON. On Wednesday evening the Rev. James Ryder was inducted to the vicarage of St. Peter's, Newton, by the Rev. F. J. Madden, Archdeacon of Warring- ton, in the presence of a numerous congregation The service was preceded by evening prayer, after which the Archdeacon and the Vicar elect, preceled by the wardens, proceeded to the main door of the church aril going outside the Arc odeacme lecked the door. The Archdeacon then taking the hand of the Rev. J. Ryder, laid it on the key iu the door, and said, "By virtue of thii mandate from the Lord Bishop of toe Dioceses, I in -tint you intn the real, actual and corporeal possession of the Vtcarage of St. Peter, Neartol.in-Makerfield, in all its fruits, members and appurtenances." Leaving the Arch- demon out,ide, the new incumb-mr, entered ties church and t3iled the bell, after which he went out again and accompanied by the Ar ctidealon ant pre- ceded by the churehwerdens and choir, re-eaterel the church, and procee led to the Lord's table. After the usual prayers the Archdeacon pronounced the usual blessing, an I afterwar ls taking the Incum- bent by the hand, placed him in a seat on the south side of the Holy Table. After singing a hymn, the Archdeacen preached al instructive sermon from tee songs of S )lemon. 4t ciapter 16 verse "Wake oh North wind atri come down south, blow upon my garden that the spice thereof may I flow out."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE MARRIAGE OF Miss G GAMBLE.</p>
        <p>The mtrriage of Mke Gram Gtmbte. younze .t daughter of Sir DLvid Gamble, Bart., C. 8.. with Mr. Mtrtin J. Hammitt, of Bay City, Michigan, U.S.A., and formerly of 31.• Helens, hat b fi.xe.,l to taka place at S. Helens PArieh Chur3h oi Siturclay, February 11th. It is understood th it the ceremony will be performed by the Rev. A.rnur Gamble, vicar of Bradborne, brother of dal bride, and the Rev. Ctn )ri Willink, vicar of St. Helens. Tne service will be fully choral.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>CHILD BURNT TO DEATH.</head>
        <p>CHILD BURNT TO DEATH.</p>
        <p>Henry Harefsoa two-year-old soo of JosepY H trri- son aniironworkersof 94,Friar-street,St. .lelerts,,iied about `six o'clock on Monday morning as a result of being severely burnt. It appears that about four o'clock on the morning of Wednesday las, :he father got up to go to his work, and the deceasul and Mrs Elarrison got up. Mrs Harrison then left the child in the parlour, where there was a fire.. Immediat Ay after she heard sereanu, and on go;ngiato the room found the deceased in flames. She wrapped nim in a shawl and roll hira along the floor' and extingu.sh-ld the flinals. De. Unsvurch wai vntfor and attended up to his d-ata Delelsed wai badly burnt about the lower part of the bodv.—A.,l in- quest was held on Wednesd sy, the ver Act being one of accidental death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>DEA.TH OF MR. It. B. MATTHEWS.</head>
        <p>DEA.TH OF MR. It. B. MATTHEWS.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning at an early hour the death took place of Mr. Robert Bond Matthews,at his resi- dence, The Mount, Penketh. The deceased,who W 2,9 ab3ut 50 years of age, was a man of fine educatitm and physique, and though of rather reserved de- meanour, was justly popular both in Penketh and Warrington. His death was somewhat sudden though he had been in declining health since August last, his professional attendant being Dr. Murray. The deceased was born in South Wales, and was educated at Hereford School, aud King's College, London; and for many years followed the profession of -a civil engineer and contractor until he retired from business, taking up his residence in Warrington some years ago. It was under his direction that the Cheshire Lines Railway from Liverpool to Widnes—or a part of it was constructed,and he was also concerned in the construction of the Macclesfield and Bollington line and the Widnes dock. Mr. Matthews was well- known as a pr3minent member of the Conservative party and an active member of the ar- rington Conservative Club. He was also president of the Penketh Conservative Club. In connection with Colonel Pilkington's recent election to the Newton Division, Mr. Matthews ad- dressed meetings at Sankey and Warrington, and at the latter place he spoke upon the new vaccina- tion act, and asked Colonel Pilkington to endeavour to get it modified. He OA an active interest in public matters, and was a few years ago a candi- date for the representation of St. Austin's Ward in the Warrington Town Council, but be suffered de- feat. He at one time represented Little Sankey og the Warrington and District Highway Board, and was chairman of the now defunct Little Sankey Parish Council. At the time of his death he was vice-chairman of the Penketh Parish Council and one of the overseers for the village. In many ways the deceased has rendered most useful public ser- vices.</p>
        <p>When asking for Cacoa, insist on having Csneuars.—sold only irPackets and Tins as other Cocoas are often BOW f- pted for the sake of extra proftt,--Advt (1)</p>
