        <p>The,Chairman said a letter had been receive] from Mr. R. Stone, who 61131111 have presided, in which he said he was very sorri that an engagement prlventod him at- tending the annual meeting that nignt. H4d it seen possible he should have liked to be present to refer to the sad death of their poor old friend Mr. John Ball,wh3 as a member of the Newt ra Cricket Club, and an en- thusiastic cricketer hal done so much in days gon3 by t) mike the club's nam3 known in the county. Thl Chairman said ho was sure thy would all sip. port th) sesititnents Mr. Stone expressed in his letter touching their old friend Mr. JC0,13 Bill, and h 3 ttiouglal they would be in order in moving a vote expressive of their feelings in thq mitter. His experience of him was no much more than 20 years,and he was sure there were older friends of his in the room.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Crouchley moved that a vote of coviolence be sent to Mr. Ball's fatuity. Tney could not do more than tell them that they were very sorry for what had hap- pened. Tna Chairman said 1351 was infornael that the Com- mitt te of the club had already ptssed such a resolution as that proposed to them but as tiat was a prier tl meeting he thought the eubj3ct shoild be dealt with a 3 Mr. Cronellay proposal. Mr. E. Fairclougn said he was no doubt oae of the oldest crictleters who had played crick3t with Mr. Ball and he sec mded the resolution. Tao notion was put and carrie I. THE BALANCE SHEET.</p>
