        <p>was then proceeded with. The ChairmAn sail that they had lo3t one of the p ttr ms (Lord N, w'e t) through death. z. vote of condohmce htel Len RA at on be'nalf of the club to the relatives of tne late Lord Newton,and te following acknnwleiglinent had been recqivel. " Lyme -cernber 31t. Tn.' 133 wager Ludy Newton is must gr dieted for tie retolation passed by the Newton Cricket Club, nl begs Hr. Gee to convey her b3st thanks to the members."</p>
        <p>'me election of patroos was then carried out, the nano)o Colonel Richard. Pilkington, M.P., b ling a idid to tha list. Mr. Robert Stone was re-elected president. On the question ot vic ',presidents, the Chairman said he had to refer with regret to the death of one of their vice pr,sidents, Mr. R3beit Stuart. A resolution of condo- lence had been passed and forwarded to the relatives of toe late Mr. S urt, and a suitable a.ek so w«.d ;se flint hai been received.-01 the motion of Mr. Sankey, the whole of the vice-nreiiie its, except Mr. Stuart, were re-elested.—Mr. W. H. Elindley was appointed treasurer on the m )tioa of Mr. B rimer, secindei by Mr. Mayor. Mr. W. G,e WAS re•appointedsecretary. Mc. T. Mayor and Mr. F. Wagg were elected auditors. Mr. W. H. Hindley an Mr. R. Chaniley were appointed Ist team captains, and lAr J H Pen try and Mr W Wilkinson 21d tains. As bowling eantains, Messrs Bodmer, T Fair- clougl, A llico3y and J McCormick were appointed. Mr. Hinitey moved that the election of tennis captains be left tti toe t-nnis members. Mr. Parkei seconcled,and it was car.ied.</p>
