        <p>The Chairman said tha-, the Preside Mr. K. Stole hal suggested that ia the selection of the General Committee Slnal c onsideratinn should be givel to the Earlestown in,rnbers if po3sible. He pointed out that there were no Earlestown members upon the committee and in ordor to make the c marnittee as representative as possible, he su.zgested that one or two be elected. Mr. Fairclough said that the old 09111 nittee were in touch with the alterations which were going on in the field, and if they appointed a lot of now members the whole thing would have t be gone over again. _ _ _</p>
        <p>• Mr. Bodin3r said there was a sub-annamittee who had had the alterations in hand, and Mr. Berry was one of the most ac-;ive inemb?rs in that sub-committee: He uuderstood h 3 was not voinz to s:and as tennis captain but if he became a tennis captain ho would b 3 a mem- ber .sf the committee, but if he was not elected a ten- nis caltain, he would not be a m3mb3r of the crknamit. tee. and he proposed that he be added to the General Committee. A lengthy discussion afterwards took place as to the composition of the committee, ant several propositions and amendments were made, finally it was decided to take nocainitions, and vote upcm them seriatim. The following ware eleatEd : Messrs Beer;, J Crouch- ley, E Fsirclouzh, H Hindley. J Liptrott, W H Parkes, J Sankey. El Sweetleve, W R Taker. and W Sp3akm 4n.</p>
