      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SAD SUICIDE AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SAD SUICIDE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse (coroner) hell an inquest on Wed- nesday evening at the Police Parade-room on the body of Mary Ann Syre, (42) wife of Henry Syre, puddler, re- siding at 14, Howard-etreet,Froghall-lane, and whe was found drowned early on Tuesday morning near Butter- milk Bridge. Henry Syre, husbaed of the deceased, said she had been suffering from pains in the head for about a fort- bieht and had been attended by Dr. Liston. During that time her mind had been rambling, but some member of the family had always been looking after her. Witness was in the kitchen with her from 5 o'clock on Sunday until 12.30 on Monday m)rning when he went to sleep. He awoke at a qaarter to two o'clock and found his wife gone. She would have to unbolt the dooreto get out. He next saw her dead at the mortuary.— By the Coroner: She had got a drop of beer on Newt Year's Day and that had started her head. She had noe touched for a week previous to her death.—By ths Chief Constable: Sne said on Sunday that someone was coming to take her away but she would not go. He thought it was a delusion.—By a Juror: She had no delusions before taking the drink. P.C. William Smith (60) raid that at a quarter past one on Moeslay morning he was on duty in Selby-Btreet, and he ad screams as if someone was in trouble, but he could not tell where the sound came from. He searched the backs of the houses and then entered a pit, near Buttermilk Bridge, which proved to be deep. He eventually got the body out, the deceased lying on her stomach. From where he found the body, she would have had to wade from shallow water. He could sot reach the body owing to the pit being so slippery, but begot the assistance of two men, and with a brick tied to a clothes line, he dragged the body within reach, the prop itself being rather short. If it had not been for the assistance of the two men he would not have been able to get the body to the banks. —By a juror: It would be about ten minutes after he heard the screams when ho found the body. He was five minutes before he got to the pit, as he could not tell where the sound came from owing to the wind.— The jury returned a verdict of " suicide whilst tempora- rily insane " and commended the officer for his conduct though he was unfortunately unsuccessful in arriving in time to save the deceased's life.</p>
