      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>r</p>
        <p>NOTE 2 ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOFBALL.</p>
        <p>Warringtoa's in and out form was again exemplified on Saturlay, when they went to Tyldesley and defeated tie" Boigers "on their,own heath. The performance was a most creditable one, for though Tyldesley are in low water this year they are a sturdy, powerful lot. On Saturday the weather was in their favour too, fora we day and a muidy ground suit a heavy team much bettev than a light one. The Warrington lads Were on their mettle however, and being encouraged by thAr recent victory at Wlgan, they played with a certain amount of cmfidence. Two tries to nil was the score at , the close, the two Warringtoa half back; being cielited ; with the tries.</p>
        <p>A worse dtv for Rugby football than Saturday it would be difficult to imagine. The whole of the morning it rained, but as it hat rain2d for 60 consecutive days previously nobody was much surprised, and sotni even forgot to vituparate. The moisture continned to des- cend daring a good portion of the afternoon, and those who are a-.all acqutintei th th 3 Tyliesley ground will have some id ea or the c maitions which obtainel then. Mad and water raised to the consistency of gum did not deter the two teams from trying thair strangth how- eier. What cared thy about the wet. After the first fall it wt s all so muca fun and could be endured light he trtfdtv, except, of course, when a FO tiol of the one- time turf was plastered over an optic. Teen, of a truth there was Elgals little difficulty in seeing the joke.</p>
        <p>A notable absentee from tile ranks of the hom3 side was T. Kirk Ba 4le', F.14q.,t ie alleged Cu lob Irland county threequarter. Mr Bagley hs.s played fr fyldesley—hA op.rattd against Oldham a Week or two azo and saw 33 points piled on in an hour and a half. The Rpntacto rnly have so saddened him that he has retired fr im Northern Ilaion foothill: Whether or no he was mi-ising last Saturday. I fancy two or three of the Warrington hacks were disappointed at no% having the opnortuaity of see- in he was made of. Personally I felt aggrieve 1- Men with such aristocratic nil/133 ar3 somewhat nf a rarity on the football field, and espz2cialiy on Ih3 Tyld3s- ley football field.</p>
        <p>The match its.,lf was n)t of a very entertaining de- scription. S;imtifia football was c tst to the winds,and in its place was tried a soft of Sockier-cum-Rugger com- pound which answered very well. Warriniton were a good bit the smarter all through, but they had great diffi ty in scoring. The first try was a gift. George Sew, the ex-Broughtonian, who has crossed the War- rington line on many occasions in the past, was officiating in the position of full back when he failed to catch the greasy ball and the next moment Ashairst had it over the line. Nlt- withstanding tie wetness ef their jerseys, the Warring- ton players felt and looked exceedingly joyful. Tne second half was but a repetition of the first, though the Warrington wings were a little more conspicuous. b )th trying hard to =c)reaad failing by a trifle. Dockworti, though t” could not cross tne line himself, was instru- m,nt sl in obtaining another three-point er for Warring- ton. Berry tackled him near the line after a good run, hut failed to prove,nt a clever pass CO Bate, who scored far out. Hostilities ceased shortly after and Warrington returned home two points better off.</p>
        <p>Little caa be said aboir, the doings of the players in- dividual(y. Du*worth made a good impression, and juitifi ,d his selection for the match at Sto3kp ,rt. At half bAck Warrinzton were greatly superior to their opeoaeres, while forward and full-b Lck they easily held t ieir own. Hallam wi3 par-hularly steady. Tyldesley's one fam)us forw Ire's were in poor form, and consiier- lag their strength they did little indeed.</p>
        <p>The most eensational rrsult on Siturdav was' the de- fora oC the Rocndate Elornets by Morecamhe who actu- ally acco nplished thi; feat at Rochdale. It is true that the Hornets were not playinly, on their own gr mad, which was inundated, but that is really no excuse. The wooden-sp,soniits play.° 1 up determinedly, and when they got a couple of chances to score they took them. I hive not heard of any public thanksgiving and rejoic- ings at Morecambe, but I feel pretty sure there were same.</p>
        <p>01 lham and Leigh played a tight game at Water- shed iinv, a place wtlich on Saturd-ey did not belie its name. A.fter a ftne fight by the visitors, the Oldham men ware declared winners by a try to nothing. It was a distinctly creditable performance on the part of Leigh. Runcorn made short work of Stockport at the port, and won by thirteen pointi t none. Salford entertained W,does at New Barns, and evangel themselves for the defeat suet deed earlier in the season. They won by seven point ; to three, and thus retained their lead at the heed of the table. Bronghton Rengart were unable to play Wigan owing to the condition of the latter club's ground.</p>
        <p>Toe positions of th) clubs in the Lancashire Sonior Competition and the results of ma!,zh!s pllyed up to date, are as follows : Von_ Against. _</p>
        <p>.• P. W. L. O. G. T. P. G. T. P P. Salford 2t...16... 3... 2 19...46-176...19 14— 80...34 Oldham 19...15... 4... 0...24 76-276...-9 10— 48...30 Broughton Rang 19...15... 4... 0 32-52-221. 9 13— 57...30 Widnes 18...12...14... 2 ...3 30-138_25 14— 7/...26 Leigh 2L..13... 8... 0...29 23-136-11 26-100-26 *Swinton... ...... 13...i2... 4... 2...23 41-169 ,10 8— 41-24 Runcorn ...... 18... 9... 7... 2..19 23-107-13 2?. 92..21 Warringto.u. 18... 9.. 8... 1...10 25 95...13 27-107-19 St. Helens lB. 7. 8 3...20 23-109...13 30-116...17 Rochdale Hornetsl9... 5...11... 3...16 12— 68..26 43-181...13 Tyldesley 21.. 2..15.. 4...11 14— 64...23 56-214... 8 Wigan 19.. 3...15... 1...14 11— 61...29 36-166... 7 Stockport 18.. 2...15... 1...12 14— 66...37 53-233... 5 Morecambe l9. 2...16... 1... 4 9 35...29 50-238... 5 *Swinton have had two pJiats delucted for breach of professional rules.</p>
        <p>• Self praise is said t) be no recommendation so I will nos assirt that the Warrington Committee have brought Morrism back frqm Dilton-in-Farness as a result of the hint I give thinn a few,weeks ago. Initead I will join in the general joyfulness that he has coins back to the fold. Morrison is a good forward, and proved exceed- ingly useful last season. Re is already working in toWn and has been selected to play with the "A" team to-day, if footfall is possible.</p>
        <p>The '• A" team had an easy time of it with Tvldesl.y "A," who are the wooden spoonists of the "A" team League. They showed little knowledge of fo3tball and were defeated by 17 points to nil. But for the terrible state of th 3 ground the score would have been very much larger, passing being almost impo3eible. A fea- ture of the'match was 1131 croft's goal kicking.. Out of four attempts he landed four Ivautiful goals. If he could only do the same thing when playing for the first tean3 he would be an invaluable acquisition. The injury to his knee still prevents him playing much, and he has to und3rgo special treatment: Fish has !lot qu ts recovered from his damaged shoulder, but will be ready again very shortly.</p>
