        <p>„ P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P SAlford 16...13... 2... 1... 28 58...216 3 12... 42...27 Warrington 13...11... 1... 1... 13 29...113... 6 6... 30...23 Oldham 12 9.. 3... 0... 21 42...168 q 8... 42...18 Broughton Rangl2... 7... 4... 1... 9 36...136...10 17... 71...15 Widnes l2. 7 4.. 1. 18 33...135... 4 8... 35...15 Swinton 1? 6 4. 2. 17 31...127... 7 21... 77..44 Rochdale H'ts —15... 6... 9... 0... 15 23... 99...23 45...181...12 Stockport 13.. 5.. 8. 0.. 7 19,.. 71... 7 28...104...10 Runcorn N.. 3... 7... 2... 5 16... 58...15 56...138... 8 Leigh Tyldeslev l4. 0..14.. 0. 4 7... 29...33 61...249... 0</p>
        <p>r TEA MEETINGS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ST. ANN'S</p>
        <p>The annual congregational gathering of St. Ann's was held in the sch )oiroom 3, Dannett-strest, on Monday evening. The proceedings were 7.ommenced with an excellent tea, to which about 150 sat down. Tables were presided over by the following :—Miss Davies, Mrs. Byrne, Mrs. Bunch, Mrs. Baker, Miss RiVey, Miss M. Rigbev, Miss R. Rigby, Misses Johnstone. Mrs. Glover, Mrs. Groands, Mrs. Bisselbrooke, Mrs. Warner, Mrs. Owen, and Ars. Hankinsen. A concert was after wards held presided over by the Vicar (Rev. J. Davies) and there were also present 11-w. B. J. Byrne and Rev. A. Hurst. Daring an interval in the wogramme the Vicar cordially thankei thos3 who had assisted or given towards the proceedings. - Toe following programme was excellently rendered during the evening t—Violin solo, '6 Home, sweet Home," Master 0. Ashton ; song, " R thin Adair," Miss Ada Hewitt ; s,n. " Bonny Mary of Argyle," Mr. W. H. Ireland ; trio," Three little maids," MisS3s Rigbev, Bate and Hewitt ; song, "Anchored," Mr. F. Taylor ; tableau, 66 The Nations," Miss Rigbey's party ; gle, "Little B 37 Blue," Cho choir; tableau, " The Dirty Girl," Miss Rigbey's party ; violin solo," Der Kleine Gondolier," Master Egerton ; song, "Irish Potheon." Mr. W. H. Ireland ; tableau, " The Seasons," Miss Rigbey's party ; song, " The Diver," Mr. F. Taylor ; song, "The Cricket ou the Hearth," Miss Hewitt; finale, "Grand Tableaux," Miss Rigbey's party'. The concert conclud3d with the singing of the National Anthem. Mrs. Dobson and Mr. E. A. Padntore, made efficient accompanists. EMMANUEL REFORMED CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Oa Taeeday evening at the seeo3lroorn over Arpley Station, the annual congreeatioaal tea party and eoctal gathering of this church was held. There were about one hundred present at the tea, the tables being presided over by tie following :—Mesdames Reed, l'ar- sons, H Gandy, A. Gandy, Norman, Youle, T Aelen,l!di Mason' and Miss M E Hintlley. A meeting was after- wards held, the Rev. E. r. Reed, past ir,presiding.There were also present—the Rev. H. Nicholls, Eccles ; Messrs. Thomas Gandy, Norman' W Atherton. U. Landon, .W Dickens, Banner, Fish, JAllen,Mason, Jones,and Brown. —The Rev. E. T. Reed expressed satisfaction at seeing so many present and extended a cordial welcume to all, as there were some people present they diet not always see. • Their g.ttiering at tee would have been muc e larger if . there had not been such an unusual amount of sickness amongst the congregation. During the seven years he had been pastor of tree Emmanuel Church he had never known so many cases of s'ckness amongst their people at once. They would sympathise with them. It would be invidious to mention names, but he regretted the ahsence of Mr.Lee,a charChwarden, who had a seeere illuess. It had ben his custom at their annual meetings to give a review of the year's work. When he was in Canada he became acquainted with a better plan. At the vestry meetings the secretary of .34.Cil depertment gave a report of the work. That added inte.reet in the proceedings of the vestry. He suggested that they should have a similar plan in Warrington, and the repents would be given at the next Easter vestry meet- ing. Personally ha would be relieved from hav- ing to do the work. The Church was. a social organization and it was only by co-operation that tee work could be effectively done. (Applaus?). —rho "erev. H. Nicholls said if there were any towns that ought to be Protestant they were the towns in Lan- cashire and Yotkshire. They would in time be able to teach the Church of England a severe lesson far turning them out. There were now more than 400 people con- nected with their prayer meetings which had b ten estab- lished up and down the country and they wanted to re- tain as wed as possible the good old Evaegelietic faith. There was never a greeter need iu England flr the Emmanuel Church than at the preqsat time. People , seemed to be becoming more solf-oentred ; yet the Chris- tian Church existed for the purpose of brining more together, in the 1070 of Jesus Christ. They mu,t not be selfish, as they could not escape their responsibilities. He hoped the churches in the north would come more closely together in the future. --The musical praeramrne was excellently submitted. The gramaphome solos were well received by the audience. Miss Taylor gave a Vanot.erte solo, and Mr W H Podmore was responsible !et' a couple of flute solos. The choir rendered some excellent glees and anthems, including " I will lift up mine eyes," " Hail, smiling morn," " Ash Grove," an I "Canadian Boat Song." Master Johnson very effectively sang" The Holy City." as did Mr. Gandy the song" God protect England." Mr Perkins' songs were also well rendered. Mrs. Pendle'3ury made an efficient accompanist. The usual votes of thanks concluded a pleasant evening. 11/..../....114./.•••••••MIN WARRINGTON-.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>. • 7,4 – s-,-77•7" • ;. . • – •</head>
        <p>. • 7,4 - s-,-77•7" • ;. . • - •</p>
        <p>COMING EVENTS.</p>
        <p>The Royal Hand-Bell Ringers and Concert Party are announced to appear at Parr Hall, on Friday afternoon and evening next. Their reputation is of the highest, as they have appeared before Royalty and by Imperial cammand\on seventeen occasions. Selections will be made from a varied and choice programme, and those who pay a visit may be assured of a treat, as the entertainment is a novel one to Warrington. We would remind our readers that the Warring- ton Musical Society's gubscription concert takes place on Wednesday. February Bth, at Parr Hall, and those who intend co be present had better book early. The plan of reserved seats is cpen at Mr R. ll. Pearson's, Bold-street. At Parr Hall on Tuesday evening a public demon- stration will be held to advocate the necessity of closing public houses on Sunday. Mr. E. J. Guest, J.P., will preside and the deputation will include Ves. Archdeacon Madden, Rev. Charles Garrett, Major Ballantine, and Mr. Ernest Ashton (District Secretary), Sslections of music will be given and we would urge all those in sympathy with the movement to put in an appearance. The Seurday evening concert at Parr Hall for this evening promises to be an attractive one. The appearance of Mr. E. Thoraley-Dodge will no doubt be welcomed as he is popular in Warrington. The other artistes are :—Professor Wyman, con- juror, shadowgraphist,and ventriloquist; Mr. Arthur Warnley, bass; Miss Nellie Leesan, soprano ' - Miss Emily Harries, juvenile soprano; Miss Kitty Harries juvenile contralto; and Mr. Alfred Ainsworth, ac- companist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>NiEETING- OF THE WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</head>
        <p>NiEETING- OF THE WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</p>
        <p>Toe second part of the winter programme was opened by a meeting in the Museum Lecture Room, at which a large number of members and friends assembled to hear a lantern lecture by Mr. L. Greening. M.R.1.A., on " The Life History of a Spider.' 1-Ti:.h an elaborate and beautiful series of slides, he traced the growth of a spider trom the fertilization of the ovum to the attainment of adult life. Mr. Greening was cordialiy thanked for his excellent lecture. ACADEMY STREET HALL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday and Monday Mr. J. B. Tetlow occupied the platform at the Ac Ldemy-street Hall, and good audiences assembled. On Sunday afternoon the address was upon the subject of "Aediumvlip," and in the evening upon " Influences," in the .course of the latter he pointed out how we both consciously and unconsciously influence and are influenced by others psychologically. On Monday evening Mr. Tetlow answered questions from the audience. One question was to why it was necessary to be a spirit- ualist, or at least attend a few meetings, before ex- periencing spiritual influences, and in reply the speaker pointed out the fallacy of the statement. Spiritualism was the outcome of spiritual manifes- tations and influences which had not been sought, but had compelled the attention of those upon whom they had been directed, After each address Mr. Tetlow gave a number of clairvoyant and psychic delineations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>MYSTERIOUS FARM FIRE NEAR WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MYSTERIOUS FARM FIRE NEAR WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Fire Brigade were summoned to Mr. John Wade'a,Aston Manor Farm, Aston Grange, near Warrington, shortly after eight o'clock on Monday evening, where a Dutch barn, containing five stacks of oats and two other stacks of oats near the barn were on fire. Under the direction of Superintendent Turner, a contingent of men, with the steam engine Major, in charge of Engine Rad- eliffa, proceeded to the farm. They found the stacks fully ignited. Superintendent Turner had a conversation with Air. Wad e,and the latter thought it advisable to let the fire burn itself out. The dam- age is estimated at £BOO. Mr. Wa4e stated that he believed the. fire was the work of an incendiary. NEW GOSPEL HALL,</p>
        <p>There was an annual meeting of Sunday school teachers and officials in connection with the above place, on Saturday last, the 21st inst., in the small schoolroom, where a substantial tea was provided. The existing officials were unanimously re-elected for another year, and additions were made to the teaching staff. After a number of speeches and encouraging remarks both from officials and teachers, the meeting was brought to a do se in the usual manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-27_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>APPOINTMENT OF PARISE{ CHURCH ORGANIST.—OWing to the appointment of Mr. R. B. Musgrove, A.R.C.0.,t0 the pest of organist at Christ Church, New Brighten, the p)sition at the Parish Caurch, Frodsham, has been given to Mr. Charles H. Hibbert, at present the organist of the Iron Church, Frodsham. CHINA TEA SERVICES, Dinner Services, Zoilet and Trinket Sets. Cheapest honse.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>-SUPPUSED-DIUBLE-SUICIDE AT LEVENSHULME.</p>
        <p>A tragic affair :WAS disco4ered yesterday- ~.; Levens- hulme. A clerk named William Percival and hii wife, living in Lime-street, were found dead. It is supposed they poisoned themselven, as a bottle containing poison was founi in Perciv4l's pocksr. The affair was at once reported to the police, and great sensation was createl in the district when news ot the tragic affair got abroad.</p>
        <p>THE PARR'S BANK ROBBERY. RA:TURNFD BY POST.</p>
        <p>At the annual m.eting of Parr's Bank Limited, ia London yesterday, Vie Chairman Mr. Cecil F. Parr said —I have a more welPome piece of intsiligencs to give you than even your 19 pr cent. dividend. AI intim% tion has just bpen olaced in my hands that £40,000 of the large n-itrs have come back to us by post (cheers). A Shareho dr : Have you that from a reliable source ?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I don't suppose we shculd have raad it out if wo had not:.</p>
        <p>Another Sharlholder : DOB 3 that ampunt include all the £l,OOO notes ? The Chairrnsn : YAg, nI sime othi,rs as well. A CLUE.—ONE OF TEIE STOLEN NOTES TRACED. One of the lower denomination notes of the £60,03a worth stolen frorn Messr9. Parr's Bank was found to have been pas;f dat Brixton, a suburb, where many bank clerk; and betting men live. The police arc ac- tively following up this clue.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CHRISTMA.S TREE AND ENTERTAINMENT.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Children's Christmas Tree Com- mittee was held at the School Attendance Office, Sankey-street, on Wednesday afternoon, to receive the report and balance sheet in connection with th* recent treat to poor children at the Parr Hall. Mrs. J. Lyon Whittle presided, in the absence of the Mayoress.—Mr. Henry Taylor read a letter of apology for non-attendaace from Mrs. James Fair- clougo.—Mr.Jenkins reported that the total amount received in subscriptions was £57 7.3 9d, but of that amount Li 23 61 was outstanding. He had no doubt that it would be paid shortly. The payments amounted to £39 12 6d, leaving a balance of £l7 153 2d in the bank. He explained that the substantial balance was not accounted for merely by subscriptions, but by the kind- nes3 of friends who abstained from charging for various things. If they had had to pay for every- thing, they would not have had a balance on the right side.—Mrs. Byrne proposed, and Mrs. Fergu- son seconded, that the accounts be passed.—This , was unanimously agreed to.—Mr. Whittle proposed that the best thanks of the committee be given to Sir Gilbert Greenalle Messrs. P. Walker and Son, Mr. Henry Thornton, Mr. H. Taylor, the staff of young men from the Y MC A, Inspector Greatrex (It S P C A), the officials at the Ragged &amp;loot, the School Attendance Officers, and others who gave their assistance in any way.—This was also unani- mously carried.—Mrs. Whittle stated that every. thing passed off satisfactorily.—M.r. Jenkins said he thought the entertainment tor the children an- other year might be extended over two nights, and it would then give every child a chance of seeing it. It would make little difference in the expendi- ture.—This was approved of by the committee.</p>
